pmc:AbandonmentAfterFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Praktiker, die das Instrument nach dem Versagen aufgaben"@de,
        "Two practitioners who gave up the instrument after it failed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. VIII, Of the Religion of the Laplanders, p. 33" ;
    skos:definition "Two cases are reported together. A man of a Lapp village whose reindeer were dying prayed to his idol, strewed fir boughs about it, offered the skins, horns and skulls of reindeer, and prostrated himself with his whole family beseeching it to testify by some sign that it was the true god; after a whole day of prayers and devotions, finding no sign given, he set fire to the wood he had brought and burned the idol down, and asked the neighbours who sought to kill him why they would not let the god avenge himself. And a man whose mother was grievously sick sought remedy from the drum; his mother died notwithstanding, whereupon he cut his drum in pieces, alleging that he saw no use of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Both cases have the shape a protocol has: a question, a stated period, an outcome that could have fallen either way, and an act that follows from the outcome. In the first the period is a whole day and the demanded sign is named in advance. Neither man explains the failure away, and that is the whole point of entering them: it is the counter-case to a transmission that meets every disappointment with a named excuse."@en,
        "The provenance is the reason no more is made of them. Both stand in the chapter on the reception of Christianity, both are credited to a named priest of the mission, and both end with the man becoming a Christian or being praised as one. A missionary reporting that heathens destroyed their own idols after they failed is reporting the thing he most wanted to find. The cases are entered as claims standing in the work, and what they are evidence of is a separate question from what they say."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie hoch die Schwelle der Vertiefung liegt"@de,
        "How high the threshold of absorption stands"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what is required for absorption. The discourses describe it as reached in the ordinary course of the training; the handbook sets a far higher bar, remarking that only one in a hundred or a thousand accomplishes it."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement bears directly on every prerequisite in this file that runs through the fourth absorption. If the handbook's threshold is the right one, the capacities it makes conditional on that absorption are conditional on something very few reach; if the discourses' is, they are not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:FourAbsorptions .

pmc:AbsorptionsAreGraded a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Vertiefung setzt die vorhergehende voraus"@de,
        "Each absorption presupposes the one before it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:note "The series is strictly graded in both canon and handbook, each stage defined by what it has let go of. No passage was found that forbids skipping in so many words, which is why this is recorded as an order and not as a necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:AcaraLadder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die aufsteigende Ordnung der ācāras"@de,
        "The ascending order of the ācāras"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kulārṇava Tantra, in the section on the superiority of the Kula-dharma, at the verses giving the order of superiority of the ācāras (text with English translation by Ram Kumar Rai, Varanasi 1999)" ;
    skos:definition "A graded order of ways of practice in which each is declared superior to the one before: the Veda above all others, the Vaiṣṇava above the Veda, the Śaiva above the Vaiṣṇava, the Dakṣiṇa above the Śaiva, the Vāma above the Dakṣiṇa, the Siddhānta above the Vāma, the Kaula above the Siddhānta, and nothing above the Kaula."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter and verse are not recoverable from the copy read, which is an OCR of a printed translation whose running structure the scan does not carry reliably. Work, section and edition are named instead, which meets the standard of findability; a later reader working from another edition should expect a different numbering. See prima-materia#45 and prima-materia#56."@en,
        "This is the passage Wilson translates in 1832 without naming a place in the work, and on which pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature rests. It is now located in an edition, so the order is a claim of the tantra and no longer only a claim of its reporter."@en,
        "Two things follow that the Western pair of names cannot carry. The order has seven steps and not two, so a scheme with two is not a simplification of it but a replacement. And within the order the left stands above the right, so the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses its valuation. Whoever explains the modern left-hand path out of this passage explains it out of a text that ranks the left higher than the right and both below two further steps."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:AddressWithoutSight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Haupt-Huaca wird angesprochen, ohne gesehen zu werden"@de,
        "The principal huaca is addressed without being seen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II and cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "Not all who address the huaca see it. Although everyone knows in which direction it stands, few see it, because they hang back and only the priest goes forward, speaks and offers. Places where a huaca is said to have rested, called zamana, and places from which it is invoked, called cayan, are reverenced too."@en ;
    skos:note "In one village a woman who kept the huaca told the people to cover their eyes when they adored it, because the divine essence cannot be seen with bodily eyes. That is the transmission stating a reason for the arrangement, and it is entered because a reason given from inside is worth more than the recorder's explanation, which is that the devil had put fear into them."@en,
        "The arrangement has a consequence the recorder reports with satisfaction and which is a finding whoever reports it: when the villagers were finally made to look at what they had feared, that was itself the strongest argument against it. A cult whose object is addressed without being seen is a cult that can be undone by showing the object."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:AffectCouplingProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Verschiebung eintritt, und ob die Behauptung etwas über sie sagt"@de,
        "Whether the shift occurs, and whether the claim says anything about it"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether a strong affect shifts attention, readiness and the threshold for risk, and of what remains of the claim once it does."@en ;
    skos:note "A convergence worth following separately. Several transmissions warn against exactly this coupling on the ground that it deceives the practitioner about his own position, and traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl already carries pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle in that shape. If the direction of the shift is unknowable in advance, the warning and the finding say something close to the same thing from opposite ends, and that is a candidate for a convergence node rather than a remark here."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried and not favourable to either side. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, while the association between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The opposition of directions survives; its symmetry does not."@en,
        "On the state. This protocol is the case that made pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate necessary, and it is deliberately not recorded as cases without deviation. There the claim says something and the cases bear it out; here they bear it out because the claim does not say enough to be borne out against anything. Filing it as an ordinary confirmation would have added a success to a record that had in fact found the claim empty."@en,
        "That the shift occurs is not in doubt and the claim is borne out in that narrow sense. Strong affect measurably moves risk judgement and the choices that follow from it, in induced and in naturally occurring form alike."@en,
        "The consequence for anyone acting on the claim, which is the reason to enter this node at all. Whoever couples an intended state to a strong affect in order to shift his own threshold does not know from the claim whether he has raised or lowered it. Confirmation at this level of generality is not usable guidance, and the node says so rather than recording a success."@en,
        "What the evidence takes away in the same motion. The direction of the shift is set by the particular affect and not by its strength. Fear and anger are both strong and both unpleasant, and they move the threshold for risk in opposite directions: the fearful judge risk pessimistically and choose cautiously, the angry judge it optimistically and choose boldly, with the angry closer to the cheerful than to the fearful. A claim that names only the strength of the affect therefore cannot say which way anything moves, and is compatible with every outcome."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Beyond valence: toward a model of emotion-specific influences on judgement and choice, Cognition and Emotion 14 (2000), no. 4, pp. 473-493",
        "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Fear, anger, and risk, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 81 (2001), no. 1, pp. 146-159, doi:10.1037/0022-3514.81.1.146" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off experimental work on specific emotions and their appraisal tendencies, and off a registered replication of it" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Strong affect, induced or occurring, leaves risk judgement and the choices following from it unmoved against a neutral condition."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A threshold for risk that has been raised, and one that has been lowered. The claim covers both alike, so acting on it cannot tell the practitioner which of the two he has brought about."@en .

pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor"@de,
        "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:Piti .

pmc:AngelOfLightDeception a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Täuschung, die als Licht erscheint"@de,
        "The deception that appears as light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 39" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that an experience of luminosity or consolation may be counterfeit, and that the counterfeit is recognised by its circumstances rather than by its brightness."@en ;
    skos:note "Diadochus distinguishes the true consolation, which comes with the body awake and in warm love, from the false, which comes in a light half-sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:ApanaForcedAgainstItsCourse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der abwärts gerichtete Wind wird mit Gewalt aufwärts getrieben"@de,
        "The downward wind driven upward by force"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.62 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The wind whose course is downward is said to be made to go upward by force; the manual states the natural course and the reversal in the same verse."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the direct reversal of any scheme that assigns downward carriage to an operation in the lower abdomen. The manual has the abdominal operation carry upward, and has the operation at the throat press the other wind down. Both assignments are the opposite of the one the modern draft makes. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ApatheiaPresupposesAscesis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Apatheia setzt asketische Arbeit voraus"@de,
        "Apatheia presupposes ascetic labour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Apatheia is called the flower of ascetic labour and ascetic labour is defined as the keeping of the commandments, so that where the labour is absent the freedom from the passions does not appear at all rather than appearing in a lesser degree." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Apatheia ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:AppearancesAreSelfArising a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erscheinungen sind Selbst-Erscheinung"@de,
        "The appearances are self-arising"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bar do thos grol (Karma Lingpa recension, 14th century; trans. Gyurme Dorje 2006), the intermediate state of reality" ;
    skos:definition "The teaching that the figures met in the intermediate state are appearances of the one who meets them and not independently existing beings, and that recognising them as such is what frees."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan rang snang, self-appearance. A dating warning belongs on this node: the work is traditionally ascribed to Padmasambhava in the eighth century but is a treasure text of the fourteenth, revealed by Karma Lingpa; the older ascription is not held in the scholarship. Note also the scope. The doctrine holds of all appearance in this tradition, waking included, and is therefore not a statement about the threshold state in particular."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:AraqielTeachesSignsOfTheEarth a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Araqiel offenbart die Zeichen der Erde"@de,
        "Araqiel discloses the signs of the earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheEarth ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Araqiel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AristeasJourney a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seelenreise, Aristeas zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The soul-journey ascribed to Aristeas"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a figure who is not available to report it, in a historian's account of what was told of him."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:ArmarosTeachesLoosingOfSpells a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Armaros offenbart das Lösen von Beschwörungen"@de,
        "Armaros discloses the loosing of spells"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:LoosingOfSpells ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Armaros ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AscentPresupposesTheRungs a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau der Schönheit selbst setzt den Aufstieg über die Stufen voraus"@de,
        "The vision of beauty itself presupposes the ascent by rungs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 211c" ;
    skos:note "The word rendered as rungs stands in the base text at 211c, which is why this counts as a stated ordering and not as a modern reading of a sequence of paragraphs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ascent is described as rightly conducted only when it proceeds as by rungs of a ladder, from one body to two, from bodies to pursuits, from pursuits to studies, and thence to beauty itself; the one who does not go this way does not arrive at the final vision." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BeautyItself ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:BeautyOfOneBody ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:AzazelTeachesAdornment a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Azazel offenbart Schmuck und Kosmetik"@de,
        "Azazel discloses adornment and cosmetics"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Azazel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AzazelTeachesMetalworking a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Azazel offenbart Metallverarbeitung"@de,
        "Azazel discloses metalworking"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Metalworking ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Azazel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BadMideCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der böse Midē, der die Vermögen zum Schaden wendet"@de,
        "The bad Midē who turns the powers to harm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168-169" ;
    skos:definition "The society names a figure of its own kind who employs the powers for evil purposes, who can assume the form of any animal and in that shape destroy the life of his victim. The warning is not against an outsider but against a member who has passed the same degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition that catalogues capacities and warns against their use is the shape this collection meets in Pātañjala Yoga as well, and the two warnings are not the same warning. There the capacities are called obstacles to the goal of the practice; here they are called dangerous in the hands of a man who wants harm. One is a caution about the practitioner's direction, the other about his intention."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MideDistantTouch,
        pmc:MideSoulSeizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:BaraqelTeachesAstrology a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel offenbart die Astrologie"@de,
        "Baraqel discloses astrology"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Astrology ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Baraqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BaraqelTeachesSignsOfLightning a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel offenbart die Zeichen der Blitze"@de,
        "Baraqel discloses the signs of the lightning flashes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with astrology."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfLightning ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Baraqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BengeAtFirstContact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Giftprobe beim ersten dauerhaften Kontakt"@de,
        "The poison ordeal at the first sustained contact"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries" ;
    skos:definition "The earliest European account, from travels of eighteen sixty-eight to eighteen seventy-one, already describes the whole apparatus: an oily preparation from a red wood, given to a hen, whose death or survival decides the question; the rubbing-board consulted before any undertaking of consequence; and the use of both to establish the guilt or innocence of the accused, with suspected witches tried by the same ordeal. It records a case in which a hostile chief called off an attack because his fowl had died."@en ;
    skos:note "The corroborative second test is not in this account. That is an argument about the account and not about the transmission: the writer was a botanist passing through with an ivory company, not an observer of consultations, and he reports the outcome of single fowls because that is what he saw. What the silence does establish is that the double test is not attested before the monograph in anything read here, and the question of when it can first be shown is left open as an issue."@en,
        "The passage is the reason the contact route on this tradition is not simply documented. The oracles stand in the record at the first sustained contact with the Islamic north and cannot have come in with it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:BengeCorroborationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rate, mit der der Bestätigungstest scheitert"@de,
        "The rate at which the corroborative test fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "A count of the corroborated consultations printed in the source: how often the second test fell opposite to the first, as the transmission's rule of validity requires, and how often it fell alike and voided the verdict."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the two seances printed, the first carries two corroborated pairs and both are recorded as contradictory: a question about taking on the taboos of vengeance against a question about the questioner's own survival under them, and a question about a man's safety in a named settlement against its explicit corroboration. The second seance carries one explicitly corroborative question, and it agreed; the recorder calls that seance a wholly successful consultation. Three pairs, two of them void by the transmission's own rule."@en,
        "The count is the recorder's material and the counting is this graph's. He states outright that the seances are given so that the reader may judge for himself the proportion of fowls that die and the order of deaths and survivals, so the arithmetic is invited by the source and is not imposed on it."@en,
        "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is from the transmission's own stated requirement rather than from a base rate of chance. A base rate is deliberately not entered: the poison is administered in a number of doses that varies with the size of the fowl and with the operator's reading of its earlier answers, so the probability that a given fowl dies is not fixed and no honest chance rate can be stated from what was read."@en,
        "Three pairs decide nothing about a rate and the number is entered so that a later reader can see how small it is. What it does establish is that the falsifying condition is not hypothetical: it occurred in two of the three cases this graph has actually read, and the source says elsewhere that the oracle frequently kills or spares both fowls."@en,
        "What the deviation is not. It is not evidence against the oracle in the transmission's own terms, because the transmission assigns every occurrence of the condition to one of eight causes that leave the instrument untouched. That is why the finding of this protocol is about the standing of the falsifying condition inside the transmission and not about the oracle's accuracy, which nothing read here measures."@en ;
    pm:caseCount 3 ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), the two seances printed at pp. 141-143" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "The Azande, by the corroborative second test their own rule prescribes; the two consultations counted here were recorded and printed at length by E. E. Evans-Pritchard, who was present at both" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The two tests of one consultation fall alike, both fowls dying or both surviving, so that by the transmission's own rule the verdict is contradictory and void."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch .

pmc:BengeFailureCauses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Ursachen, denen ein Versagen des Orakels zugeschrieben wird"@de,
        "The eight causes to which a failure of the oracle is attributed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 155 and pp. 155-157" ;
    skos:definition "Where the oracle contradicts itself the failure is attributed to one of eight causes: the wrong variety of the creeper having been gathered, the breach of a taboo, witchcraft, the anger of the owners of the forest in which the creeper grows, the age of the poison, the anger of the ghosts, sorcery, and use. Two varieties of the creeper are distinguished by name, and poison that kills without discrimination is set aside for some months to cool and is thrown away if it does not."@en ;
    skos:note "The list is the reason the falsifying condition on the neighbouring node does not function as one inside the transmission. Every occurrence of the condition is assigned to a cause that leaves the oracle itself untouched, and the recorder reports that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. That sentence is the recorder's summary of an attitude and not a quoted Zande statement, and it is entered as such."@en,
        "This is also the passage on which the whole later argument about whether Zande thought is closed against refutation rests. None of that argument was read at the wording in this run; it stands as an issue with the works named."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:BhastrikaPresupposesLevelNeckAndTrunk a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Blasebalgatem setzt einen gerade gehaltenen Hals und Rumpf voraus"@de,
        "The bellows breath presupposes neck and trunk held level"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.60 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The verse binds the lotus seat, a level neck and trunk, and a carefully closed mouth into the one instruction that opens the bellows breath. It is the only place in the breath chapter where the alignment of the spine is stated as a condition of a breath practice, and it is stated for one practice, not for the chapter. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:BhavaClassification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Dispositionen: paśu, vīra, divya"@de,
        "The three dispositions: paśu, vīra, divya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.50-57 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.19-20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "A division of practitioners into three kinds, the bound, the heroic and the divine, which governs the rites a person is competent for. The source describes the divine as all but a god, pure of heart, alike towards opposites and unattached, and the bound as one who must gather his own offerings and who may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the tradition's own answer to the question why one person takes one way and another another: not by choosing, but by what he is held competent for. The text presses the point polemically — it argues that if the bound disposition cannot exist in this age, the divine cannot either, and that only the heroic rites bear visible fruit. A record that reports the two paths as a choice between camps reports the later Western reading and not this one. See prima-materia#54."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:BirchBarkRecord a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Birkenrindenkarte"@de,
        "The birchbark chart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-165" ;
    skos:definition "The record the priests keep on birchbark, incised in fine lines, giving pictorially the ground plan of the degrees to which the owner is entitled."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things follow from this and both bear on how the rest of the file is to be read. The society keeps a written record by its own means, which is unusual among the transmissions this graph registers as lacking a corpus. And the capacities of the degrees are stated as readings of marks on such a chart, so what the report carries is an informant's exegesis of a pictograph and not a free verbal account; that is a different kind of witness, and arguably a firmer one, because the marks were there before the question was asked."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:Camac a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Camac, der angerufene Macher"@de,
        "Camac, the maker that is invoked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the invocation of the huaca; cap. VII, the pacarina of each lineage; cap. XIV, the third question of the visitor's interrogatory" ;
    skos:definition "The name under which the power is addressed at the moment of asking. When they invoke the huaca they call it Runapcamac, maker of men, and ask it for life, health and food, and they ask it nothing for any other life. Each lineage names its own pacarina, the place it says it came out of, and calls that Camac, maker; and each says it has its own maker, one a hill, one a spring."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is entered at the strength the three passages give it and no higher. All three are the same recorder, and all three show him using the word as evidence that the Indians give to a stone what is due to God alone. What can be taken from them is that camac was the word used in address and that it was understood as making rather than as owning or protecting. What cannot be taken from them is a doctrine of camac as a substance or a force, which is a reading of the Quechua of Huarochirí and stands on a document this run could not open."@en,
        "The third of the three places is a question of the interrogatory the visitor was to put in every village. That the word had to be asked for by name shows that it was the recorder's own diagnostic and not an incidental report, which strengthens the evidence that it was in use and weakens the evidence about what it meant."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wurf bringt das Vorauswissen hervor"@de,
        "The casting yields foreknowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:IfaCasting ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:IfaForeknowledge .

pmc:CatequillaPrediction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Huaca, die einen Tod voraussagte und dafür verbrannt wurde"@de,
        "The huaca that foretold a death and was burned for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, on the idol Catequilla" ;
    skos:definition "A famous idol of one province was consulted through its priests by an Inca passing with an army, about whether he would return victorious from a battle or die in it; the answer was that he would die, and so it fell out. From this the huaca came to have so great a name that people came from very remote provinces to consult it and to make offerings, and it grew rich and had a sumptuous temple. His son, passing later and seeing that greatness, and learning that it was the huaca which had announced his father's death, ordered the temple and all in it to be burned; the priests stole the idol out of the fire and carried it elsewhere and built it another temple."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated hit, with its consequence stated too: the reputation of the huaca is derived from a single prediction that came true, by the recorder and presumably by the tradition he heard it from. It is the only place in this file where a claim about accuracy is made about a particular occasion rather than about a method."@en,
        "Entered as a claim standing in the work and not as a report of an event. Its chain is long — an extirpator writing in sixteen twenty-one about what another visitor was told about a consultation before the conquest — and no part of it was checked against another witness in this run."@en,
        "The second half is the more useful. The correct answer cost the huaca its temple, because the person it was correct about was the father of the man who burned it. A transmission in which an accurate oracle can be destroyed for its accuracy is not one in which reputation tracks accuracy, and the story says both things at once."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CautionAgainstOpinionAboutHiddenThings a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Warnung davor, sich über die verborgenen Dinge eine Meinung zu bilden"@de,
        "The caution against forming opinions about hidden things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-20" ;
    skos:definition "The reported attitude that these are great and difficult questions best left alone, that men know so little beyond food and sleep and rest that it might easily seem presumptuous to form any opinion about hidden things, and that people who are content should not trouble themselves with thinking."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition warning against the very enquiry the rest of it makes possible. It is entered because a record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports what it holds, and because this caution is directed at the enquirer and not at the practitioner: it does not say the hidden things are unreal, it says that opinion about them is presumption."@en,
        "The passage also gives a second and worldlier ground for the same reticence, which the ethnographer reports of himself and not of the tradition: that his hosts feared their séances and their taboo observances would be laughed at. Both grounds are in the same pages, and separating them is the reader's work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:InuitHiddenThings ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheFireReaders a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Feuerleser galten am wenigsten, weil sie immer logen"@de,
        "The fire-readers were least esteemed because they always lied"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc" ;
    skos:definition "The report adds, of this class and of no other in its list of specialists, that these were the least esteemed, because they always lied."@en ;
    skos:note "It bears directly on the claim, open elsewhere in this project, that the old traditions test origin and fruits and never a rate of hits. Here a transmission is reported as having formed a standing comparative judgement about a whole class of practitioners on the ground that they were always wrong, which is an aggregation over cases however loosely it was arrived at. Whether the judgement was reached by counting or was itself a piece of received esteem is not decidable from the one sentence, and no examination node is written on it."@en,
        "The most valuable sentence found in this run. It is a ranking of divinatory techniques by reliability, made inside the transmission, with the reason given as repeated failure rather than as impurity, wrong descent or broken taboo. Every other failure in this file is attributed to something about the person; this one is attributed to the method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:FireReading ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheRubbingBoard a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Reibbrett lässt sich vom Betreiber führen"@de,
        "The rubbing-board can be worked by the operator"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 173 and p. 153" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission holds the rubbing-board inferior to the other oracles because the man who works it can cheat with it, and only he may work it. The charge is raised in practice when the oracle has spoken against the questioner or when he dislikes the operator. Of the poison oracle, by contrast, the source records that a manipulation is never even suggested."@en ;
    skos:note "The warning names the failure mode of an oracle whose outcome one party controls, and names it from inside the transmission rather than from the recorder's side. It is the counterpart of the passage on the second test that is not made."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheSightItself a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen kommt ungerufen und lässt sich nicht ablegen"@de,
        "The sight comes unbidden and cannot be laid down"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:definition "Those who have reached the third degree are said to see distant things even against their will. A first-person report is given: a man who surrendered his drum to the recorder on complaint confessed with tears that though he parted with it and made no other, he would have the same visions as before; he instanced the recorder himself, giving a true and particular account of what had happened to him on his journey to Lapland; and he complained that he did not know how to make use of his eyes, since things altogether distant were presented to them."@en ;
    skos:note "The same passage carries a claim of a verified hit — the man is said to have told the recorder correctly what had happened on his own journey — and the recorder reports it without endorsing it. It is a claim standing in the work and no more; nothing about the account can be checked, and the man is not named."@en,
        "The warning is not against misuse and not against the anger of a spirit. It is that the capacity, once complete, is not under the will and cannot be given up with the instrument. That is a caution of a kind this graph holds very few of, and it is the transmission warning against the thing it exists to produce."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DistantSight ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:ChinToChestInTheChiefSeats a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das an die Brust gedrückte Kinn in den beiden bevorzugten Sitzhaltungen"@de,
        "The chin pressed to the chest in the two seats the manual prefers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.46 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.48 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "In the two seats the manual singles out as chief, the chin is set firmly against the chest and the gaze is fixed between the brows or at the tip of the nose."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the counterweight to any claim that the manual requires an erect spine throughout. The seats it recommends most warmly close the neck rather than extend it, and the same posture chapter has the tongue set against the roots of the upper teeth while the wind is drawn upward. Uprightness of the trunk is attested; uprightness carried through the neck and head is contradicted in the same work. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ColdAsSourceProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Kälte Quelle oder Widerstand ist"@de,
        "Whether cold is a source or a resistance"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether exposure to cold supplies the practitioner with something, in the physical sense the word energy carries, or draws on him."@en ;
    skos:note "Read as a report of experience, the claim describes something that occurs. Deliberate cold exposure raises circulating catecholamines sharply and is followed by a state the practitioner calls charged. The experience is real and the description of it is inverted: what is felt as taking in is a release of the body's own reserves. That is a finding about the wording and not a dismissal of the practice."@en,
        "The claim has two readings and they come out in opposite directions, so the protocol keeps them apart as prima-materia#341 requires. Read physically, it is turned around: cold exposure raises energy expenditure rather than supplying it. The heat produced in the brown adipose tissue that the cold recruits comes from oxidising the body's own substrate, and the deposit is depleted by the work, not filled by it. Cold costs; it does not deliver."@en,
        "The source transmission stands on the other side, and the issue that opened this said so before any evidence was consulted. The tibetan inner-heat literature sets cold as the resistance against which the heat is raised, never as what the heat is raised from. Where the draft and its transmission disagree, the measurements agree with the transmission. That is the reverse of the expected result and is the reason this node is worth having."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "Energy expenditure and substrate use at thermoneutrality in the same participants."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "A. M. Cypess and others, Identification and importance of brown adipose tissue in adult humans, New England Journal of Medicine 360 (2009), pp. 1509-1517, doi:10.1056/NEJMoa0808718",
        "M. Kox and others, Voluntary activation of the sympathetic nervous system and attenuation of the innate immune response in humans, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111 (2014), no. 20, pp. 7379-7384, doi:10.1073/pnas.1322174111" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the thermogenesis literature; the second reading off the intervention study cited below" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners exposed to cold are found to expend less energy, or to end the exposure with greater substrate reserves, than matched participants at thermoneutrality."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold .

pmc:CompletionPresupposesGeneration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vollendungsstufe setzt die Erzeugungsstufe voraus"@de,
        "The completion stage presupposes the generation stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The order is set by the arrangement of the treatise, which takes the generation-stage meditations before the completion-stage yogas. That the six yogas belong to the completion stage places tummo after the generation stage as well."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:CompletionStage ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GenerationStage ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:ConcealmentFromTheRecorder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Praxis wird dem Fragenden verborgen"@de,
        "The practice is concealed from the questioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos I and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The recorder states that the people of this land, like those of Peru, hide the matter with the greatest diligence; that they deny it stubbornly even when accused and convicted, until they die impenitent; and that neither the fear of God nor an oath nor anything else avails to make them confess."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison with Peru is the recorder's and is not evidence of any connection between the two. It is entered because it shows he was generalising across two colonial situations, which is one of the ways a report acquires content its informants never supplied."@en,
        "This is the most valuable sentence in what was read, and it cuts two ways. It records a gate: the transmission was held closed against an outsider who had the power to punish, which is a fact about the tradition. And it records the limit of the whole manual as a witness, in the words of the man who wrote it: what he has is what could not be hidden from him. Every other node in this file is to be read against it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus"@de,
        "Concentration presupposes virtue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Sila ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ConfessionYieldsAverting a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beichte wendet den Zorn ab"@de,
        "The confession turns aside the anger"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas .

pmc:ConsciousnessCalibrationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob sich eine Bewusstseinsstufe am Muskeltest ablesen lässt"@de,
        "Whether a level of consciousness can be read off a muscle test"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that the position of a mental or emotional state on a numbered scale can be established by testing the strength of a subject's muscle, as the scale's author reports having done over some quarter of a million times."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reason for caution about the direction of the finding. The source itself names conditions under which its procedure is said to fail: synthetic clothing on the subject, metal jewellery, the voice of the tester. A procedure whose stated interferences are all channels through which an expectation could travel is one whose null result would be expected on ordinary grounds, and whose positive result would need to exclude them. That observation belongs in the protocol and not in a verdict."@en,
        "The counter-search is owed and has not been carried out. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists, some of it from within the field, and a rejection that has read only one side is by SPEC §13 a one-sided dispute and not a refutation. Until it is read, the state of this protocol stays where it is."@en,
        "What the available evidence covers. Three controlled studies bear on the muscle test as a diagnostic instrument, all with blinding, and none of them supports it. A test-retest study on wasp-venom allergy with four examiners, seven patients and twenty bottles each reports an overall kappa of 0.03 with sensitivity around forty per cent and specificity around sixty; a dental double-blind study on materials testing reports the test unreliable; a double-blind randomised study on therapy localisation reports the same. The first states plainly that as a diagnostic tool the method is no more useful than guessing."@en,
        "What the available evidence does not cover, and this is the point. Every one of those studies asks whether the muscle test can identify a substance or a bodily site. None asks whether it can place a mental state on a numbered scale. The evidence therefore strikes the presupposition, that the test yields answers better than chance, and not the claim itself. No blinded replication of the calibrations is known, and the claim is unexamined in the strict sense even though its foundation is not."@en,
        "Why this claim and not another. Almost every claim in this graph is about an inner state for which no procedure exists, and pm:noProcedureDevised is accordingly the commonest state in the holding. This one names its own procedure, the procedure requires no instrument, and blinding it is cheap. Among the claims the graph carries it is the most nearly testable, and that is the reason it was opened."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H.-J. Staehle, M. J. Koch and T. Pioch, Double-blind Study on Materials Testing with Applied Kinesiology, Journal of Dental Research 84 (2005), no. 11, doi:10.1177/154405910508401119",
        "R. Lüdtke, B. Kunz, N. Seeber and J. Ring, Test-retest-reliability and validity of the Kinesiology muscle test, Complementary Therapies in Medicine 9 (2001), pp. 141-145",
        "S. A. Schwartz and others, A Double-Blind, Randomized Study to Assess the Validity of Applied Kinesiology as a Diagnostic Tool and as a Nonlocal Proximity Effect, Explore 10 (2014), no. 2" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The procedure is the one the source prescribes; the falsifying condition is the ordinary requirement of inter-rater and test-retest agreement, and nobody has yet applied it to the calibrations themselves" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Blinded testers, given the same states without knowing which, do not agree on their placement above chance; or the same tester, blinded to his earlier results, does not reproduce his own placements."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale .

pmc:ConsultationPresupposesQualifyingWood a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung setzt sonnenläufiges Holz voraus"@de,
        "Consulting the drum presupposes wood that turns with the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drum must be of such wood, and does not say what follows from using a drum that is not. The strength is the strength the text gives, not the strength the requirement sounds like."@en,
        "This is the condition the graph nearly lost. It was read at the wording in the same run as the rest of this file but reached only a local working copy, not the branch, and was recovered only because an uncommitted file was noticed. Recorded as a process finding in prima-materia#351."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RunicDrumConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:ConsultationPresupposesTaboos a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung setzt die Tabus voraus"@de,
        "The consultation presupposes the taboos"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 132" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the source does not merely prescribe the abstinence but says what its want does to the instrument. An unclean man may still address the oracle from a distance, which is a graded rather than an absolute exclusion and is recorded here rather than smoothed away."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Contact of an unclean person with the oracle is certain to destroy its potency, and even the close proximity of an unclean person may have that result."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeTaboos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ContestOfTwoNoaidi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wettstreit zweier Praktiker"@de,
        "The contest of two practitioners"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 477" ;
    skos:definition "When two noaidi wished to know which of them excelled the other in the art, they painted on a drum two reindeer striking each other with their horns, and he whose reindeer had the victory was the better."@en ;
    skos:note "A procedure with a stated criterion, applied by practitioners to practitioners, with an outcome that could fall either way and that neither controls. Nothing else in this file, and very little in this graph, has that shape."@en,
        "What the source does not say is how the victory of a painted reindeer is determined — whether by the ring, by the movement of the drum, or otherwise. Without that the procedure cannot be reconstructed, only recorded, and no protocol node is written on it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DescentPresupposesDepth a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg setzt die Tiefe voraus, die den Atem sammelt"@de,
        "The descent presupposes the depth that gathers the breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at stated necessity because a consequence is quotable, which is unusual: most sequences in this collection state an order and leave the consequence unsaid. The strength rests on the closing line, not on the chain of conditionals, which on its own would carry only a prescribed order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The inscription closes by setting the two outcomes against each other: to go with the sequence is to live, to go against it is to die. The consequence is stated for the order as a whole, and every stage is joined to the one before it by the same conditional form." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:XingqiDescent ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:XingqiDepth ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus"@de,
        "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Cetopariyanana,
        pmc:Dibbacakkhu,
        pmc:Dibbasota,
        pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase"@de,
        "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:definition "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."@en ;
    skos:note "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it."@en,
        "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana,
        pmc:XingqiDescent ;
    pm:independenceGround "One side carries the claim and the other has been withdrawn from it. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era, earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China, and for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions; it cannot be declared contact-free, and it was listed here as an independent attestation until this ground was required, which is what the requirement is for. Neither witness is an ethnographic gathering, so no recorder's feedback is at issue." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Daoist .

pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Zurschaustellung der Kräfte verweigert"@de,
        "Display of powers refused"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 11 (Kevaṭṭa Sutta)",
        "Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8" ;
    skos:definition "The discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, and holds only instruction to be reliable; the display of power for the sake of persuasion is refused as something to be ashamed of."@en ;
    skos:note "The Vinaya rule against displaying superhuman states to laypeople stands alongside it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Cetopariyanana,
        pmc:Dibbacakkhu,
        pmc:Dibbasota,
        pmc:Manomayakaya ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unterschiedenheit als Streben des Geschöpfs gegen die Auflösung"@de,
        "Distinctiveness as the striving of the creature against dissolution"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1916" ;
    dcterms:source "C. G. Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos, Sermo I (English translation of the 1916 private printing, Gnostic Society Library electronic edition)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a creature which ceases to distinguish falls into the undifferentiated fullness, ceases to be a creature and is given over to dissolution in nothingness, so that its natural striving runs towards distinctiveness against a primeval and perilous sameness; and that the striving is nevertheless not after difference but after the creature's own being."@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the telic axis as pm:individuatingTelos. The placement is a reading and rests on the two refusals the passage makes in the same breath: dissolution is called the death of the creature, and the striving is said not to be after difference. Stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted."@en,
        "Recorded because it is the only position located so far that names both ends of the axis as a loss and then states a third movement. Dissolution into the fullness is called the death of the creature outright, which rules out the unitive end; and the striving is expressly not after difference as it is thought, which rules out the separative one. What remains is a striving after one's own being, and the source gives it the name principium individuationis."@en,
        "The attribution is why this is an origination and not a witness of gnostic transmission. The author printed the text under the name of a second-century teacher of Alexandria who did not write it, and it continues to be read and cited as gnostic teaching. pm:circulatesAs records that, and the node makes no judgement about the author's own reading of the ancient material."@en,
        "The god named above the god of the pairs of opposites belongs to the same argument and not to a separate doctrine: the qualities of the fullness are given as pairs, and what is set above them unites the full and the empty. A reading that takes this for a third party in a pantheon takes the figure for the argument."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A gnostic text of the second century, printed under the name of Basilides of Alexandria and read as ancient teaching rather than as a work written in 1916." ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. G. Jung" ;
    pm:telicOrientation pm:individuatingTelos .

pmc:DivineDreamIsNotOrdinarySleep a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der göttliche Traum ist nicht der gewöhnliche Schlaf"@de,
        "The divine dream is not ordinary sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that the mass of dreams occurring in full sleep carries no divination, and that only the state at the edge of sleep is treated as a sign of divine presence."@en ;
    skos:note "Load-bearing against any claim that sleep as such is an access. The source that comes closest in the whole collection to naming the threshold state is also the one that refuses the wider claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:DiviningPresupposesPupilage a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Befragen setzt die Lehrzeit voraus"@de,
        "Divining presupposes the pupilage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage rather than of the tradition. The source sets the pupilage, its minimum length and the division of its course, and says nothing about what befalls a man who divines without it. A stated consequence would raise this to a necessity; none stands in what was read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:IfaCasting ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BabalawoPupilage ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:DogonSiriusExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Steht die Kenntnis des Begleiters in der Überlieferung?"@de,
        "Is the knowledge of the companion found in the transmission?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol addressing the claim that Dogon transmission carries knowledge of an invisible companion of Sirius. It asks whether the star names and the doctrine can be found among Dogon outside the circle of the original informants, and whether any account of them leads anywhere but back to the recording work."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-testimony has not been read at the wording in this run. A reply appeared in the same journal in the following volume, and a further exchange in the two years after; the deviation recorded here therefore rests on one side of a controversy that has two. See prima-materia#252. Under SPEC §13 this state is a one-sided dispute waiting for its second side, not a refutation, and the state below is set on the cases and not on the conclusion."@en,
        "The one trace that remains is itself the finding. Informants in that village say that in former times people knew of a star appearing in the year of the ceremony, and further enquiry leads back to the recorder. What looks like independent survival is feedback from the published account into the field it describes, and any later confirmation must be tested against that path before it counts."@en,
        "The re-study reports the ethnographic facts as straightforward. Dogon know Sirius as a star and call it the hunter's star; knowledge of the stars matters neither in daily life nor in ritual, the sun and the phases of the moon being what reckoning runs on. No Dogon outside the circle of the original informants had heard the star names the account turns on, and no one, not even inside that circle, had heard or understood that Sirius was double. The scheduling of the ceremony is done in several ways in the village the account draws on, none of them astronomical."@en,
        "The re-study's explanation of how the account arose is not adopted here as a finding, only recorded: that an informant spoke of stars of different generations, meaning two adjacent visible stars held as father and son to Sirius as grandfather, and that the recorder, who had read astronomy in Paris, made a double and triple system of it. That is a reconstruction of an intention and cannot be examined; the absence of the doctrine in the field can."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "Walter E. A. van Beek, Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule, Current Anthropology 32/2 (1991), pp. 149-150 with its footnote 10, and pp. 156-157" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Walter E. A. van Beek, field re-study among the Dogon of Sanga and Yugo Doguru, reported 1991" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A report of the star names or of the doctrine of an invisible companion from Dogon speakers outside the original circle of informants, where the chain of the report can be shown not to run back through the published account; or a fixation of the doctrine in any text or record datable before nineteen thirty-one. Either would return the claim to contested and undo the deviation recorded here." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion .

pmc:DoingPresupposesBeing a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Es wird nichts getan, solange niemand da ist, der tut"@de,
        "Nothing is done until there is someone to do it"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the first talks on the human machine and in the chapter on the absence of unity" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a person is a machine governed by outward influences, that everything happens and nobody does anything, and that in order to do it is first necessary to be; together with the account of a person as lacking unity and consisting of a multiplicity of I's rather than one."@en ;
    skos:note "It bears directly on the disagreement recorded in convergences/left-hand-path-divergence.ttl. Both sides there dispute what becomes of the self at the end; this source holds that at the beginning there is no single self to be kept or surrendered, and that one has to be made first. That is not a third answer to their question but a claim that the question has a precondition neither states."@en,
        "The interest for this graph is the order of precedence. The claim is not that people do the wrong thing but that the doer is not yet assembled, which puts every question of method downstream of a question about who is applying it. A path chosen by a multiplicity is chosen by whichever part happens to be present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A finding of an older school, transmitted rather than argued." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:DreamDivinationPresupposesPurityOfTheVehicle a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumdivination setzt die Reinheit des Fahrzeugs voraus"@de,
        "Dream divination presupposes purity of the vehicle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:note "The condition is on the state of the seer, not on a technique of sleeping. Recorded as order rather than necessity because the treatise makes the clarity of the dream depend on the vehicle without stating at that place what befalls one who proceeds with an unpurified one. Chapter divisions vary between editions; the work and its subject are given so that the passage can be found in whichever edition is at hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:DreamDivination ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:PneumaticVehicle ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:DreamYogaPresupposesClearLight a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga setzt das Halten des klaren Lichts voraus"@de,
        "Dream yoga presupposes holding the clear light of sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The treatise takes the recognition of the clear light before the training in the dream. It does not state at that place what befalls one who begins elsewhere, which is why the strength is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:DreamYoga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ClearLightOfSleep ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:DrumMustBeInherited a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Trommel muss ererbt sein"@de,
        "The drum must be inherited"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 474" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drums were not thought sufficiently fit for their uses unless got by inheritance, and does not say what follows from using one that was not. The rule sits oddly beside the same recorder's report of a practitioner explaining how his own drum was made, and the oddity is left standing rather than resolved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RunicDrumConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:SunCourseRule ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DurationTracksDistance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dauer des Liegens folgt der Entfernung und endet bei einem Tag"@de,
        "The time he lies still follows the distance, and stops at a day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57" ;
    skos:definition "How long he lies is stated to depend on how near or far the place is of which he makes enquiry — an hour or two for a near one — and to have an upper bound: the time never exceeds twenty-four hours, let the place be at never so great a distance."@en ;
    skos:note "A quantity that varies with a measurable quantity, and a ceiling that does not. Both halves are unusual: the first makes the claim comparable across cases, and the second makes it refutable by a single case that runs longer. Nothing else in this file states a number."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:Dvadasanta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ende der Zwölf"@de,
        "The end of the twelve"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 50 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 51 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "A limit named as the end of twelve, at which the mind or the risen power comes to rest. The verses give the number and leave the unit unstated."@en ;
    skos:note "The count itself fluctuates. What is verse 50 in the numbering used here appears as 51 in other editions, and the following verse shifts with it. A reader working from a different edition should expect the two verses cited here to sit one number further on."@en,
        "Two things this node deliberately does not say. It does not fix the unit: the base text says end-of-twelve, and the reading that makes this twelve finger-breadths from the tip of the nose belongs to the commentarial layer, not to the verse. And it does not fix the place: the exegesis of Lakṣmaṇjoo counts several such limits in the body, at the crown, the brows, the throat, the base of the heart and the navel, and the verse that follows says outright that it may be reached in any manner and in any place. Nothing here yields a fixed radius. See prima-materia#38."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:EighthHogonTeaching a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der achte Häuptling lehrte die Menschen die Merkmale des Sterns"@de,
        "The eighth chief taught men the characteristics of the star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-284" ;
    skos:definition "The account that the eighth supreme chief, having discovered the star, escaped the sacrifice his seven predecessors had undergone by feigning death, returned claiming to have been inside the star and to know its secrets, and then taught men its characteristics and reordered the reckoning of time."@en ;
    skos:note "An ascription inside a myth of origin, and the article gives it as such: it is offered expressly as an indication of the changes and combinations of reckoning that appear in the course of what the recorders put in quotation marks as the history of the Blacks."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics ;
    pm:ascribedTo "the eighth supreme chief, in the Dogon account of the origin of the sixty-year reckoning" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:EjiOgbeFirstOfTheSigns a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eji Ogbe als erstes der Zeichen"@de,
        "Eji Ogbe as the first of the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number" ;
    skos:definition "The sign held to be the principal one, called the leader, the commander and the king. Its appearance on the divining bowl is taken to mean that the message delivered is of very great weight."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated order of rank among the signs, and therefore a place where a later reader can check whether a modern list has kept the tradition's own order or imposed another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:ExcessiveAsceticismCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Unterscheidung gegen das Übermaß"@de,
        "Discretion against excess"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Cassian, Collationes II (De discretione)" ;
    skos:definition "The teaching that discretion is the mother of the virtues, directed against the practitioner who is undone not by laxity but by an excess undertaken in good faith."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:ExteriorisationCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entsprechung der Exteriorisation"@de,
        "The exteriorisation correspondence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that a body or seat of awareness distinct from the physical one is claimed in the Pāli handbooks, in the Yogasūtra, and in Greek reports of men whose souls left them."@en ;
    skos:note "The Indian pair share a sphere and a technical vocabulary and are not independent of each other. The Greek reports are of a different kind entirely: they are second-hand ascriptions to figures long dead, made by historians rather than by practitioners, and a route of contact with the Indian material has been proposed but not shown."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:Manomayakaya,
        pmc:ParakayaPravesa,
        pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:independenceGround "The Greek witness is fixed in the fifth century before the common era, before any documented route by which Indian technique could have reached the Greek world; a Scythian route has been proposed in the scholarship and not shown. The ground carries the independence and not the strength: the Greek report is a third-party ascription to figures long dead, made by a historian and not by a practitioner, which is a weakness of the testimony and not of its separateness from the Indian material." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:GraecoRoman ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Theravāda and Pātañjala Yoga share the Indian sphere and a common technical vocabulary. A Scythian or shamanic route to the Greek reports has been proposed in the scholarship but is not documented." .

pmc:EzeqeelTeachesKnowledgeOfClouds a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Ezeqeel offenbart die Wolkenkunde"@de,
        "Ezeqeel discloses the knowledge of the clouds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:KnowledgeOfClouds ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Ezeqeel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:FabricationRatherThanMisreport a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lüge ist eine erfundene Befragung, nie eine falsch berichtete"@de,
        "The lie is a fabricated consultation, never a misreported one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 37 and p. 154" ;
    skos:definition "The source states that no Zande would report the declaration of an oracle otherwise than it was given, and that a man who wishes to lie fabricates a declaration without consulting the oracle at all. Against the fabrication the transmission sets three things: the elder who makes an official test takes two or three witnesses with him, the counterparty consults his own oracles to check the first man's, and a man who believes he was not fairly tested may appeal to the king."@en ;
    skos:note "The cross-check by the counterparty is the part worth noticing. It is a control against the questioner rather than against the instrument, and it is the only one in this file that does not depend on the instrument being honest."@en,
        "The sanction is stated and is not merely moral: the king sends for the elder, tells him he is a cheat and a liar, and bars him from ever again conducting official consultations."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:FastKeptWellIsAlsoChecked a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Fasten gehalten wurde, wird nachträglich geprüft"@de,
        "Whether the fast was kept is checked afterwards"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "When the feast is over, guinea pigs are sacrificed in some places and it is seen by which part the blood runs in the entrails whether they have fasted well and kept all the ceremonies of the feast. The last act is named, and the name is given in the source."@en ;
    skos:note "A second procedure of the same shape as the confession test and applied to a different object: not to a statement but to a period of conduct. Together with the confession test it makes the checking of the participant, rather than of the answer, a recurring feature of this transmission and not a single case."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:FatherPerceivesHiddenThings a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Dem Vater der Erzählerin wird zugeschrieben, die verborgenen Dinge verstanden zu haben"@de,
        "The narrator's father is said to have understood hidden things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 23-24" ;
    skos:definition "The narrator relates that her father, called a great shaman, understood hidden things and perceived that the children she wanted to run out and play with were the souls of her dead brothers and sisters, whereupon his helping spirits set a barrier between her and them."@en ;
    skos:note "The case is worth the node because of its shape rather than its content. The perception is not reported as a vision or a journey but as a correction of an ordinary judgement: the child saw children, the shaman saw who they were. A claim in that form is answerable in a way that a claim about an inner state is not."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:InuitHiddenThings ;
    pm:ascribedTo "The narrator Takornâq's father, named in the narrative as a great shaman" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:FiftyAgainstSixty a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Fünfzig gegen sechzig: Bahnperiode und Zeremonienabstand"@de,
        "Fifty years against sixty: the period of the orbit and the interval of the ceremony"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 282 with its first footnote",
        "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 283 with its fifth footnote" ;
    skos:definition "The account gives the period of the seed-star's orbit as some fifty years and the interval between two Sigui as sixty, and states in a footnote that there is here a contradiction which has not yet been resolved. It then offers, as a question and not as a finding, that the last ceremony may have been brought forward and that the profane may be kept in the belief that the official period is sixty years."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute although both sides stand in the same work, because the work states the disagreement itself rather than smoothing it. The dispute is decisive for everything the outside reception built on the passage: fifty years is the figure that matches the companion of Sirius, sixty is the figure the ceremony actually runs on, and only the second is attested by anything a later worker could count."@en,
        "The same footnote that reports the fifty-year period cites a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for the closeness of that period to the companion of Sirius. Whether the fifty came from the informants or from the reading is the question this graph cannot decide from the article alone; see prima-materia#250."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:PoToloOrbit .

pmc:FinerBodiesNotInnate a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die feineren Körper sind nicht angeboren, sondern gezüchtet"@de,
        "The finer bodies are not innate but cultivated"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the chapter on the four bodies of man" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a person is not born with the bodies beyond the physical, that these can only be cultivated artificially and only where favourable conditions, inner and outer, are present, and that immortality therefore is not a property one is born with but something acquired."@en ;
    skos:note "It also fixes what the paths in pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal are for. If the goal were innate, a way would be a matter of recognising it; here it is a matter of building something, which is why the classification falls entirely on how much of a person is worked on. See prima-materia#65."@en,
        "The claim is stated against the traditions it borrows its terms from: most teachings that repeat the divisions are said to have forgotten or omitted precisely this feature. That makes it a correction offered to a corpus rather than a report of it, and it is the load-bearing move of the whole system — everything about ways, effort and time follows from a possession that must be made and can fail to be made."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The most important feature of an ancient teaching, lost by the systems that preserved its divisions, rather than a claim first published in this form in 1949." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:FivefoldPrimaryRasaRanking a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf primären Hingabeverhältnisse, nach Rang"@de,
        "The five primary devotional relations, ranked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 2.5.115 (trans. Haberman 2003)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the five primary forms of devotional love stand in a single ascending order: peacefulness, respectful service, companionship, parental affection, amorous love, with the first counted lowest and the last highest."@en ;
    skos:note "The ranking is asserted in the verse itself and not by a commentator. The operative words are yathā-pūrvam anuttamāḥ, rendered in the bilingual edition of two thousand and three as a hierarchical manner in which the first is considered the lowest, and in the independent translation published by a different lineage as an order of excellence running from first to last. Two translations from different hands and different schools agree on the direction, which is worth recording because the compound admits a second parsing that would reverse it."@en,
        "What the verse does not do is give the criterion. It states the order and moves on. Any statement of the form that the ranking follows increasing intensity, density or nearness of affection is drawn from elsewhere in the corpus and must be cited from there, not from this verse. The nearest thing the text supplies is the graded series inside a single relation, held separately below."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava .

pmc:ForcedBreathCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Ein erzwungener statt allmählich gezähmter Atem tötet den Übenden"@de,
        "Breath forced rather than tamed by degrees kills the practitioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.15 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.16-17 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A warning that the breath is to be mastered by degrees as a lion, an elephant or a tiger is mastered, and that it destroys the one who proceeds otherwise; performed wrongly, the practice is said to generate the very ailments it removes when performed rightly."@en ;
    skos:note "The named consequences are hiccough, laboured breathing, cough, and pain in head, ears and eyes. This is the strongest thing the manual says about the manner of breathing, and it concerns pace and force, not the opening the breath passes through. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Vierteilung unter vier Namensreihen"@de,
        "One fourfold division under four sets of names"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), at the table of the four bodies and its four terminologies" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that four terminologies name the same four bodies: carnal, natural, spiritual and divine; carriage, horse, driver and master, glossed as body, feelings and desires, mind, and the I with consciousness and will; physical, astral, mental and causal; with the assertion that comparisons of this kind are found in most systems that grant a person more than a physical body."@en ;
    skos:note "Held here because of the shape of the claim, not its content. An identity is asserted between a Pauline triad extended to four, an unnamed eastern set of images, and a nineteenth-century theosophical scheme. No route of contact is given for any of the three, and none is asserted by this node. The correspondence is recorded as claimed and not as established: this is exactly the case pm:Converging exists to keep separate from, and it does not meet that bar, because a shared table of names is not a shared claim."@en,
        "The theosophical column is the one that dates the table. Physical, astral, mental and causal in that sequence is a set fixed in the late nineteenth century; a table containing it cannot be older than that, whatever the antiquity of the images beside it. Anyone reading the four columns as four independent witnesses to one ancient doctrine has taken a nineteenth-century arrangement for a convergence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A correspondence found in most teachings, presented as recovered rather than as drawn up." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Wege zu einem Ziel, unterschieden danach, wie viel am Menschen sie bearbeiten"@de,
        "Four ways to one goal, distinguished by how much of a person they work on"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the chapter on the ways to immortality and the fourth way" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that all known ways to immortality fall into three — that of the fakir working on the body, that of the monk on the emotions, that of the yogi on the mind — each long, hard and one-sided; and that a fourth works on all three at once, requires no withdrawal from life, and reaches the same goal sooner for that reason."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded chiefly as a negative finding. The fourth way is regularly listed among the third options beside a right-hand and a left-hand path, and the source will not carry that. All four ways are said to lead in one direction, and the direction is named: immortality. What separates them is how much of a person is worked on and whether life must be left. That is an axis of completeness, not of the direction of the will, and it is compatible with either end of the other one. See prima-materia#65."@en,
        "The case is more useful than a mere correction, because it is a whole system in which the goal is held fixed and the entire classification falls on the means. Whoever doubts that method and goal can be varied independently has here a taxonomy that varies only one of them. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67."@en,
        "The goal as named — an immortality that the person acquires and is not born with — sits on the separative side of the telic axis rather than the unitive one. That placement is not asserted on this node: it would need the passages on the four bodies read as well, and those have not been read here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "Fragments of an unknown and older teaching, as the book's own subtitle has it, rather than a doctrine set out in talks of the early twentieth century and published in 1949." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:FourfoldNegationOfTheEndState a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vierfache Verneinung des Endzustands"@de,
        "The fourfold negation of the end state"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā XXV, at the four theories concerning the state after release and the four concerning the limits of the world (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)" ;
    skos:definition "The treatment of the state after release under four theories that are all refused: that the released one exists after death, that he does not exist, that he both exists and does not, and that he neither exists nor does not; with the parallel refusal of the corresponding four theories about the limits of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "For the telic axis in prima-materia#52 this is the plain form of pm:selfBoundaryDeclined, and it is worth keeping distinct from the case in traditions/advaita-vedanta.ttl, where the question is not refused but assigned to a level that is itself superseded. The two look alike from a distance and are not the same move. No orientation is asserted on this node yet; the placement needs the verses on the self read as well. See prima-materia#63."@en,
        "The point of holding this is that it is not a third answer. The four alternatives are laid out in order to be declined together, and declining the fourth is what keeps the move from collapsing into the third: a position that said only neither would be the fourth alternative rather than the refusal of it."@en,
        "The same source guards against the reading that is nearest to hand. Reality is said to be neither existent nor non-existent, and the dialectic is described as negating not only every affirmation about it but every negation as well; it is expressly not the claim that the absolute is a non-entity, only that it is inaccessible to thought. A record that files this as nihilism reports the objection and not the position."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Madhyamaka .

pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsSoulSeizing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der vierte Grad bringt das Ergreifen des Schattens hervor"@de,
        "The fourth degree yields the seizing of the shadow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideSoulSeizing .

pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der vierte Grad bringt das Gedankenlesen hervor"@de,
        "The fourth degree yields the reading of thoughts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideThoughtReading .

pmc:FraudKnownAndBeliefHeld a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Betrug ist bekannt und der Glaube bleibt"@de,
        "The fraud is known and the belief is held"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 105-107" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission holds both at once: that the objects the witch-doctors draw from the bodies of their patients are produced by sleight of hand, with the manner of the deception described in detail by laymen, and that some of them nevertheless work real cures. Scepticism is directed at particular practitioners and turned into an explanation of their failures, so that it supports the standing of the rest."@en ;
    skos:note "A layman's account taken down by the recorder explains not only how the trick is done but how a cured man comes to believe he was cured by it. The doubt is stated in the terms of the transmission itself: a witch-doctor is a cheat because his medicines are poor and a liar because he has no witchcraft in him."@en,
        "This is the passage this graph most needs from the tradition, and it cuts against the usual report of it. What is described is not credulity but a transmission that has a settled place for its own failures, states its scepticism openly, and is not thereby weakened. Whether that makes it closed against refutation is the whole later argument, and none of that argument was read here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:FusionOfTheFourInformants a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vier Gewährsleute zu einer Darstellung verschmolzen"@de,
        "Four informants fused into one account"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1950" ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-274" ;
    skos:definition "The recorders state that the material was put in order in the sense that the sayings of the four principal informants were fused into one and the same exposition. What the printed system presents as a single doctrine is therefore a composite, and no sentence in it can be assigned to a named speaker."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an ordering act because the recorders describe it as one in the same breath in which they claim to give the raw documents. The two claims are in tension on the same page: material that has been fused across four speakers is no longer raw, and the fusion is the step at which a disagreement between informants would have become invisible."@en,
        "One informant is described as having supplied the system in its entirety and the others only its essential parts, and one of them obtained a supplement between the visits of the enquirers, from people elsewhere. The account is thus composite in a second sense: part of it was gathered by an informant on the enquirers' behalf between their stays."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:EmmeYaTolo,
        pmc:PoTolo,
        pmc:PoToloAsAxis,
        pmc:PoToloOrbit .

pmc:GadreelTeachesWeaponsOfDeath a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Gadreel offenbart die Waffen des Todes"@de,
        "Gadreel discloses the weapons of death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:WeaponsOfDeath ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Gadreel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:GradedAttainmentYieldsDistantPerceptionAndOtherMinds a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Eine gestufte Erlangung soll Fernhören und Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervorbringen"@de,
        "A graded attainment is said to yield hearing at a distance and knowledge of other minds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
        "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168 and 170" ;
    skos:definition "Two transmissions state the same claim in the same form. A named procedure, entered by stages, is said to produce two named capacities: perception of what is beyond the range of the senses, and knowledge of what is in another mind. In the one the procedure is the sustained application of attention to a single object, and the capacities follow verse by verse. In the other the procedure is an initiation into a graded society, and the capacities are read off the marks of the degree."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact question, carefully. No route is proposed by anyone between the Anishinaabe of the upper Great Lakes and the Sanskrit commentarial literature, and the dispute recorded at pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute does not touch this: whether the society formed before European contact or in reaction to it, a seventeenth-century Great Lakes formation has no access to the third pāda either way. What the dispute does affect is the age of the Midewiwin claim, not its independence."@en,
        "The one real objection to independence lies in the recording and not in the traditions, and it is stated here rather than left for a reader to find. The Midewiwin account was written down between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine, by which time the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for a decade through theosophical publishing. Two things weigh against the objection. The recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts — marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The objection is not thereby closed; it is entered so that it can be closed or sustained by someone who reads the charts."@en,
        "Two differences that a correspondence must not smooth. The Indian text ranks its capacities as obstacles with respect to the goal of the practice; the American report names no such rank and warns instead against the member who turns them to harm. And the Indian claim is a claim about attention, repeatable in principle by anyone who applies it, where the American one is a claim about an institution, obtainable only from priests and only for a fee that the report says impoverishes the candidate. The capacity is the same; what it costs and what it is for are not."@en,
        "What makes this worth holding is not the capacities, which are common, but the form of the claim. Both traditions bind the capacity to a procedure and to a place in a sequence, rather than ascribing it to a figure or a birth. That form is what a protocol can address; an ascription to a hero is not."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:DivyaSrotra,
        pmc:MideDistantHearing,
        pmc:MideThoughtReading,
        pmc:ParacittaJnana ;
    pm:independenceGround "This is the case in the collection where the gathering situation decides, and it is entered in full because the date alone would clear a claim the date cannot clear. The American material was gathered between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine and printed in eighteen ninety-one; the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for roughly a decade by then through theosophical publishing, so the fixation does not precede the material it agrees with. What carries the independence instead is twofold: the recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts, marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The second point is what a date cannot supply, because it puts the agreement in an object rather than in an answer. The ground is not thereby closed; it can be sustained or overturned by someone who reads the charts." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:GradedSpecialists a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gestuften Spezialisten"@de,
        "The graded specialists"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 278 [280], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "The offices are named separately because they are separate. The one who speaks with the huaca, keeps it, carries the offerings, sets the fasts and answers the people in its name; the ones who do the same for the ancestor bodies, for the lightning and for the sun; the lesser minister who assists each of them; the one who cures; the one who hears confession; the one who makes the chicha; and a series of diviners named by their instruments — by heaps of maize counted odd or even, by the twitching of the upper arms, by the legs of a large spider broken one by one, by dreams, and by opening a guinea pig with the thumbnail and reading where the blood runs."@en ;
    skos:note "Every office in it is held by women as well as men, the confessing included, on the recorder's express statement; he adds that the principal offices were more often men's and the lesser ones, divining and making the chicha, more often women's."@en,
        "The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen names four classes with Quechua terms and the extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one names two of the same as its general terms. Two recorders of opposite standing therefore agree on the vocabulary while disagreeing about almost everything else, which is the strongest thing that can be said for this list."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:GreaterPresupposeLesser a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die großen Mysterien setzen die kleinen voraus"@de,
        "The greater mysteries presuppose the lesser"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C" ;
    skos:note "The requirement is institutional and concerns admission, not efficacy. The inscription regulates participation in both rites; nothing in it says that the greater rite would fail for one who had skipped the lesser, only that such a one is not admitted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GreaterMysteries ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:LesserMysteries ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EleusinianMysteries .

pmc:HandSideTransferProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Seite der Hand einen Unterschied macht"@de,
        "Whether the side of the hand makes a difference"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the narrow half of the claim: whether contact through the left hand differs in its reported or measured effect from contact through the right."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution about what a positive result would mean. Handedness, the side a practitioner was taught on, and the expectation carried into the contact would all have to be controlled before a difference between the sides could be read as a property of the sides. A protocol that found one without controlling them would have measured the teaching."@en,
        "Only the lateral half is examined here. Whether anything passes at all is the section's broad claim and is not addressed by this protocol; a result on the sides would say nothing about it either way, and the two must not be allowed to borrow from one another."@en,
        "The state is procedure without cases, which is rare in this holding and is the point. The falsifying condition below needs no instrument the field lacks and no access to a tradition: blinded practitioners, both hands, and a comparison. Nobody has run it. That is a gap in the record and not a verdict, and the distinction is the one SPEC §13 exists to keep."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The falsifying condition is the ordinary two-sided comparison, stated because the claim names a side and thereby invites it" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners blinded to which hand is in contact report or show no difference between left-hand and right-hand contact beyond chance."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:EnergyTakenFromAPersonThroughTheLeftHand .

pmc:HathaChapterOrder a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Handbuch ordnet Haltung, Atem, Siegel und Versenkung"@de,
        "The manual orders posture, breath, seal and absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I-IV (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The four chapters run posture, breath, seal, absorption, and that arrangement is often cited as though it were itself a rule. The rule is in II.1; the chapter order is only an arrangement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NadiPurification ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Asana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Textbestand und Verszählung des Handbuchs hängen von der Ausgabe ab"@de,
        "The text and the verse numbering of the manual are edition-dependent"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, Kaivalyadhama S.M.Y.M. Samiti, Lonavla",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, ed. J. Mallinson, J. Birch, J. Hanneder, M. Demoto and N.-J. Liersch (2024)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, ed. and tr. P. Sinh (1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement among editors about what belongs to the manual and how its verses are numbered. The widely circulated editions descend from single manuscripts and differ in the count and extent of their chapters; a critical edition drawn from more than two hundred manuscripts, with the oldest dated 1496, settles neither question in favour of the numbering that most citations use."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because every citation in this file carries a verse number, and a number is only as stable as the recension it comes from. The numbering used throughout this file follows the widely circulated vulgate text; a reader working from the critical edition may find the same passage under a different number. Naming the manual without naming the edition conceals that anything is at issue."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:HauAsSpiritOfTheThing a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Gabe trägt einen Geist, der ihre Erwiderung erzwingt"@de,
        "The gift carries a spirit that compels its return"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1925" ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Mauss, The Gift, translated by Ian Cunnison (London: Cohen and West, 1966), pp. 8-10" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a thing given holds a spiritual power belonging to its giver, that this power seeks to return to its place of origin, and that the obligation to make a return gift follows from it. The claim is put forward in a work of nineteen twenty-five, on the strength of the single Māori passage above."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an origination and not as a Māori claim because the generalisation is the author's. The letter speaks of one kind of valuable and of a forest rite; the obligation of the return gift as a universal law of archaic exchange begins here. That the reading of the passage was itself contested from nineteen twenty-nine onward is recorded in prima-materia#107, because those sides were not read at the wording in this run."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "the Māori doctrine of the spirit of the gift, and beyond it a universal principle of archaic exchange" ;
    pm:originatedBy "Marcel Mauss" .

pmc:HauOfTheGiftWordingDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wessen Hau: der Wortlaut des Ranapiri-Briefes"@de,
        "Whose hau: the wording of the Ranapiri letter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Elsdon Best, Maori Forest Lore, part III, Transactions and Proceedings of the New Zealand Institute 42 (1909), p. 439",
        "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271, as collated in Hēnare, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what the source text says, not about how to interpret it. The manuscript reads the possessive of the second person, which makes the hau that of the giver and keeps the giver attached to the thing after it has changed hands. The printed version of 1909 has the definite article in both key sentences, which makes the hau that of the object. Every later reading of the passage rests on one of the two."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a dispute over the text rather than over its meaning, because that is the part a later reader can check and because the substitution was made silently. Both sides were read in the same study that reports the substitution, so the collation itself has not been verified against the manuscript or against the 1909 printing in this run."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:HauOfTheGift .

pmc:HeatWithoutBreathProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die sanftere Form dieselbe Hitze erzeugt"@de,
        "Whether the gentler form produces the same heat"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether the effect measured in the practice survives the removal of its forceful-breath component."@en ;
    skos:note "No new evidence was gathered for this protocol and none was needed. The measurements already held at pmc:TummoHeatExamination separate the two components and report the temperature rise as depending on the forceful breath together with the visualisation, with the visualisation alone yielding the lesser effect. The claim that the breath may be dropped is therefore addressed by evidence that has stood in this file since the twenty-eighth of August."@en,
        "The finding matters more than its size. Where a draft and its source transmission differ on a component, and the difference is exactly the component the measurements isolate, the draft is testable at that seam and almost nowhere else. Removing a prescribed element and claiming the effect survives is the one kind of modification that instruments can settle."@en,
        "What is not settled. That the gentler form yields less heat is not that it yields none, and the studies were not designed to find the floor. The deviation recorded here is from the claim of sameness and not from the claim that the gentler form does anything at all."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "Temperature change under the visualisation alone, against the change under visualisation together with the forceful breath, in the same participants."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The separation of the two components is the design of the second study already cited in this file" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners of the gentler form show the same rise in measured body temperature as practitioners of the form with the forceful breath."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02" ;
    pm:tests pmc:HeatWithoutForcefulBreath .

pmc:HermeticPolarityOfMentalStates a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Polarität der Gemütszustände"@de,
        "The polarity of mental states"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1912" ;
    dcterms:source "Three Initiates, The Kybalion (Chicago: Yogi Publication Society, 1912), chapter X, Polarity" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that every mental state is a rate of vibration, that states fall into innumerable classes each of which has two opposite poles, that the positive pole of a class stands at a higher degree than its negative, and that a state may be moved along its own scale but never across into another class."@en ;
    skos:note "Opened for prima-materia#12, to date the step between the theosophical colour key of nineteen hundred and two and the numbered scale of nineteen ninety-five. Read at the wording, chapter ten."@en,
        "The finding is a denial, and it is sharper than a confirmation would have been. The vibration vocabulary is fully present and every mental state carries a rate. But the text states outright that the states belong to innumerable classes, each with its own two poles, and that movement is possible only within a class: love and hate are transmutable into one another, so are fear and courage, and fear cannot be turned into love. That is many two-ended scales and expressly not one scale carrying all feelings. A ranking of unlike feelings against each other is therefore not merely absent here, it is ruled out."@en,
        "What the later scales do inherit is the direction. The positive pole is said to stand at a higher degree than the negative and to dominate it, and a practitioner is described as raising his own vibration and inducing the change in another. Sixty years separate that from a numbered ladder, and the step is still undated."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A study of the Hermetic philosophy of ancient Egypt and Greece, so described on its own title page and taken by its readers for a transmission from Hermes Trismegistus rather than for a work of its own decade." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Three Initiates, the pseudonymous authorship under which the work appeared" .

pmc:HicksEmotionalGuidanceScale a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Skala der emotionalen Führung"@de,
        "The emotional guidance scale"@en ;
    dcterms:date "2004" ;
    dcterms:source "E. Hicks and J. Hicks, Ask and It Is Given (2004), ch. 22" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that emotions form a graded ladder that indicates how near a person stands to a desired state, with joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love and appreciation at its top rung."@en ;
    skos:note "The diagnostic marker is the top rung and its sequence: joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love, appreciation. Six terms, and the fourth of them is the one that gives the sequence away, because a list built from an older stock has no reason to place freedom between empowerment and love. A later list carrying this block, in this order, carries it from here."@en,
        "The upper block of this scale reappears almost word for word in later lists that present themselves as older. Recorded so that the wording has a dated first appearance to be checked against."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A law of the emotions, presented as a description of how feeling works rather than as one author's arrangement." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Esther Hicks and Jerry Hicks" .

pmc:HorripilationCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entsprechung der Haaraufrichtung"@de,
        "The horripilation correspondence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that the raising of the body hair is codified as a sign of an inner state in two Indian genres that have no common subject matter: a meditation handbook and a treatise on dramatic performance."@en ;
    skos:note "The strongest item in this file, and still not what it first appears. The independence is generic and not cultural: both texts belong to the Indian sphere, so a common background cannot be excluded. What can be excluded is that one borrowed the observation from the other, since neither is about the other's subject. A universal physiology codified twice is a finding about the physiology."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:KhuddikaPiti,
        pmc:Romanca ;
    pm:independenceGround "Here the ground is not a date and the node says so rather than borrowing an argument it does not have. The commentary is of the fifth century of the common era and the treatise on performance is older, so priority runs the wrong way for an argument from age. What carries the independence is that neither text treats the other's subject: a handbook of meditation had no occasion to take an observation about gooseflesh from a manual for actors, or the reverse. Both are received texts, so no gathering situation is at issue. A common Indian background remains available and is not excluded by this ground." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:SanskritDramaturgy,
        pmt:Theravada ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Both codifications arise within the Indian cultural sphere; independence holds at the level of genre and subject matter, not at the level of culture." .

pmc:HuacaAsAddressedPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Huaca als angesprochene Macht"@de,
        "The huaca as a power that is addressed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II" ;
    skos:definition "What is addressed falls into two kinds. The fixed, which cannot be taken away because they do not move: the sun, the moon, the Pleiades, the lightning, the sea, the earth, springs, rivers, snow peaks, high hills and certain very large stones, and the pacarina, the place each lineage says it came out of. And the movable, which are the ordinary huacas of a place: mostly stone, mostly without a figure, each with its own name, each belonging to a lineage, each with its own priests. Beside them stand the malquis, the bones or whole bodies of the ancestors, held to be children of the huacas and given the same offerings; and the conopas, held in each house and inherited by the eldest son, which are the same worship made private."@en ;
    skos:note "The division is the recorder's and it is his working distinction as an extirpator: the movable ones could be seized and burned and the fixed ones had to be taken out of the heart instead. It is entered because it is also a real division in the practice — a lineage huaca is public and a conopa is secret — and not because his purpose was neutral."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourteenfoldMastery a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierzehnfache Beherrschung voraus"@de,
        "Supernormal power presupposes the fourteenfold mastery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that it is not possible for a meditator even to begin to accomplish transformation by supernormal power without having first completed the development of control of the mind in those fourteen ways." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FourteenfoldMastery ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus"@de,
        "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Der aufzulösende Zustand wird für den erstrebten gehalten"@de,
        "The state to be undone is mistaken for the state to be sought"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the passage on identifying and the names given to it" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that in identifying a person is absorbed into what occupies them and becomes a thing, and that those who observe this state in themselves regularly take it for a merit, calling it enthusiasm, zeal, passion, spontaneity or inspiration, and believing that good work is possible only in it."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands beside the self-caution recorded at pmc:Panchatattva, where a text prescribes a rite and warns in the same breath that men of the age will take it up for pleasure. Two traditions with no route between them both place the danger inside the practitioner's own approval. Not asserted as a convergence here; noted as a second instance of the same shape."@en,
        "Recorded as a caution that names its own misrecognition, which is the form worth collecting. The warning is not that the state is unpleasant — it is that the state is valued, under names that make it sound like the goal. A caution of this shape cannot be tested by asking practitioners how they feel, since the claim predicts they will report it favourably."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "An observation of the school, reported as long known." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:IfaGeomanticIdentificationDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die sechzehn Figuren der Apparat der Geomantie sind"@de,
        "Whether the sixteen figures are the apparatus of geomancy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
        "J. A. Abayomi Cole, Astrological Geomancy in Africa, the account of the sixteen palm nuts, read in R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what the sixteen figures of the oracle are. One account has them as the base figures of the system itself, called mothers, from whose columns every further figure is compounded, and gives the practice no derivation from outside Yorubaland. The other, written by a Sierra Leonean author and reprinted in the same appendix as the bishop's extract, counts the sixteen as twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, one judge and a further judge got by combining the judge with the figure of the first house, and calls the operations of the diviners geomantic throughout."@en ;
    skos:note "Neither side of this was read in a modern study at the wording in this run. What the twentieth-century literature makes of the question — whether the figures were taken over and grafted on an older practice, whether the sand science itself has North African roots, and how either is dated — is untouched here and is the first thing the next run on this tradition should open."@en,
        "The second account is the reason pm:contactRoute on this tradition stands at disputed. Twelve houses, two witnesses and a judge are the working parts of the Arabic sand science and of the European geomancy descended from it, and a system that has them is not without a route to the Mediterranean and Islamic material this graph already holds. The first account describes the same casting and the same sixteen without any of those parts."@en,
        "The second account was read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints it, and carries no imprint there. It is entered as a side of a dispute and not as a finding about the history of the system."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:IfaSixteenMothers .

pmc:IfaMemorisationStandardUnmet a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der als unerreicht berichtete Gedächtnismaßstab"@de,
        "The memorisation standard reported as unattained"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu" ;
    skos:definition "The same extract that states the standard states that it is not reached: scarcely anyone has been found who has learnt the whole of the narratives and can recite them."@en ;
    skos:note "The node exists because the shortfall stands in the same breath as the demand. A record that carried the demand alone would report a feat of memory that the tradition does not claim is performed, and this is one of the few places where a transmission states the distance between its own rule and its practitioners."@en,
        "This is the point at which the tradition touches the exactness material already in the graph, and it touches it from the other side: there the rule is enforced, here the rule is conceded to be unfulfilled. No convergence node is written for it. The contact route of this tradition stands at disputed, so an agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaOduEnumeration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zählung der Zeichen"@de,
        "The enumeration of the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number" ;
    skos:definition "The count of the signs as the bishop's extract gives it: behind each of the sixteen representative palm nuts stand sixteen subordinate powers, and each of the whole number is itself called an Odu, which is to say a head; this gives two hundred and fifty-six. A further sixteen are counted behind each of those, which gives four thousand and ninety-six."@en ;
    skos:note "The two accounts read in this run do not generate the count the same way. The account of eighteen ninety-four compounds the further figures from the columns of two of the sixteen; the extract used here subordinates sixteen to each of the sixteen. Both arrive at a system built on sixteen, and neither states the other's derivation. The difference is recorded rather than smoothed, because which derivation a source gives is one of the things that would distinguish a locally grown scheme from a borrowed one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaRuleOfInterpretationClosed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Deutungsregel ist fest und verschlossen"@de,
        "The rule of interpretation is fixed and closed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of a figure is reported to follow an established rule, and the rule is reported to be known only to those initiated into it. The fee paid to a priest for being taught the art is reported as very heavy."@en ;
    skos:note "Two gates named in one sentence, and they are of different kinds. That the rule is fixed is a claim about the system and is checkable against any later account of it; that it is known only to the initiated is a claim about access, and the fee gives its price. The account is an outsider's and reports what he was not told, which is a weaker witness for the content of the rule than for the fact of its closure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaVerseCorpus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die den Zeichen anhängenden Erzählungen"@de,
        "The narratives attached to the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu" ;
    skos:definition "The body of narratives through which a sign delivers its answer, given in parables. One thousand six hundred and eighty are counted to each Odu, and everyone aspiring to the office of babalawo is expected to commit them, together with those of the other signs, to memory."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unvollkommenheiten der Einsicht"@de,
        "The imperfections of insight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Ten states arising in practice, among them luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure and resolve, which are not defilements in themselves but become obstacles when taken as proof of attainment."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook illustrates the error with the monk who held himself awakened for sixty years. This is the tradition's own answer to the question of how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Piti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der für den Übenden selbst unhörbare Atem, kürzer aus als ein"@de,
        "The breath inaudible to its own bearer and shorter out than in"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8" ;
    skos:definition "Two marks set on the practice: one is not to hear the sound of one's own breathing, and a feather held before nose and mouth is not to stir; and one is always to give out less than one has taken in."@en ;
    skos:note "The intake is at the nose and the release at the mouth. The distribution is the reverse of the one a modern draft assigns, which treats mouth breathing as a fault on the intake and says nothing of the release. The passage also fixes the pace by an external test rather than by a count of seconds, which is what makes it examinable at all. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:IndoTibetanReception a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Indo-tibetische Rezeption, keine Konvergenz"@de,
        "Indo-Tibetan reception, not convergence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the Indian channel-and-heat material and its Tibetan form, held as a node in order to be counted out: the route is documented, so the agreement is reception and carries no weight as evidence of an independently arrived-at structure."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because a graph that only holds findings cannot show its own exclusions. Any later count of independent attestations must skip this pair."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:InnerHeat,
        pmc:Kundalini,
        pmc:Rtsa ;
    pm:transmissionPath "The six yogas reach Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator; the transmission is named in the Tibetan sources themselves." .

pmc:InitiationPresupposesSaktipata a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Initiation setzt die Herabkunft der Kraft voraus"@de,
        "Initiation presupposes the descent of power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII" ;
    skos:note "The exegesis asks who is qualified for initiation and answers by taking up the descent of power, which places the descent before the rite and before the path. Later and other traditions have read the descent as the fruit of practice instead; the direction recorded here is the one Abhinavagupta sets."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Diksa ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Saktipata ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:InuitForkSignOfSurvival a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das an der Gabel gelesene Zeichen"@de,
        "The sign read from the fork"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The stated reading of the divination: if the fork falls the sick person will die, if it stays upright she will live. In the case related the fork settled upright and the sick child lived."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the sign is stated in advance and in both directions, which is rare in the material this graph holds. A divination that names its own failure condition before the outcome is known is examinable; one that is interpreted afterwards is not. The single case related is of course selected by the narrator's survival."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitHelpingSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hilfsgeister"@de,
        "Helping spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28" ;
    skos:definition "Spirits belonging to a particular shaman, called up by him and reported to act in the world on his behalf: to guard a child, to raise a barrier against the dead, to find what has been lost."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:JhanaPresupposesAbandoningTheHindrances a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vertiefung setzt das Aufgeben der Hemmnisse voraus"@de,
        "Absorption presupposes the abandoning of the hindrances"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:note "The discourse places the abandoning of the five immediately before entry into the first absorption in a fixed formula, without stating at that place what befalls one who enters otherwise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FiveHindrances ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise bringt das Wissen um den fernen Ort hervor"@de,
        "The journey yields the knowledge of the distant place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:NoaidiJourney ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DistantSight .

pmc:KasdejaTeachesStrikingOfSpirits a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Kasdeja offenbart das Schlagen der Geister"@de,
        "Kasdeja discloses the striking of spirits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:StrikingOfSpirits ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Kasdeja ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KaulaAboveTheOtherWays a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die kaulische Verehrung als der in diesem Zeitalter tragende Weg"@de,
        "Kaula worship as the way that succeeds in this age"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra XIV.203 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that in the present age success is attained by Kaula worship alone, and that the mantra received from a Kaula brings knowledge of Brahman, where the mantra received from one of the lower dispositions reproduces that disposition in the receiver."@en ;
    skos:note "Kaula is set above the other ways here, not beside them. That is the same order Wilson translates from a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava, in which the left hand stands above the right and Kaula above all — an order the Western pair of names keeps while reversing its valuation. Two witnesses, one of them located by chapter and verse in a named translation and one not; the second is still to be found. See prima-materia#56."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:KevalaPresupposesSahita a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das alleinige Anhalten setzt das Anhalten mit Füllen und Leeren voraus"@de,
        "Retention alone presupposes retention with filling and emptying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The instruction is to keep practising the accompanied form until the unaccompanied one succeeds. Recorded at prescribed order because the verse sets a sequence without saying what befalls one who skips it. The direction of the breath drops out at the top of this sequence, which is what makes the claim that inhalation draws and exhalation expels a claim about the lower rungs only. See prima-materia#27."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:KevalaKumbhaka ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:KhecariYieldsRetention a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die über den Gaumen gelegte Zunge bringt das Zurückhalten des Nektars hervor"@de,
        "The tongue set above the palate yields the retention of the nectar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.42 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The contact at the palate is attested, and what it is said to do is stop a descent. It is not bound to a phase of breathing anywhere in the chapter, and it is not said to carry anything upward. The same holds for the seal at the throat, which is described as closing off a downward flow. A scheme that pairs the palate with inhalation and with upward carriage takes a stopping and reports it as a conveyance. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Khecari ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar .

pmc:KnowledgePresupposesContemplation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Gotteserkenntnis setzt Naturschau voraus"@de,
        "Knowledge of God presupposes contemplation of nature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Theologike ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Physike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:KokabelTeachesConstellations a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Kokabel offenbart die Sternbilder"@de,
        "Kokabel discloses the constellations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Constellations ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Kokabel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KokabelTeachesSignsOfTheStars a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Kokabel offenbart die Zeichen der Sterne"@de,
        "Kokabel discloses the signs of the stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the constellations."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheStars ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Kokabel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:LeftHandPathTwoBranches a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Der linke Pfad, über sein Ziel bestimmt, mit zwei nach der Methode unterschiedenen Zweigen"@de,
        "The left-hand path defined by its goal, with two branches differing in method"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1997" ;
    dcterms:source "S. E. Flowers, Lords of the Left-Hand Path: A History of Spiritual Dissent (Smithville 1997), in the chapter setting out the criteria and the two branches" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the left-hand path is constituted by the goal of self-deification rather than by any particular practice, and that it falls into two branches: an immanent one, materialistic in outlook and pronounced in its transgression, and a transcendental one, centred on intellect and eternal forms."@en ;
    skos:note "That is the case this graph was given a separate method axis for. In the same work the goal is held constant across both branches while the degree of transgression varies between them — an author varying one axis and holding the other fixed, without drawing the axes. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67."@en,
        "The passage that matters most for this record is the one that denies a methodological definition outright. The source states that there are no categorical methodological proscriptions on the path in any of its traditions, eastern or western, and that methods are chosen pragmatically; the antinomian element is described as often strong, not as constitutive. The author who is usually cited as fixing the path by transgression therefore says the opposite of what he is cited for."@en,
        "The transcendental branch is the standing candidate for the quadrant that a one-axis scheme cannot name: a goal of self-deification without transgression as its means. Whether it actually sits there is not settled by this node and is the question of prima-materia#72."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The old distinction of the Indian tantric traditions, reported as though the two-criteria definition were carried by them rather than formulated in 1997." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Stephen E. Flowers" .

pmc:LegalActionPresupposesPoisonOracle a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rechtsverfolgung setzt das Giftorakel voraus"@de,
        "Legal action presupposes the poison oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is stated flatly and is not a matter of degree: the action cannot be brought. The passage dates from the period of the monograph, in which the declarations of the oracle had already ceased to be recognized as evidence in the courts the administration had set up, so the necessity is the transmission's own and not the colonial law's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Legal action cannot be taken without a decision of the poison oracle."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:LightKnowledgeCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das veröffentlichte System ist eine Enthüllungsstufe, nicht das System"@de,
        "The published system is a stage of revelation, not the system"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274" ;
    skos:definition "The recorders state once and for all that what they print represents one stage of the revelations granted to initiates of high standing who were not themselves responsible for the calculations concerning this part of the sky, and that the stage is deliberately distorted in both directions."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that cuts against the account carrying it, which is why it is held as a node. It makes the text unfalsifiable in the direction its later readers wanted: any discrepancy can be assigned to the withheld stage. It also means the article cannot be cited as the Dogon system without citing this sentence, and the reception has cited it without."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:LightKnowledge ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:LightningStrikeElection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der vom Blitz Getroffene gilt als erwählt"@de,
        "The lightning-struck is taken as elected"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fols. 6v-7r, on the camascas",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "When a man struck by lightning is left alive, injured but living, he is held to be as if divinely chosen for the service of the huacas. The earlier report gives the claim in the first person of the transmission: those called camascas said that the grace and virtue they had was received from the Thunder — that when lightning fell and left a man in terror, he said on coming to himself that the Thunder had shown him that art, whether curing with herbs or giving answers to what was asked; and the same was said of a man who escaped a river or a great danger."@en ;
    skos:note "The two do not say the same thing about what is received. The later gives a status — he is as if elected. The earlier gives a content — the Thunder showed him an art, and names two arts. The difference is recorded rather than merged, because a status can be conferred by the community after the fact and a taught art cannot."@en,
        "Two recorders, forty-six years apart, in different regions, on different sides of the colonial administration, one writing in Cuzco and one in the archdiocese of Lima, give the same claim. Neither cites the other. This is the best-attested single claim in the file, and the only one for which the internal date spread is wide enough to be worth stating."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:LimbOrderDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Glieder der Reihe nach zu durchlaufen sind"@de,
        "Whether the limbs must be traversed in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
        "Vyāsa, Yogabhāṣya on Yogasūtra II.29" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about whether the eight limbs form a sequence that must be climbed. The sūtra that names them enumerates and says nothing about order; the commentarial layer introduces the categories of outer and inner limb, and later teaching hardens these into a rule of practice."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement is not between two traditions but between a base text and its readers, which is the commonest shape a dispute takes in this collection and the easiest to lose, because the readers usually win by default."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:AstangaLimbs .

pmc:LimbPracticeYieldsLightOfKnowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Übung der Glieder bringt das Leuchten der Erkenntnis hervor"@de,
        "Practice of the limbs yields the light of knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28" ;
    skos:note "The sūtra makes the arising conditional on the wearing away of impurity, which is itself said to come from the practice. The condition is part of the claim and is not to be dropped when the claim is quoted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:LightOfKnowledge .

pmc:LovePresupposesApatheia a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Liebe setzt Apatheia voraus, Erkenntnis die Liebe"@de,
        "Love presupposes apatheia, and knowledge presupposes love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84" ;
    skos:note "The chain is stated as generation and not as sequence: each term is the child or the flower of the one before it. That is why the strength here is necessity, and why a consequence can be quoted at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Love is called the offspring of apatheia and is said to keep the door to the contemplation of created things, so that without it the door is not passed." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Agape,
        pmc:Physike ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Agape,
        pmc:Apatheia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:MakutuCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht spucken in Gegenwart eines Kundigen"@de,
        "Not to spit in the presence of one skilled in the art"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31" ;
    skos:definition "The witness reports that a person would not dare to spit in the presence of anyone he feared might be disposed to injure him, if that person had a reputation for skill in this art."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that names its own detection problem: the material the art needs is produced involuntarily, so the guard is against being present rather than against acting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Makutu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:Mana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mana"@de,
        "Mana"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 54 and 62" ;
    skos:definition "An efficacy that belongs to someone. In the passages read it is always somebody's: a father's, which may inspire the son so that he becomes the living representative of that mana and of the karakia with it; or an opponent's, which is asked for by name in an invocation before a stroke is made."@en ;
    skos:note "What the witness does not show is the thing the term is famous for elsewhere. In these passages mana is never a quantity of impersonal force at large in the world; it is held, inherited, represented, and asked for from a named holder. The reading that made it an impersonal power belongs to a later literature and to a different archipelago, and it is not entered here because that literature has not yet been opened at the wording. See prima-materia#103 for the origination and prima-materia#105 for the philological dispute over the word itself."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:ManguMayRemainInoperative a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Substanz kann kühl bleiben"@de,
        "The substance may remain cool"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4" ;
    skos:definition "The doctrine holds that a man may carry the substance and never use it: it may remain inoperative, cool, throughout his life, and a man whose witchcraft never functions is hardly counted a witch. In practice the question asked of the oracles is never whether a man is a witch but whether at this moment he is bewitching the questioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause is what keeps the post-mortem from telling against the inheritance rule, and it belongs in the record beside that rule for exactly that reason. A finding of the substance where no injury was ever ascribed costs the doctrine nothing, and the absence of the substance is the only outcome that decides anything."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ManguSexLinkedInheritance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Hexerei geht auf die Kinder desselben Geschlechts über"@de,
        "Witchcraft passes to the children of the same sex"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 2-3" ;
    skos:definition "The substance is held to be transmitted by unilinear descent from parent to child of the same sex: all the sons of a male witch are witches and none of his daughters, all the daughters of a female witch are witches and none of her sons."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is checkable in a way few in this graph are, because the transmission itself draws consequences from it in practice and then declines the widest of them: in theory every clansman of a proven witch would be one, and in practice only close paternal kinsmen are so regarded."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ManguSubstance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hexerei als Substanz im Leib"@de,
        "Witchcraft as a substance in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 1-2" ;
    skos:definition "Mangu, described as an oval blackish swelling attached to the edge of the liver and lying beneath the xiphoid cartilage, in which small objects are sometimes said to be found. A witch performs no rite, utters no spell and possesses no medicines: the act is held to proceed from the substance itself. The location is known because the substance was formerly extracted by autopsy."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder adds that he believes the organ in question to be the small intestine in certain digestive periods. That is his identification and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here as his."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:MaoriEfficacyPresupposesExactRecitation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit einer Karakia setzt die fehlerfreie Wiederholung voraus"@de,
        "The efficacy of a karakia presupposes its repetition without error"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable. The same sentence is the tradition's own falsifying condition and is what makes the claim examinable at all; the protocol stands at pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination in convergences/recitation-exactness.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The witness says that to make an error, or even to hesitate, in repeating a karakia was held fatal to its efficacy. The consequence is stated for the whole address and not for a part of it, and hesitation is named alongside error." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:MaoriExactRecitation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:MedicineEatingYieldsSeeing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Essen der Medizinen bringt das Sehen hervor"@de,
        "Eating the medicines yields the seeing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89" ;
    skos:note "The claim is not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the medicines are vegetable preparations named by their plants and the capacity is a perception ascribed to the eater, so the practice is not the capacity under another name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:SeeingWitchcraft .

pmc:MideDegreesPresupposeOneAnother a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Jeder Grad setzt den darunter liegenden voraus"@de,
        "Each degree presupposes the one below it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-170" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passages read rather than of the society. The report sets the sequence, names the payment and the feasts due before each step, and says nothing about what happens to a man who takes a degree out of turn or without the preparation. A consequence would raise this to a necessity; none was found in the pages read. See prima-materia#119."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Gesellschaft dem europäischen Kontakt vorausgeht"@de,
        "Whether the society precedes European contact"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Harold Hickerson, The Chippewa and Their Neighbors: A Study in Ethnohistory (New York: Holt, Rinehart and Winston, 1970), p. 63, as quoted in Kidd 1981, p. 42",
        "Kenneth E. Kidd, A Radiocarbon Date on a Midewiwin Scroll from Burntside Lake, Ontario, Ontario Archaeology 35 (1981), pp. 41-43" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about the age of the society. One side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, assembled from older elements in response to epidemic, trade and mission. The other points to a birchbark scroll fragment from an archaeological context, radiocarbon dated to about the middle of the sixteenth century, which is earlier than any documented European contact with the central Algonkians."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because everything else in this file depends on it. If the society is a formation of the contact period, its agreement with anything the graph already holds has to be weighed differently, and the value at pm:contactRoute changes with the outcome."@en,
        "Only one side was read at the wording in this run. The dating, the laboratory reference and the argument against the nativistic reading were read in the note of nineteen eighty-one; the nativistic reading itself was read only as that note quotes it, and the paper of nineteen sixty-two that states it in full was not obtained. See prima-materia#118."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MidewiwinOrigin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ursprung der Gesellschaft und die Kette ihrer Übertragung"@de,
        "The origin of the society and the chain of its transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 166-167" ;
    skos:definition "The account by which the society traces itself: the culture hero instructed the otter in its mysteries, gave him the sacred rattle and the drum, shot the shell into his body, and conferred the secrets on him."@en ;
    skos:note "The chain runs through an animal and not through a lineage of men, which is why the society's own account of its age cannot be read as a genealogy and does not bear on the dispute recorded above."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:Migis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mī'gis"@de,
        "Mī'gis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 167" ;
    skos:definition "The sacred shell of the society, said in the origin account to have been shot into the body of the first recipient so that he might have immortality and be able to confer the secrets on his kinsmen."@en ;
    skos:note "The shell is not a token of membership but the vehicle of the transfer: the account makes the entry of the shell into the body the act by which the capacity passes, and the same act is repeated at every degree."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wurzelsiegel bringt das entfachte innere Feuer hervor"@de,
        "The root seal yields the kindled inner fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The pairing is set at the fire and not at the reversal on purpose. The manual defines the root seal as the drawing upward of the downward wind, so a claim that the seal produces that upward movement would assert only that the practice is what it is. The heat, the lengthened flame and the waking of the power are consequences the verses state beyond the definition, and they are what a protocol could address."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Mulabandha ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:KindledInnerFire .

pmc:Nahualli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nahualli"@de,
        "Nahualli"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The word as the recorder of sixteen twenty-nine glosses it: one veiled or disguised under the appearance of an animal. He connects the name with calendars he had found among such people, which distribute the names of animals across the days."@en ;
    skos:note "The gloss is the recorder's and is given in Spanish; the Nahuatl word is his lemma. That he offers an etymology at all is worth noting, because it shows he had the word from the people and not from a predecessor's book."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:NahualliGivenAtBirth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Tier wird bei der Geburt zugeteilt"@de,
        "The animal is assigned when the child is born"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The assignment is made at birth and not chosen later: the child is dedicated or subjected to the animal at the time it is born, on the strength of an agreement the recorder attributes to the parents."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder frames the agreement as a pact with the devil, which is his own theology and not the tradition's account of itself. What is left when that frame is subtracted is the timing — at birth — and the fact that someone else contracts it for the child. Both are entered; the frame is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:NavajoEfficacyPresupposesExactness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie setzt die Genauigkeit der Gesänge voraus"@de,
        "The efficacy of the ceremony presupposes exactness in the songs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:note "The consequence is graded rather than absolute: an error may be fatal, and only in some cases does a single syllable do irreparable harm. That grading is worth keeping, because the parallel claims elsewhere are usually reported without it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The witness says an error made in singing a song may be fatal to the efficacy of a ceremony, and that in some cases the error of a single syllable works an irreparable injury." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NavajoSongExactness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NavajoPublicErrorDetection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zuhörerschaft als Fehlerinstanz"@de,
        "The audience as the detector of the error"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "The practice, at the public night ceremony, that many of those present know the songs well enough to catch a deviation, whereupon the ceremony is broken off as fruitless and the gathering disperses."@en ;
    skos:note "The part of the claim that makes it examinable rather than merely asserted. The tradition does not only say that an error destroys the effect; it names who notices, when, and what visibly follows. Neither of the two parallel witnesses in this bundle carries that."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NoQuantitativeVerification a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Keine Zählung gegen den Zufall in den alten Quellen"@de,
        "No count against chance in the old sources"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 313-336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22" ;
    skos:definition "A negative finding, held as a node so that it is not lost: the old traditions test a claimed movement by its origin and its fruits, and none of those examined here tests a claimed capacity by counting hits against the rate obtainable by guessing."@en ;
    skos:note "Any protocol that counts outcomes against a base rate is a modern addition and is to be recorded as one, not as the recovery of an ancient method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:TestingOfThoughts .

pmc:Noa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Noa"@de,
        "Noa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 26" ;
    skos:definition "The state of being free or common, that is, not under the restriction of tapu. The witness states that persons and things under tapu can be brought into this state by ceremonies, so that the two are not fixed conditions but ends of an operation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:OfficePresupposesTheFast a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Amt setzt das Fasten voraus"@de,
        "The office presupposes the fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage. The source sets the fast, its lengths and its abstinences, and says nothing about what befalls a man who takes the office without it. A stated consequence would raise this; none stands in what was read. The variation between one month, six and a year is itself against reading a necessity into it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:OloliuhquiYieldsAnswer a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trinken bringt die Antwort hervor"@de,
        "The drinking yields the answer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer .

pmc:Panchatattva a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Tattvas des heroischen Ritus"@de,
        "The five tattvas of the heroic rite"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.58-59 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.61 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "The five elements of the rite belonging to heroic practice, named in the source as wine, meat, fish, parched grain, and the union of man and woman."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth element is parched grain and not a ritual gesture. Wilson renders the same list in 1832 with mudrā glossed as gesticulations of the fingers and maithuna as women; the divergence is his and not the text's, and it matters because grain makes that element a food where a gesture would make it an act. Recorded so that the current gloss can be checked against a verse instead of against another summary. See prima-materia#53."@en,
        "The same passage carries its own caution, and the caution is not a Western addition: the source says that the men of this age, being greedy and lustful, will neglect the discipline, drink for the pleasure of it, and lose the distinction of right and wrong. The warning against the rite stands inside the text that prescribes it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:ParalysisUniversalityProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lähmung allgemein ist und ihr Bemerken selten"@de,
        "Whether the paralysis is universal and its noticing rare"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the posited claim in two parts: whether the motor paralysis of the sleep threshold is a feature of every sleeper, and whether the rate at which it is noticed is low enough that a universal mechanism could go unreported."@en ;
    skos:note "A word on why the claim is posited and not sourced. It comes from an unpublished method draft, which SPEC §10 excludes as a witness, and the exclusion is not a formality: a draft that entered as a source could be confirmed by the very research it was written from. Holding it as a posit keeps the direction right, and the direction is that the draft is examined and never grounded."@en,
        "The claim has two halves and they are carried by two different bodies of evidence, which is the reason this protocol is worth entering at all. The universality is a matter of mechanism: the atonia of the relevant sleep stage is produced by an identified transmitter and receptor pathway and is a regular feature of that stage rather than a fault in it. The rarity of noticing is a matter of prevalence: a systematic review over thirty-five studies and more than thirty-six thousand participants finds that some seven and a half per cent of the general population report at least one episode in a lifetime, with markedly higher figures among students and psychiatric samples."@en,
        "The two halves fit, and that is the finding. A mechanism present in every sleeper and an experience reported by roughly one in thirteen are not in contradiction: they are exactly what the claim asserts, namely that what is rare is not the paralysis but the waking awareness that coincides with it. The state is therefore cases without deviation."@en,
        "What this does not settle, and the limits are sharper than the fit. The prevalence figure counts self-reported episodes and not measured ones, so it is a floor for noticing and not a ceiling for occurrence. The mechanism evidence establishes the atonia of the sleep stage and not that every reported episode coincides with it; the two are joined here by the ordinary explanation and not by a study that measured both in the same subjects. And a claim confirmed in this shape says nothing about what any transmission builds on the state, which is the part this graph actually cares about."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "7.6 per cent of the general population report at least one lifetime episode, aggregated over 35 studies and 36,533 participants; 28.3 per cent among students and 31.9 per cent among psychiatric patients" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "B. A. Sharpless and J. P. Barber, Lifetime prevalence rates of sleep paralysis: a systematic review, Sleep Medicine Reviews 15 (2011), no. 5, pp. 311-315, doi:10.1016/j.smrv.2011.01.007",
        "P. L. Brooks and J. H. Peever, Identification of the transmitter and receptor mechanisms responsible for REM sleep paralysis, Journal of Neuroscience 32 (2012), pp. 9785-9795, doi:10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0482-12.2012" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithoutDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol assembles two existing bodies of evidence, one on the mechanism and one on the prevalence, and states the joint they leave open" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A population of ordinary sleepers is found in whom the atonia of the sleep stage is absent; or reported episodes are measured and found not to coincide with it."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice .

pmc:ParityDivination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagung durch gerade und ungerade"@de,
        "Divination by odd and even"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the achicoc",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the diviner by maize" ;
    skos:definition "The diviner by maize makes small heaps of grains without counting them, then takes one away from one side and one from another, and according as they are left even or odd the lot is good or bad. The earlier report gives the same mechanism under another name and with another material: they cast lots with maize and llama dung, and if they came out even or odd they gave their answers accordingly."@en ;
    skos:note "In one village a practitioner of this office produced instead a bag of small stones which he said were called by a particular name and that he had inherited them from his grandfather for this purpose. The variant is recorded because it shows the office surviving a change of instrument."@en,
        "Two recorders, forty-six years and several hundred leagues apart, name different practitioners and the same mechanism. The count is deliberately not made before the drawing in the later account, which means the outcome is not determined by anything the diviner knows; that is the same feature the poison-oracle material in this graph rests on, and it is stated here of a maize heap."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:PenemueTeachesBitterAndSweet a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Penemue offenbart bitter und süß"@de,
        "Penemue discloses bitter and sweet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Penemue ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:PenemueTeachesWriting a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Penemue offenbart das Schreiben mit Tinte"@de,
        "Penemue discloses writing with ink"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)" ;
    skos:note "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:WritingWithInk ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Penemue ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:PolarityHarvestThresholds a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die zwei Schwellen der Polarität und der Zusammenbruch der negativen"@de,
        "The two thresholds of polarity and the collapse of the negative one"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1981/1984" ;
    dcterms:source "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 36.12 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 78.25 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 85.11 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), sessions 17.32 and 17.33 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a being becomes harvestable on one of two opposed paths at unequal thresholds — a slight majority of service to others on the one, an approach to totality of service to self on the other, with a region of indifference between them — and that the negative path is nevertheless abandoned at the density of unity, because it rests on an omission and what is not cannot endure."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination rather than as the teaching of a tradition. The material was produced between 1981 and 1984 by three named people in a stated procedure, and it presents itself as received rather than composed. That presentation is what pm:circulatesAs records; the node makes no claim about the source of the material, only about where the text begins."@en,
        "One caution for anyone opening the passages. The published wording of the omission argument names the wrong polarity at its first occurrence, and the correction is made in the exchange itself; a reader who quotes the sentence as printed quotes a slip. And the passage about abandoning the negative polarity in the early sixth density stands at 36.12, not at 78.25 as an earlier note in prima-materia#62 had it. 78.25 carries the different statement that the two paths must take in each other."@en,
        "The two halves of the claim do not sit easily together, and the record keeps them apart rather than choosing. Read from the thresholds alone the system is a dualism with two valid outcomes; read from the argument about the omission it is a monism in which one outcome is provisional. Whoever cites only the thresholds reports a symmetry the same text withdraws. See prima-materia#69."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A cosmology of great antiquity, received from a source outside the authors, rather than a text composed between 1981 and 1984." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Carla Rueckert as instrument, with Don Elkins as questioner and Jim McCarty as scribe (L/L Research)" .

pmc:PracticePresupposesInitiation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Praxis setzt die Initiation voraus"@de,
        "The practice presupposes initiation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:SaktipataTransmission ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Diksa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:PranayamaPresupposesAsana a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Atemregulierung setzt Festigkeit in der Haltung voraus"@de,
        "Breath regulation presupposes firmness in posture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.1 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The verse opens the breath chapter by requiring that the practitioner first be established in posture and in a measured diet, and that the breath practice then follow the instruction of a teacher. The condition is in the base text, not in a commentary."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Asana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:PrayerPresupposesWatchfulness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herzensgebet setzt Wachsamkeit und Führung voraus"@de,
        "The prayer of the heart presupposes watchfulness and guidance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness",
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart" ;
    skos:note "The writers of this corpus set watchfulness and the direction of a spiritual father before the prayer, and describe the way as passing from purification through illumination to perfection. The chapter divisions vary between the editions of the individual texts; the works and their titles are given so that the passages can be found in whichever edition is at hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Watchfulness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:ProphesyingPresupposesMedicineEating a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Weissagen setzt das Essen der Medizinen voraus"@de,
        "Prophesying presupposes the eating of medicines"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 96" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the source states a disqualification and not only an order. A novice who has eaten only a few times dances at the seance but does not divine, and the quantity eaten is what the source names as the reason."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A man who has eaten the medicines only a few times is not qualified to take a prominent part in a seance or to prophesy at it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:SeeingWitchcraft ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:QiListeningPresupposesEmptying a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Hören mit dem Qi setzt das Leeren des Herzens voraus"@de,
        "Attending with the qi presupposes the emptying of the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhuangzi 4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "In the dialogue the disciple is told outright that he is not yet ready, and is sent back to empty the heart before anything further; the hearing that stops at the ears and the heart-mind does not reach the qi at all." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Qi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfprotokoll zur Genauigkeitsregel"@de,
        "Protocol on the exactness rule"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the claim that a misrecited ritual utterance does not obtain its effect. The claim is unusually well placed for this, because two of the three traditions name an outward event that follows a lapse, and one of them names who notices it and when."@en ;
    skos:note "The Navajo witness supplies the sharpest form of the test and it needs no laboratory: at the public ceremony many present know the songs, an error is caught, and the ceremony is broken off as fruitless. The condition can therefore be failed by the tradition's own reporting rather than by an outside measurement, which is what the falsifier below states."@en,
        "The examination is entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised, and the difference is the whole point of the scale. A condition has been stated here; nobody has yet collected the cases against it."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the three witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case, from within one of these transmissions, of a ritual utterance known to have been misrecited and nonetheless reported by that transmission as having obtained its effect; or, on the Navajo side, a recorded public ceremony in which a detected error did not break off the proceeding." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy .

pmc:ReturnPresupposesTheSewingProhibition a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederaufleben setzt die Einhaltung des Nähverbots voraus"@de,
        "The return after death presupposes that the prohibition was kept"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the attempt to bring back a dead man failed because a prohibition had been broken, and not because the shamans were unable."@en ;
    skos:note "The structure of this passage matters more than its content, and it is the reason the node is here. A failure of the practice is reported, and the account assigns the failure to a third party's breach of a prohibition rather than to the practitioners or to the claim. That move makes the underlying claim unfailable from inside the tradition: any outcome confirms it, since success shows the power and failure shows the breach. Recording the immunisation is not an objection to the passage; it is the part a later protocol has to work against."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The narrator relates that the moon would not let the man come to life again, because his mother had sewn garments of new caribou skin on the island of Oqshoriaq, which is not allowed there; the two shamans who tried to catch the returning corpse did not succeed." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:ReturnPresupposesUnbrokenSinging a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rückkehr setzt voraus, dass der Gesang nicht abbricht"@de,
        "The return presupposes that the singing does not stop"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 56-57" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in plain terms and twice over: the singing must not cease and no one may stir him, and no fly or living creature is suffered to touch him. The recorder adds that the watching is for this reason and not, as another writer held, out of fear that the devil would take the body away; he names that writer and rejects him. The correction is his and is recorded as his."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "He would never recover out of the trance if they either ceased singing, or any one stirred him with hand or foot."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:DistantSight ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:NoaidiJourney ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:RubbingBoardSecondTestSeldomMade a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Test am Reibbrett wird selten gemacht"@de,
        "The second test at the rubbing-board is seldom made"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 171" ;
    skos:definition "Strictly the rubbing-board requires the same corroborative second test as the poison and termites oracles, with the opposite outcome for the verdict to be valid. In fact the second test is very seldom made, and important questions are taken to the poison oracle instead."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage is a self-warning of the transmission against a rule it lays down, and it is the sharpest one this file carries: the control procedure exists for the instrument that is used most often and is precisely the one it is not applied to. The recorder adds the inference that the Azande must be aware that the second test always confirms the first. That is his conclusion and not a reported Zande statement, and it is not entered as a claim of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:SakhyaRatiIntensification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf aufsteigenden Stufen der Freundesliebe"@de,
        "The five rising stages of love as friendship"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 3.3.106 (trans. Haberman 2003)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that love in the mode of friendship passes through five increasingly higher stages: friendship itself, attachment, supreme love, tenderness, and passion."@en ;
    skos:note "Given in the text as increasingly higher, so the ordering is asserted and the direction is stated. The series is named for one relation only; the edition read notes that a longer series of the expansions of love stands in the Ujjvalanīlamaṇi, which was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava .

pmc:SamyamaIsInnerToThePrecedingLimbs a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei inneren Glieder sind innerlicher als die vorhergehenden fünf"@de,
        "The three inner limbs are inner to the preceding five"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.7",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.8" ;
    skos:note "The base text ranks the last three limbs as inner with respect to the preceding five, and then ranks even those three as outer with respect to the seedless absorption. It sets a relation of inner and outer, not a rule that one cannot be attempted before the other, and it says nothing about what follows from disregarding the ranking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AstangaLimbs ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaCaksus a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama auf die Sonne bringt Weltkenntnis hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama on the sun yields knowledge of the world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DivyaCaksus .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama bringt das göttliche Ohr hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama yields the divine ear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DivyaSrotra .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama auf die Vorstellung bringt Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama on the presented idea yields knowledge of another mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ParacittaJnana .

pmc:SamyamaYieldsParakayaPravesa a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama bringt den Eintritt in einen anderen Körper hervor"@de,
        "Saṃyama yields entry into another body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Samyama ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ParakayaPravesa .

pmc:SantaAsSourceOfAllRasa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ruhe als Quelle der übrigen ästhetischen Zustände"@de,
        "Calm as the source of the other aesthetic states"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Abhinavabhāratī, on Nāṭyaśāstra VI" ;
    skos:definition "The commentator's claim that the calm state is not one aesthetic state beside the others but their source: the various feelings arise out of calm through their particular causes, and melt back into calm when those causes fall away."@en ;
    skos:note "Attestation is mediated and the reason is the language and not the reachability. The critical edition of the commentary is digitised and openly available, and so is the edition of the rival position named in prima-materia#363; the passage below was read in the English rendering of a study that prints and translates it. The mediated mode is therefore an open posting to be raised as soon as someone reads the Sanskrit, not a statement that the work cannot be had."@en,
        "The base treatise arranges its states by derivation, deity and colour and ranks none of them. This claim is the commentator's and belongs to him, which is why it stands as its own node and not as a property of the treatise."@en,
        "The reason it matters for this graph. It is a claim about the top of an order and not about an order running through: it makes one state the ground of the rest on metaphysical grounds, and it does not place unlike states on a common measure. Anyone citing it as evidence that the transmission ranks aesthetic states on a scale is reading it for something it does not say."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "J. L. Masson and M. V. Patwardhan, Śāntarasa and Abhinavagupta's Philosophy of Aesthetics (Bhandarkar Oriental Series 9, Poona: Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, 1969), where the passage is printed and translated" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SanskritDramaturgy .

pmc:SarielTeachesCourseOfTheMoon a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Sariel offenbart den Lauf des Mondes"@de,
        "Sariel discloses the course of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:CourseOfTheMoon ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Sariel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SarielTeachesSignsOfTheMoon a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Sariel offenbart die Zeichen des Mondes"@de,
        "Sariel discloses the signs of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Given under the name Sahriel; stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the course of the moon."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheMoon ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Sariel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SattvikaBhava a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unwillkürliche Körperzustände"@de,
        "Involuntary bodily states"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "Eight bodily signs held to arise from the inner constitution rather than from will: paralysis, perspiration, horripilation, change of voice, trembling, change of colour, tears, and fainting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SanskritDramaturgy .

pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernhören hervor"@de,
        "The second degree yields hearing at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:note "One node per pairing, following traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl, because a protocol would have to take the pairings one at a time and because the four capacities of this degree are not equally examinable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideDistantHearing .

pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantTouch a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernberühren hervor"@de,
        "The second degree yields touching at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideDistantTouch .

pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsForesight a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der zweite Grad bringt den Blick in das Künftige hervor"@de,
        "The second degree yields the look into futurity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:MideForesight .

pmc:SecondWeekPresupposesFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Woche setzt die erste voraus"@de,
        "The second week presupposes the first"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4" ;
    skos:note "The same annotation that sets the order removes any fixed duration from it: the weeks need not have seven or eight days, and one who needs longer in the first is to be given longer. The order is set, the schedule is not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:SecondWeek ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FirstWeek ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:SeizureTakenAsCall a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein plötzlicher Anfall gilt als Ruf ins Amt"@de,
        "A sudden seizure is taken as a call to the office"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "When a man or woman is struck by a sudden illness, loses judgement and is left as if mad, the senior ministers say that this has come because the huacas want him for their villac and priest; and on his coming to himself they make him fast and learn the office. The ground given is that they themselves lose judgement when they speak with the huacas. From this all who fall in fits are counted as chosen for these ministries."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission gives its own explanation of the sign, and the explanation is an inference from the practitioner's state back to the novice's: the office is recognised by the symptom that the office produces. The recorder adds his own competing explanation, that the loss of judgement comes from the devil or from the strength of the chicha they drink before speaking with the huaca. Both are recorded; neither is the graph's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:SerialReadingOfTheEightLimbs a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die serielle Lesart der acht Glieder"@de,
        "The serial reading of the eight limbs"@en ;
    dcterms:date "20th century" ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of the eight limbs as a ladder that must be climbed in order, each limb a precondition of the next."@en ;
    skos:note "The inner and outer categories come from the commentarial layer, and the hardening of the ranking into a rule of practice belongs to twentieth-century yoga pedagogy. Recorded here so that the reading is available as what it is. The base text at II.29 enumerates and does not order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "The commentarial tradition, sharpened in twentieth-century yoga pedagogy" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:AstangaLimbs .

pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zuordnung von sieben Zentren zu Eigenschaften"@de,
        "The seven-centre quality mapping"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1927" ;
    dcterms:source "A. Avalon, The Serpent Power (1919)",
        "C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras (1927)",
        "Christopher Hills, Nuclear Evolution (1977)",
        "Ken Dychtwald, Bodymind (1977)" ;
    skos:definition "The assignment of a fixed set of seven centres to spectral colours and to psychological qualities such as will, expression and security. An ordering imposed in the twentieth century on material that did not carry it."@en ;
    skos:note "Colour scheme in Leadbeater 1927, rainbow spectrum in Hills 1977, on the textual basis made available by Avalon's 1919 translation of the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa. Recorded here so that the mapping is available in the graph as what it is, and cannot be mistaken for the tantric material it was built on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "C. W. Leadbeater, extended by Christopher Hills and Ken Dychtwald" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:Cakra .

pmc:ShamsielTeachesSignsOfTheSun a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Schamsiel offenbart die Zeichen der Sonne"@de,
        "Shamsiel discloses the signs of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheSun ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Shamsiel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShemihazahTeachesRootCutting a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Schemichasa offenbart das Wurzelschneiden"@de,
        "Shemihazah discloses root-cutting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:RootCutting ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Shemihazah ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ShemihazahTeachesSpellCasting a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Schemichasa offenbart die Beschwörung"@de,
        "Shemihazah discloses spell-casting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SpellCasting ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Shemihazah ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kräfte sind Hindernisse für samādhi"@de,
        "The powers are obstacles to samādhi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.37" ;
    skos:definition "The capacities of the third chapter are named obstacles with respect to samādhi and attainments only with respect to the outward-turned state. The tradition that catalogues them is the same one that warns against them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:DivyaCaksus,
        pmc:DivyaSrotra,
        pmc:ParacittaJnana,
        pmc:ParakayaPravesa ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:SiguiDatePrediction a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorhersage der nächsten Zeremonie im Artikel selbst"@de,
        "The article's own prediction of the next ceremony"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on a dated prediction the account makes about itself. A footnote states that the next ceremony would probably fall in nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three if the reckoning holds, and adds that it was publicly known at the end of nineteen fifty that it would not be celebrated for another ten years or so."@en ;
    skos:note "A rare thing in this collection: a claim that fixes its own due date, published before the date fell. Whether the ceremony fell in those years is a matter of record, and the record was not read at the wording in this run. The date is therefore not entered and the state stands at procedure without cases. See prima-materia#254."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account's own footnote; the date of the ceremony has not been established from a work" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A dated report of the ceremony beginning in a year other than nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three refutes the reckoning as the account states it, and with it the claim that the interval is governed by a fifty-year period doubled." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Sigui .

pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Masken- und Fermentträgerserien als datierbarer Nachweis der Zeremonie"@de,
        "The mask and ferment-holder series as a datable record of the ceremony"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the one part of the account that leaves objects a later worker can count and date without asking anybody: the great masks carved for each sixtieth-year ceremony and kept in rock shelters beside their predecessors, and the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each and hung in the hogon's house. The account infers from series of nine, of eight and of fourteen that ceremonies were held back to the fifteenth and, on one reading, the twelfth century."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the falsifiable half of the account and the half nobody examined. The astronomical claims cannot be tested against anything but memory; wood and fibre can be dated. The protocol asks whether the intervals between the objects of a single shelter are compatible with sixty years, and whether the oldest are as old as the inference requires."@en,
        "No case series has been collected here and none is known to this graph. The state is therefore procedure without cases and not cases without deviation: nobody has carried the examination, which is a different report from having carried it and found nothing. See prima-materia#253 for the radiocarbon and dendrochronological work on Bandiagara material that would have to be searched before this protocol can be advanced."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A dated series from one shelter whose intervals depart from sixty years beyond what the dating method's error admits, or whose oldest object is materially younger than the count of objects requires. A single shelter dated cleanly against the count would equally carry the claim." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Sigui .

pmc:SitaliYieldsCooling a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Einatmung über die Zunge bringt Kühlung hervor"@de,
        "Inhalation over the tongue yields cooling"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the opening through which the breath is taken is not incidental here but is what the practice consists in. Two of the eight retentions the manual lists take the breath in through the mouth and give it out through the nose; two others require the mouth held shut. The manual therefore assigns the opening per practice and does not set nasal breathing as a general rule, still less mark mouth inhalation as an error. See prima-materia#26."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Sitali ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:CoolingAndQuenching .

pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit"@de,
        "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Majjhima Nikāya 118" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SixteenSupports a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzehn Stützen"@de,
        "The sixteen supports"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A set of sixteen named stations of the body, called supports, which the seal at the throat is said to bind; the manual names the number in a compound and treats the binding of all sixteen as one operation."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the number is the whole of the finding. A modern draft gives thirteen segments as the path along which the force is led; sixteen is what the base text names here, and other works of the wider corpus give eighteen. The count therefore varies across the transmission, which means there is no single path to be had, and a scheme that presents one number as the path has chosen a branch without saying so. The other witnesses to the sixteen and the eighteen have not been read at their wording and are not entered here. See prima-materia#35."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:SixtyYearReckoningPresupposesTheDiscovery a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Sechzigjahres-Comput setzt die Entdeckung des Same-Sterns voraus"@de,
        "The sixty-year reckoning presupposes the discovery of the seed-star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-283" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable, and not because the sequence is historical. What the passage establishes is that the tradition itself makes the calendar depend on the star, which is the claim the outside reception took over and the claim the examination addresses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The account states what held before the discovery: the year was not yet fixed as a unit, the only reckoning known was by seven harvests, and at the end of the seventh the supreme chief was sacrificed. The consequence of the prior step being absent is therefore named twice over, as a defect of the calendar and as a death." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Sigui ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Geisterrufen soll Verlorenes wiederbringen"@de,
        "Spirit calling is said to recover what has been lost"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made by the narrator about her second husband, that when a knife was lost he would hold a spirit calling and in that way recover it."@en ;
    skos:note "The most examinable claim in the portion read, and the reason it is entered separately from the capacity it names. It states a repeatable occasion, a named class of object, and an outcome that either occurred or did not; it is not a claim about a state of the practitioner. What it does not state is any case in which the calling was held and the knife stayed lost."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:InuitSpiritCalling ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings .

pmc:SpiritPresupposesBreath a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verfeinerung des Geistes setzt die des Atems voraus"@de,
        "Refining spirit presupposes refining breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4" ;
    skos:note "Recorded from the Yuan-period witness, not from the Han or Song works to which the sequence is commonly credited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:NeidanThreeStages ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Qi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:StatesAreNotOrdered a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zustände werden nicht der Reihe nach erlangt"@de,
        "The states are not attained in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:definition "A warning against treating the given states as a further ladder: they descend and pass, and no course of effort produces them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Ahwal ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:StationsPresupposeTheOneBelow a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine Station setzt die darunterliegende voraus"@de,
        "A station presupposes the station below it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:note "The handbooks set turning back as the first station and hold that the one below must be kept before the one above is reached. The enumeration and the count of the stations differ between the handbooks; the precedence of turning back does not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Maqamat ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Tawba ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:StillnessPresupposesRepentance a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Stille und Liebe setzen Buße und den Kampf gegen die Leidenschaften voraus"@de,
        "Stillness and love presuppose repentance and the labour against the passions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30" ;
    skos:note "The work is emphatic that neither stillness nor the love of God is reached without the prior repentance and the fight with the passions. It does not, however, state for every rung that the next is unreachable without it, and the image of the ladder is didactic before it is ontological, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:SuccessPresupposesNadiPurification a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Erfolg der Praxis setzt die Reinigung der Kanäle voraus"@de,
        "Success in the practice presupposes purification of the channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The text directs the breath practice at clearing the impurity of the central channel and gives the outward signs by which accomplished purification is known. It makes success depend on the purification without stating in these verses what befalls one who proceeds without it, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:NadiPurification ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:SuperimpositionUnderliesEveryPath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Überlagerung trägt jeden Weg, auch die Texte über die Befreiung"@de,
        "Superimposition underlies every path, including the texts on release"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction, at the definition of superimposition as nescience and the statement that all practical distinctions including the texts on final release presuppose it (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that self and non-self are mutually superimposed, that this superimposition is what learned men call nescience, and that it is the presupposition of every practical distinction — those of ordinary life and those laid down in scripture alike — between means of knowledge, objects of knowledge and knowing persons, in texts of injunction and prohibition and in texts concerned with final release."@en ;
    skos:note "For the axes in prima-materia#52 this is neither a value nor a third position but a denial of the question's standing. It does not say that the bounded self is dissolved or kept; it says that the level at which either could be said is the level of nescience. Recorded so that a scheme which files it as a unitive telos can be checked against the argument. See prima-materia#66."@en,
        "The clause that decides the matter is the last one. Not only the ordinary distinctions but the texts on release itself are said to rest on the superimposition. A path, a choice between paths and the scripture that recommends one are therefore all inside what the argument calls nescience — not false, but valid only where the superimposition holds."@en,
        "The same section removes the one who would choose. Without the superimposition the self does not become a knowing agent at all, and where there is no knowing agent the means of knowledge do not operate. The agent that a choice between paths presupposes is thus itself among the things superimposed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AdvaitaVedanta .

pmc:TapuBreachSign a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die verletzte Kopfhaut als Anzeichen des gebrochenen Fastens"@de,
        "The cut scalp as the sign that the fast was broken"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 42" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the tradition states for itself. During the hair-cutting of a child under tapu nobody attending may eat; the witness says that a breach is discovered at once, because the skin of the child's head is cut while the hair is being cut, and calls this a sure sign."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the tradition names an outward, observable event as the detector of a hidden breach and calls it certain. That is a falsifying condition offered by the material itself, which is rare enough in this collection to be worth holding as a protocol rather than as a remark."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case in which the fast was kept and the scalp was cut all the same, or in which the fast was broken and the scalp was not cut. The tradition claims the sign is sure, so a single clean counter-case on either side falls due against it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:Tapu .

pmc:TheEatersOfMen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Menschenesser"@de,
        "The eaters of men"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the cauchus or runapmicuc" ;
    skos:definition "A class named apart from all the other specialists and said to be the only ones who properly deserve the name of witch. They are said to gather at night under a master, who goes to a chosen house scattering a powder made of the bones of the dead, by which everyone in the house is put so deeply asleep that neither person nor animal stirs; he comes to the one he means to kill, draws a little blood with his nail from any part of the body and sucks what he can, for which they are also called suckers; what is so taken is carried to the assembly, where it is said to be multiplied into flesh, and is cooked and eaten, and the person dies within two or three days. The common phrase for the assembly is that tonight we are to eat the soul of such a one."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder states how the class came to be known and the account is unusually traceable for this file: it was not established until one man volunteered it after his examination was over, naming others, who were then arrested and examined singly and against one another. Sixty-three were found culpable in four villages of the coast, none at all in the highland villages at that time, and more later in two other provinces. He also reports the confessions in the words of the accused, including one who said he had been made to eat by force and threatened with death if he did not come again."@en,
        "The whole of it was obtained under an examination conducted by a tribunal with the power to punish, and the recorder says the punishment given during the visitation was deliberately light in order to keep them quiet until a heavier one could be arranged. What such an examination produces is a well-known thing and the count of sixty-three is not evidence of sixty-three witches. It is entered because it is what the record says the transmission says about itself, and because the shape of the claim — the night assembly, the taking of a little blood, the eating of the soul, the death within two or three days — is the material a comparison is made from, not because the events are credited."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:TheSpecialistIsPaid a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Fachmann wird bezahlt, und gut bezahlt"@de,
        "The specialist is paid, and paid well"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The recorder states that the practitioner is paid very well for the consultation and is additionally courted with food and drink after their own fashion."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because payment is one of the gates this project counts, and because it stands beside the other one: the same chapter says the practice is hidden from outsiders. A transmission can be closed by price and by secrecy at once, and the two are not the same gate."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lesung des Gefühls aus Farbe und Schwingung"@de,
        "The reading of feeling from colour and vibration"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1902" ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, Man Visible and Invisible (London: Theosophical Publishing Society, 1902), the frontispiece plate and the chapter Colours and their Meaning, pp. 81-82" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that every feeling has a colour, that the colour can be read off a body of subtle matter surrounding the person, and that the brightness of the colour measures the strength of the feeling."@en ;
    skos:note "Opened for prima-materia#361, on the suspicion that the modern one-dimensional ranking of emotions begins here. Read at the wording. The suspicion is only half borne out, and the half that fails is the more interesting one."@en,
        "What is here: a fixed key from feeling to colour, given as a list of the commonest hues; brightness and depth of colour stated outright as a measure of the strength and activity of the feeling; the vocabulary of vibration throughout; and an axis of self-reference applied to every colour alike, on which a thought with something of self in it turns downwards and one without turns upwards. The axis is the part that later scales inherit."@en,
        "What is not here, and the point of the node: a ranking of the feelings against one another. The plate is a grid of twenty-five fields, not a series, and the chapter introduces its colours as a list of the commonest ones. Where the text does rank, it ranks within a single colour by purity and luminosity, so that the same feeling has a lower and a higher form. That is a second dimension and not a scale. A later list that puts unlike feelings in one numbered order therefore does not have it from here, and prima-materia#12 cannot be answered by pointing at this node."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "An ancient science of the aura, reported as eastern in origin and as the common property of the older traditions." ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. W. Leadbeater; Annie Besant" .

pmc:ThreefoldAscentOrder a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Naturschau setzt asketische Praxis voraus"@de,
        "Contemplation of nature presupposes ascetic practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Physike ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:ThreefoldSeizure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die dreifache Heimsuchung"@de,
        "The threefold seizure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:definition "The office is not sought but suffered. The one marked for it is seized in infancy by an illness in which he is haunted by apparitions, and from these he learns, according to his years, what belongs to the art. Those seized a second time see more visions and gain greater knowledge. Those seized a third time — which is seldom without great torment or the utmost danger of life — arrive at the perfection of the art and become so knowing that without the drum they can see things at the greatest distances."@en ;
    skos:note "A graded scale with a stated cost at each step and a stated end state, which is the material an examination is made of. The recorder's own explanation of the seizure is that the devil takes a liking to the person in infancy; that explanation is his and is not entered as the transmission's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:ThresholdIsNotTheExecutionState a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Schwellenzustand die Vermögen trägt"@de,
        "Whether the threshold state carries the faculties"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about where the higher perceptual capacities are exercised. The handbooks of absorption place them in a waking, fully concentrated state and say so with a stated necessity; the ascetic literature marks the light half-sleep as the place of counterfeit consolation; the theurgic account, by contrast, treats the edge of sleep as the mark of a genuine divine communication."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Two of the three sides name the edition or translation they were read in. For Diadochus the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; the passage is to be reopened, and until then the chapter number stands on whichever numbering the earlier session used."@en,
        "Recorded as a dispute between sources and not as a verdict on the modern draft. A draft is not a side: it carries no attestation, so a claim it makes has no standing here. What the sources actually disagree about is narrower and more interesting than the draft's question, namely whether the threshold is evidential at all, and both sides of that are attested."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:Theopemptos .

pmc:ThresholdReceptionCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schwelle als Ort des Empfangs"@de,
        "The threshold as a place of reception"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that several traditions mark the passage between sleeping and waking as the place where a communication is received, while none of them makes it the place where a faculty is exercised."@en ;
    skos:note "Two axes have to be held apart, and the modern draft that prompted this examination runs them together. On the reception axis the threshold is well attested: a voice, a light and an immobility of the eyes in the Neoplatonic account, the nightly ascent of the soul in the Kabbalistic one, the true dream in the Islamic one. On the execution axis, where sight, hearing and the mind-made body are actively exercised, the sources bind the capacities to waking absorption instead. No source found so far puts an actively exercised faculty in the threshold state."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:ClearLightOfSleep,
        pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:transmissionPath "The correspondence must not be counted as independent attestation. The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa and is reception, as the file that holds it records. The Mediterranean witnesses are worse still for this purpose: the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory rests on a Neoplatonic substrate, so an agreement between the Greek, the Jewish and the Islamic account is a shared inheritance and not three independent observations. What would count as a finding is a tradition outside both blocks, and none has been located." .

pmc:ThresholdShutdownProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Lähmung eine Abschaltung ist und ob sie am Übergang geschieht"@de,
        "Whether the paralysis is a shutdown and whether it happens at the threshold"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the posited claim in its two parts: whether the event is located at the transition into or out of sleep, and whether the mechanism is the switching off of motor control there."@en ;
    skos:note "The location holds. Reported episodes occur on falling asleep or on waking and not in the middle of consolidated sleep, and the systematic review classes them accordingly."@en,
        "The mechanism does not hold as stated, and the correction is not a quibble. Motor control is not switched off at the transition: it is already off throughout the sleep stage in question, produced there by an identified transmitter and receptor pathway, and the experience arises when that shutdown persists past the transition instead of ending with it. The claim has the direction backwards. What happens at the threshold is the failure to switch motor control back on, not the switching of it off."@en,
        "The word regular carries a second and separate deviation. The mechanism is regular, but the recurring form of the experience is classified as a parasomnia in the standard nosology, which is to say as a disorder. Both can be true at once and the claim does not distinguish them: a normal mechanism appearing at an abnormal moment. Whoever repairs the claim must say which of the two the word regular is doing."@en,
        "This is why the two posits in this file were kept apart. The universality claim survives its protocol; this one does not, on the same evidence, and a single node carrying both would have had to be entered as confirmed or as deviating and would have been wrong either way."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "American Academy of Sleep Medicine, International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (Darien, Illinois, 2014), recurrent isolated sleep paralysis among the parasomnias of the relevant sleep stage",
        "B. A. Sharpless and J. P. Barber, Lifetime prevalence rates of sleep paralysis: a systematic review, Sleep Medicine Reviews 15 (2011), no. 5, pp. 311-315, doi:10.1016/j.smrv.2011.01.007",
        "P. L. Brooks and J. H. Peever, Identification of the transmitter and receptor mechanisms responsible for REM sleep paralysis, Journal of Neuroscience 32 (2012), pp. 9785-9795, doi:10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0482-12.2012" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the same two bodies of evidence that carry the neighbouring protocol, plus the standard nosological classification" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The atonia is shown to begin at the transition rather than to persist across it; or reported episodes are found in consolidated sleep away from either threshold."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:ParalysisIsTheRegularShutdown .

pmc:TokenBroughtBack a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Zeichen wird von dem Ort mitgebracht"@de,
        "A token is brought back from the place"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57" ;
    skos:definition "He returns with a knife, a ring or some other token of his knowledge of what is done in those parts, and on rising shows what he has brought from thence, so that the account may seem certain. The recorder notes that the other report he uses mentions no such thing."@en ;
    skos:note "That the two reports the compiler used disagree about whether the token exists is recorded with the claim and not smoothed away. He names the report that lacks it. Which of the two is right is not decidable here, and the disagreement is itself the reason no examination node is written."@en,
        "This is the most examinable claim in the file. It is not a report of a perception but the production of a physical object said to have come from a named distant place, in front of witnesses, at a stated moment. What would refute it is obvious and requires nothing but the object and the place, and it is entered chiefly for that reason."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:TrainingPresupposesPreliminaries a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Training setzt die Vorbereitungen voraus"@de,
        "The training presupposes the preliminaries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GenerationStage ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Preliminaries ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:TraversalPresupposesSequence a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Durchquerung setzt voraus, dass ihre Stationen der Reihe nach genommen werden"@de,
        "The traversal presupposes that its stations are taken in order"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, on purpose and against the reading a modern draft makes. The sources say kramaśaḥ and kramāt kramam, in sequence and step by step, and they say it more than once. What no source consulted says is what happens to one who skips a station. A prohibition needs a consequence somebody can quote, and this one has none; without it the claim is an instruction that a later reader hardened into a rule. See prima-materia#36."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:WorldPathTraversal ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:TummoHeatExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfung der Tummo-Hitze-Behauptung"@de,
        "Examination of the tummo heat claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that the tummo practice produces inner heat, taken up because this is the one pairing in the collection whose capacity has an outward side that instruments reach."@en ;
    skos:note "What the measurements bear on is the somatic claim and nothing beyond it. Reported temperature rises are consistent with the practice producing heat; they say nothing about the cosmology the practice is embedded in, and the node must not be read as though they did. The reported effect also depends on the forceful-breath component together with the visualisation, which means the claim that is actually supported is narrower than the one the tradition makes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:protocolledPractice ;
    pm:baseRate "Temperature change under comparable breathing effort without the practice, in matched participants." ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H. Benson et al., Body temperature changes during the practice of g-tummo yoga, Nature 295 (1982), 234-236",
        "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "H. Benson and colleagues; M. Kozhevnikov and colleagues" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners of the forceful-breath tummo method show no rise in measured body temperature beyond that produced by the breathing alone in matched non-practitioners." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat .

pmc:TwoPathsOfKnowledge a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Verzweigung der Menschheit in einen rechten und einen linken Pfad des Wissens"@de,
        "The branching of humanity into a right-hand and a left-hand path of knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1888" ;
    dcterms:source "H. P. Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine I: Cosmogenesis (London 1888), in the discussion of the fourth race, at the sentence on the branching of humanity into the right- and left-hand paths of knowledge or Vidya" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that humanity divided at a datable point of its history into two opposed paths of knowledge, a right-hand and a left-hand one, and that the two magics called white and black were sown as seeds at that moment."@en ;
    skos:note "Checked against the earlier work rather than assumed from it. The full text of Isis Unveiled (1877), both volumes, carries no occurrence of a right-hand or a left-hand path, and none of the tantric vocabulary the names come from. The usual account, which dates the introduction of the terms to 1877, is wrong by eleven years: the passage stands in the work of 1888. See prima-materia#58."@en,
        "The claim is presented as a quotation from a commentary of unstated antiquity, and it is the commentary rather than the author that later reporting cites. That is what pm:circulatesAs records here, and it is why this node is an origination and not an ordering: an ordering names what it arranges, and this one arranges nothing."@en,
        "What is new here is not the pair of names, which stands in the orientalist literature from 1832 and is held as pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature. What is new is that the pair becomes a history: two parties, a moment of origin, and a lineage of adepts on either side. Everything the later occult milieu inherits — that there are two camps and that a person may join one of them — rests on this move and not on the naming."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The ancient meaning of the two paths, credited to a commentary of unstated age rather than to the work of 1888 in which it first stands." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Helena P. Blavatsky" .

pmc:UbbegaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erhebungsverzückung"@de,
        "Uplifting rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of the five degrees of rapture, described as strong enough to lift the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:UncreatedLight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ungeschaffenes Licht"@de,
        "Uncreated light"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts (trans. Gendle 1983)" ;
    skos:definition "A light held to be uncreated, and held to shine through the whole person, body as well as soul; the defence is explicitly that the body participates and is not merely the site of an event in the soul."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a claim about what shines through the practitioner, not about an emanation the practitioner produces or directs."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:ValentinianConsummation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vollendung als Hochzeit, nicht als Verschmelzung"@de,
        "The consummation as a marriage rather than a merging"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I, in the account of the Valentinian consummation, at the passage on the mother's return, the brides of the angels and the removal of the creator (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)" ;
    skos:definition "The account of a final state in which the mother returns within the fullness and receives the saviour as her spouse; those who call themselves spiritual are stripped of their souls, made purely intellectual spirits, and become brides of spiritual angels; the creator, being merely animal, moves into the mother's place; and the souls of the righteous rest in an intermediate place."@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the telic axis as pm:conjugalTelos. The placement is a reading and carries the same burden as the claim it sits on: it rests on the bridal state reported in the passage, which has two parties and therefore neither one term nor an absolute. It is stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted."@en,
        "Recorded because it does not fit either end of the axis it is regularly assigned to. The spiritual ones are not dissolved: they are stripped and transformed, but a bridal state presupposes two, so what is transformed persists as a party to it. Nor are they made into absolutes of their own: they are married, not set apart. The end is a union that keeps what it unites, and that is a third case, distinct from the one in pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution, where the creature stays with itself against dissolution rather than entering a twoness. See prima-materia#70 and prima-materia#52."@en,
        "The passage is a report by an opponent and is attested as such. What can be said on this source is that the doctrine was ascribed in this form in the second century, not that its holders stated it so. See the caution on pmt:ValentinianGnosis."@en,
        "Two further things in the same passage that a summary loses. The demiurge is not destroyed but relocated, which makes the account less anticosmic than its modern reception; and the souls of the righteous, who are not of the spiritual class, are neither saved into the fullness nor lost, but given a place between. A scheme with two outcomes cannot hold either detail."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:telicOrientation pm:conjugalTelos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ValentinianGnosis .

pmc:ValidConfessionPresupposesCompleteness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Eine gültige Beichte setzt voraus, dass nichts verschwiegen wurde"@de,
        "A confession that holds presupposes that nothing was withheld"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source names the outcome of the want and not merely the rule: the confession was not good and is to be made again. That the finding of the want is itself made by a randomising device is the point of the neighbouring node and does not weaken the necessity, which is stated independently of how it is detected."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Where the bead breaks in two rather than three, or the straws come out odd, the confession was not good and the penitent is told to confess his faults again."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ConfessionValidityTest ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:ValidVerdictPresupposesCorroboration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein gültiger Spruch setzt den zweiten Test voraus"@de,
        "A valid verdict presupposes the second test"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity because the source names what fails to happen without the second test and not merely the order of the two. The qualification that a single test is often accepted stands on the practice node and does not weaken this: where the transmission requires the corroboration, it also says what the want of it costs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the second test no judgement is accepted as valid; where both tests fall alike the verdict is contradictory and void and the matter must be put again on another occasion."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:VengeanceSystemVeiledBySilence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Racheverfahren trägt nur, solange die Opfer ungenannt bleiben"@de,
        "The vengeance system holds only while the victims are unnamed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 6" ;
    skos:definition "Every death is held to be caused by witchcraft and to be avenged by lethal magic, and the death of the witch so struck is in turn avenged by his own kin. The transmission keeps the names of those killed by avenging magic secret between the kin and the prince, and the source states that were the names known the whole procedure would stand exposed as futile. Some Azande are reported as doubting the honesty of the princes who hold the oracles, and a few as having seen that the system is fallacious."@en ;
    skos:note "The defence offered when the recorder put the objection is recorded with it: a prince whose oracle has declared that one man died of another's vengeance-magic will refuse to put the second death to his oracle at all, so that the chain is cut by the authority that holds the instrument."@en,
        "The strongest self-warning in this file, and one reported as spoken rather than inferred. It names the exact condition under which the transmission's own central procedure collapses, and names the arrangement by which that condition is kept from arising."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:VespasianHealing a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Heilung durch Berührung, Vespasian zugeschrieben"@de,
        "Healing by touch ascribed to Vespasian"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a named ruler, in an account whose author remarks that those who were present still tell it although there is now no reward for the lie."@en ;
    skos:note "The remark about eyewitnesses is part of the ascription, not an endorsement of it. It records how the historian wanted the claim received."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:HealingByTouch ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Vespasian ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:VitalEnergyCorrespondence a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Entsprechung der Lebensenergie"@de,
        "The vital-energy correspondence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)" ;
    skos:definition "The observation that several traditions name something conducted or borne in the body and susceptible of deliberate direction."@en ;
    skos:note "The correspondence is weaker than it looks. Pneuma is a principle of cosmic cohesion and not a faculty; the breath of the Sefer Yetzirah is a medium of formation and not a conduction through the limbs. Only the Chinese and the Indian material carries the operative sense the modern draft assumes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:InnerHeat,
        pmc:Kundalini,
        pmc:Piti,
        pmc:Pneuma,
        pmc:Prana,
        pmc:Qi,
        pmc:Ruach ;
    pm:independenceGround "The two witnesses that carry the claim of independence are fixed long before any route between them. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows; the Chāndogya passage belongs to the early Upaniṣads and is older still. The earliest documented traffic that could have carried Indian breath technique into China is the Buddhist transmission of the first centuries of the common era, and both fixations precede it. Neither is an ethnographic gathering, so no question of a recorder's feedback arises; what each carries is the wording of a received text." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Daoist,
        pmt:Upanisadic ;
    pm:transmissionPath "The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator and is therefore reception, not independent attestation. Buddhist and Daoist exchange along the trade routes is documented and forbids treating the Chinese and Buddhist testimony as wholly separate. The Greek and Hebrew items are neither route-linked nor operatively parallel." .

pmc:WhatLiesStillWhileHeTravels a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was da stillliegt"@de,
        "What it is that lies still"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 56" ;
    skos:definition "Three positions are reported side by side. Some hold that those who cast themselves down are not really but only in appearance dead. Others hold that the soul departs from the body and, after its travel abroad, returns again. The compiler holds both to be false, on the ground that it is impossible for either man or devil to restore a soul to the body it has once left, and concludes that the devil only stifles the faculties of the soul for a time and hinders their operations."@en ;
    skos:note "No dispute node is written, and the reason is a rule of this repository rather than a judgement about the material. A dispute cites each side from its own work; here all three positions stand in one compilation, reported by one man, and two of them without an owner. What the passage establishes is that the question was open among those the compiler drew on, not who held what."@en,
        "The compiler's own position is reached from a doctrine and not from the material: he rejects the departure of the soul because it would be theologically impossible, not because anything he was told tells against it. That is worth recording, because the same move settles the question for him everywhere else in the chapter."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:WhetherTheHuacaSpeaks a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Huaca spricht oder der Priester für sie"@de,
        "Whether the huaca speaks or the priest speaks for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v",
        "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 280 [282], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the huacapvillac" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement between the recorders about what happens at the moment the whole transmission turns on. The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen says outright that these priests deceived men, saying that the huacas eat and drink and speak, which they did not do. The extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one says that the one who speaks with the huaca answers the people what he pretends it tells him, and then adds that sometimes the devil does speak to them through the stone. The earlier report of about fifteen seventy-five says without qualification that they had many huacas and temples where the devil gave his answers."@en ;
    skos:note "The chronicler's denial has a provenance he states himself: he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. Whether that makes his denial better evidence, as an insider's, or worse, as a man writing for the side that was burning them, is exactly what a later reader has to weigh, and it is why the sentence is in this note."@en,
        "The disagreement is not the ordinary one between a believer and a sceptic. All three recorders are Christians who hold the huacas to be nothing or to be demons, and the one who denies the speaking outright is the one who is himself Andean. What separates them is whether the office rests on an event or on a performance, and that is a question about the transmission and not about their theology."@en,
        "What the three do agree on is that few laymen ever saw the principal huaca and that only the specialist went forward and spoke. That much is common ground between the denial and the two affirmations, and it is entered separately as its own node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:MocharTheHuaca .

pmc:WhetherTheSelfIsKeptOrSurrendered a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Bewahren des Selbst das Ziel des Weges ist oder sein Scheitern"@de,
        "Whether keeping the self is the goal of the path or its failure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. Crowley, Magick Without Tears, chapter XII, on the left-hand path and the black brothers (electronic text of the collected letters)",
        "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what becomes of the bounded self at the end of the path, carried on inside the modern occult literature with the same term on both sides. One party holds that a self which is not surrendered ossifies and is consumed by time, and calls that the second death; the other holds that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment, and names it with a word of self-generation."@en ;
    skos:note "One asymmetry the record should not smooth. The earlier side values the matter from a unitive end and treats the surviving self as a remainder that time consumes; the later values it from a separative end and treats the same survival as the work itself. Neither is a misreading of the other. A continuity story running from the one to the other as a single tradition with a changing mood reports a break as a development."@en,
        "That is why the disagreement is worth a node. Read as a quarrel about practices it dissolves into nothing, because neither side defines the path by practices. Read as a quarrel about the end state it is sharp and not decidable from inside either work, because each describes the same movement and calls it by the opposite name. See prima-materia#61 and prima-materia#68."@en,
        "The sides do not differ over means, and both say so. The one states that the signpost of a right-hand and a left-hand path is barely legible, that the two look alike for a long way, and that the question is at which point they diverge; he puts the divergence at the crossing of the Abyss and separates the sorcerer from the brother of the left-hand path as sharply as a thief from a tyrant. The other derives his word from an Egyptian term for coming into being and applies it to the transformation of the will, not to any particular practice. Whatever is at issue between them, it is not method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration .

pmc:WhoseTheDrumIs a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Trommel der Sonne gehört oder Gott"@de,
        "Whether the drum belongs to the sun or to God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 476-477" ;
    skos:definition "The two recorders describe the same instrument and disagree about what is invoked over it. The compilation of sixteen seventy-four has the drum made from a tree whose grain turns with the course of the sun, because the tree is held acceptable to the sun, which they worship under the image of Thor; the whole art is treated as heathen and the answers as the devil's. The account of seventeen sixty-seven reports a named practitioner who, going to use his drum, marked himself and it with the sign of the cross as an earnest of good success, said the Lord's Prayer, invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel to assist him, and in the middle of the work broke out into a prayer to the God who created the heaven and the earth, calling himself a man liable to error, old and unworthy, and saying it were better that he should die than that God should deny help to the one on whose behalf they entreated."@en ;
    skos:note "Ninety years lie between them and the second recorder had read the first, so this is not two independent descriptions of one moment. What it is, is a transmission caught in the middle of taking something over: the instrument, the hammer, the ring and the rule of the sun's course are unchanged, and what is said over them has been replaced. The reading rule survived the theology that was given for it, which says something about which of the two the practice actually rested on."@en,
        "That the later station added something to what it received is exactly the shape pm:Reworking is for, and no reworking node is written: the addition here is Christian and the transmission it came from is not the one that performed the act. The class as it stands wants one tradition taking over from another, and this is a tradition taking over from the mission that was suppressing it. The gap is recorded rather than forced."@en,
        "The second recorder notices one detail against himself and reports it: that in calling the Holy Ghost an angel the man used a word he had never heard the Lapps use for it anywhere he knew, where they say Passe Vuoign. He records the anomaly instead of correcting it, which is why the passage can be used at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:RunicDrumConsultation .

pmc:WindAsSourceProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Wind eine Quelle ist"@de,
        "Whether wind is a source"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that moving air supplies the practitioner with something over and above respiration."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered on no procedure devised, and the reason is worth stating rather than passing over. The neighbouring claim about cold names a boundary condition that instruments already measure, so it could be turned around within an afternoon. This one names no quantity that distinguishes it from breathing in still air, and until it does, there is nothing to fail."@en,
        "What would make it examinable is a difference the claim itself must state: the same breathing, in moving and in still air, with the two conditions expected to come apart in some named respect. That is a small addition to the claim and it is the whole difference between this node and its neighbour."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "Nobody. The state records that no falsifying condition has been stated, which is a fact about the means of examination and not about the claim" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:EnergyTakenFromWind .

pmc:WitchcraftDreamIsExperienceNotSymbol a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein böser Traum ist eine Erfahrung der Hexerei, kein Zeichen für sie"@de,
        "A bad dream is an experience of witchcraft, not a sign of it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix II, pp. 230-231" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission divides dreams into witchcraft-dreams and oracular dreams, a nightmare being of the first kind and a pleasant dream of the second. A bad dream is held to be not a symbol of witchcraft but an actual undergoing of it: in waking life a man learns that he was bewitched only from a subsequent misfortune or from an oracle, but in a dream he sees the witches themselves and may speak with them. The soul is held to leave the body in sleep, and the witch to send the soul of his witchcraft to eat the soul of his victim while both sleep."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it stands squarely against a claim this graph holds open elsewhere, that the figures met at the threshold of sleep are the signature of the sleeper's own state and not visitors. Here the transmission asserts the opposite in as many words. It is one side and not a refutation; the other side is not in this file, so no dispute node is written."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:WoundsSharedWithTheAnimal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Mensch trägt die Wunden, die das Tier davontrug"@de,
        "The man is found with the wounds the animal took"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the man and his animal are one body for the purpose of injury: after a threatened person had a fight in the river with a caiman or in the open with some other animal, the man who had uttered the threat was afterwards found bearing the same wounds the animal had taken from the fight, although he had been elsewhere while it happened."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most examinable claim in what was read, and it is examinable in the ordinary way: it asserts a coincidence of injuries between two bodies at a distance, with a stated time relation. Nothing about it requires an opinion on whether the transformation occurs. No protocol stands on it yet; see prima-materia#231."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:XingqiInscriptionDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Datierung und Lesung der Jadeinschrift"@de,
        "The dating and reading of the jade inscription"@en ;
    dcterms:source "D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement among editors about the age of the inscription on circulating the qi and about how its opening is to be construed. One dating places it in the late fourth or early third century before the common era, another in the middle of the sixth; one reading takes the opening characters as a title and begins the text at the second line, another counts and construes differently."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node rather than as a remark, because the dating decides whether this is the earliest datable witness to deliberate circulation of the qi or one witness among several, and because a citation that names only the inscription conceals that anything is at issue."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:Xingqi .

pmc:YuruguPursuit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Yourougou in Verfolgung Yasiguis"@de,
        "Yourougou in pursuit of Yasigui"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 292" ;
    skos:definition "The account in which one twin tore away a piece of the placenta that became the earth, was turned into the pale fox for it, and communicated his own impurity to the earth so that it became dry and barren; his fate is to pursue his twin, who is also his female soul, until the end of time. On the mythical plane the seed-star is this fox, held in space and turning tirelessly around Sirius, that is around the pursued twin, without ever reaching her."@en ;
    skos:note "The pursuit that never arrives is the same figure as the orbit that never closes on its centre. Whether the star is the image of the myth or the myth the reading of the star is exactly the question the article declines to raise."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:ZandeOracleSetDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Welche Orakel die Überlieferung als die ihren zählt"@de,
        "Which oracles the transmission counts as its own"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande: introduction à une ethnographie générale des bassins de l'Ubangi-Uele et de l'Aruwimi (Bruxelles: Maurice Lamertin, 1921), chapter V, Procédés divinatoires (mantique), pp. 183-184",
        "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, p. 228, and p. 164" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement between the two recorders about the extent and the naming of the Zande set of oracles. The monograph gives the generic term as soroka and names four principal oracles: the poison, the rubbing-board, the termites and a three-stick oracle called mapingo. The earlier Belgian ethnography gives the generic term as lora, lists eight procedures, and assigns the pile-of-sticks oracle to the Abarambo, Mangbetu and Bakango and to Azande of those origins rather than to the transmission at large. It also describes the termites procedure differently, as a plaited grass braid laid in a small pit and read from whether the termites cut it through or gnaw only its underside."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement matters beyond terminology. Which procedures belong to the transmission and which were taken over from neighbours is exactly the question a contact route turns on, and the two recorders answer it differently for the same set. Neither was read against the other in a modern study in this run."@en,
        "The two accounts also differ on who receives the poison. The Belgian ethnography, written sixteen years earlier and on the other side of the border, gives the ordeal as administered to a suspected person or to a fowl, in the present tense and without qualification. The monograph gives administration to human beings as a thing of the past and very rare even then. Whether that is a change over sixteen years, a difference between the two administrations, or a difference of observation is not decidable from the two passages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:OracleRankOrder .

pmc:ZiqelTeachesSignsOfShootingStars a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Ziqel offenbart die Zeichen der Sternschnuppen"@de,
        "Ziqel discloses the signs of the shooting stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with Ezeqeel and the knowledge of the clouds."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfShootingStars ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Ziqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ZuniEfficacyPresupposesExactness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das öffentlich gesprochene Gebet setzt buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe voraus"@de,
        "The public prayer presupposes letter-perfect delivery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616" ;
    skos:note "Entered one step below the Māori and Navajo parallels and for a reason that is a property of the passage and not of the tradition: the requirement is stated, the consequence of failing it is not. Raising this to stated necessity would need a place in the corpus that says what a misrecited prayer does, and the body of the collection was not read. See the convergence node and prima-materia#112."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmc:ZuniPrayerPossession a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gebet als Besitz"@de,
        "The prayer as a possession"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615" ;
    skos:definition "Prayers held to carry power in themselves, so that having them is a source of power and losing or impairing them is a great danger."@en ;
    skos:note "The nearest thing the passages read give to a stated consequence of getting a prayer wrong, and deliberately not entered as one: the sentence speaks of loss and impairment of the prayer as a possession, not of the effect of a misrecited performance."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

<https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology> a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dcterms:title "prima-materia core ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Consciousness-first meta-ontology. Processes, not substances."@en .

pm:DeepSleepState a pm:AwarenessContext ;
    rdfs:label "Tiefschlafzustand (Suṣupti)"@de,
        "Deep sleep state (Suṣupti)"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 5" ;
    skos:definition "Undifferentiated awareness in which no object is apprehended."@en .

pm:DreamingState a pm:AwarenessContext ;
    rdfs:label "Traumzustand (Svapna)"@de,
        "Dreaming state (Svapna)"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 4" ;
    skos:definition "Inward-turned awareness of subtle objects generated by the mind."@en .

pm:GnosticState a pm:AwarenessContext ;
    rdfs:label "Gnostischer Zustand"@de,
        "Gnostic state"@en ;
    skos:definition "Operative consciousness in which an assertion is made from direct insight rather than from historical reconstruction."@en ;
    skos:note "Use this context when a triple expresses a claim from within a tradition's first-person perspective."@en .

pm:Manifesting a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Manifestieren"@de,
        "Manifesting"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The emergence of a form out of a prior undifferentiated ground."@en .

pm:Perceiving a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrnehmen"@de,
        "Perceiving"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of apprehending form within an awareness context."@en .

pm:Relating a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Beziehen"@de,
        "Relating"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of placing one concept in relation to another."@en .

pm:ScholarlyState a pm:AwarenessContext ;
    rdfs:label "Wissenschaftlicher Zustand"@de,
        "Scholarly state"@en ;
    skos:definition "Philological and historical mode of assertion: claims are reports about what a tradition holds, not claims made from within it."@en ;
    skos:note "Default context for tradition files in Phase 1."@en .

pm:TranscendentState a pm:AwarenessContext ;
    rdfs:label "Transzendenter Zustand (Turīya)"@de,
        "Transcendent state (Turīya)"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 7" ;
    skos:definition "The fourth state: pure awareness that is neither outward- nor inward-turned, and not a negation of either."@en .

pm:WakingState a pm:AwarenessContext ;
    rdfs:label "Wachzustand (Jāgrat)"@de,
        "Waking state (Jāgrat)"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad, verse 3" ;
    skos:definition "Ordinary outward-turned awareness of gross objects."@en .

pm:addedElement a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hinzugefügtes Element"@de,
        "added element"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Reworking ;
    skos:definition "The node carrying what this station added and the exemplar does not have. Required: an addition that cannot be pointed at is an assertion about a chain and not a link in it."@en .

pm:analogueConcept a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "analogue concept"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "A cross-tradition structural analogue, weaker than owl:sameAs and distinct from skos:closeMatch in that it asserts role-in-process similarity rather than lexical overlap."@en .

pm:ascribedCapacity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "zugeschriebenes Vermögen"@de,
        "ascribed capacity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "The capacity that an attribution ascribes."@en .

pm:ascribedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "zugeschrieben an"@de,
        "ascribed to"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Attributing ;
    skos:definition "The bearer to whom the capacity is ascribed: a named figure, a class of beings, humanity before a stated event."@en .

pm:assertedIn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "asserted in"@en ;
    rdfs:domain rdf:Statement ;
    rdfs:range pm:AwarenessContext ;
    skos:definition "The awareness context in which a statement is asserted."@en .

pm:attestedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bezeugt durch"@de,
        "attested by"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:Attesting ;
    skos:definition "Declares by which route a claim entered the record. Mandatory on every attribution, so that non-evidence cannot be forgotten."@en .

pm:baseRate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Basisrate"@de,
        "base rate"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The rate against which observed outcomes are compared, with the population it was taken from."@en .

pm:byPractice a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "durch Praxis"@de,
        "by practice"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:range pm:Practicing ;
    skos:definition "The practice a yielding claim proceeds from."@en .

pm:caseCount a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Fallzahl"@de,
        "case count"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    skos:definition "The number of cases recorded under this protocol so far."@en .

pm:cautionsAbout a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "warnt vor"@de,
        "cautions about"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Cautioning ;
    skos:definition "The capacity, practice or pursuit a warning is directed at."@en .

pm:circulatesAs a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "zirkuliert als"@de,
        "circulates as"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Originating ;
    skos:definition "The ascription under which the claim is commonly reported: the tradition, the age or the authority it is credited to and does not have."@en .

pm:collectiveAwareness a pm:AwarenessSpace ;
    rdfs:label "Kollektives Bewusstsein"@de,
        "Collective awareness"@en ;
    skos:definition "First-person inclusive awareness bounded to an identified group."@en .

pm:compiledBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "geordnet von"@de,
        "compiled by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Systematizing ;
    skos:definition "The named compiler who performed the ordering act. Without it, an order made in the present is indistinguishable from an order found in the source."@en .

pm:conformingMethod a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Ordnungstreue Methode"@de,
        "Conforming method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative means keep the norm of the order the path is transmitted in: what is prohibited there stays prohibited here."@en .

pm:consequenceOfSkipping a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Folge des Überspringens"@de,
        "consequence of skipping"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    skos:definition "What the source says happens if the prior step is passed over."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever the strength is pm:statedNecessity. Claiming that a text sets a necessity obliges the claimant to say what the text says goes wrong without it; a necessity nobody can quote a consequence for is an order read as a necessity."@en .

pm:contactRoute a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "contact route"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:range pm:ContactRoute ;
    skos:definition "The state of the contact question between this tradition and those the graph already holds."@en .

pm:convergingConcept a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "konvergierender Begriff"@de,
        "converging concept"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "A concept that participates in the observed correspondence."@en .

pm:corpusHarvested a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Korpus geerntet"@de,
        "Corpus harvested"@en ;
    skos:definition "The harvest has been carried out for the works actually opened, and what was left unread is recorded. The state claims completeness for what was opened, never for the corpus."@en .

pm:coverageState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "coverage state"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:range pm:CoverageState ;
    skos:definition "How far the corpus of this tradition has been carried into the graph."@en .

pm:dependentStep a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "abhängiger Schritt"@de,
        "dependent step"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    skos:definition "The later step: the practice, state or capacity said not to be reached without the prior one."@en .

pm:disputedClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "strittiger Gegenstand"@de,
        "disputed claim"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Disputing ;
    skos:definition "What the disagreement is about."@en .

pm:dyadicAwareness a pm:AwarenessSpace ;
    rdfs:label "Dyadisches Bewusstsein"@de,
        "Dyadic awareness"@en ;
    skos:definition "First-person exclusive awareness shared between two subjects (I and you)."@en .

pm:evidenceFrom a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Evidenz aus"@de,
        "evidence from"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "A modern work bearing on the claim under examination, given as a bibliographic reference or a work identifier."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A modern study is never a witness to a tradition and must never appear as the source of one of its claims; it bears on a claim only as evidence inside an examination. Keeping the two predicates apart is what allows research to enter the record at all without loosening the rule that sources are received works."@en .

pm:examinationState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfstand"@de,
        "examination state"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range pm:ExaminationState ;
    skos:definition "How far the attempt to fail the claim has been carried."@en .

pm:examinedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "geprüft von"@de,
        "examined by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "Who devised or carried the protocol. A protocol without a named author is unaccountable, and an unaccountable protocol is not evidence of anything."@en .

pm:falsifiedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "widerlegt durch"@de,
        "falsified by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The stated condition under which the claim counts as refuted."@en .

pm:firstPersonReport a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Erfahrungsbericht"@de,
        "First-person report"@en ;
    skos:definition "An experiential account given in the first person by the one who had it, recorded once."@en .

pm:hasAwarenessSpace a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has awareness space"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:AwarenessSpace ;
    skos:definition "Associates a process instance with the pronominal reach of its awareness."@en .

pm:hasClusterMember a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has cluster member"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:MeaningCluster ;
    skos:definition "Associates a MeaningCluster with the individual nodes that constitute it as a bounded subgraph."@en .

pm:hasSymbolicForm a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has symbolic form"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:Symbolizing ;
    skos:definition "Associates a concept with one or more symbolic representations (sigils, diagrams, glyphs)."@en .

pm:independenceGround a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Grund der Unabhängigkeit"@de,
        "ground of independence"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    skos:definition "The statement that carries a claim of independent attestation: when the testimony relied on was fixed or gathered, and against which publication or route that date stands."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever pm:independentAttestation is set, enforced by pm:IndependenceGroundShape. The burden is reversed on purpose. Feedback from a published account back into the field it describes can almost never be demonstrated, so a predicate that marked only the demonstrated cases would let the ordinary case pass in silence — the failure pm:CoverageState and pm:ExaminationState exist to prevent. A claim of independence must instead carry the fact that makes it checkable."@en,
        "The date alone does not settle it, and the sentence must say more where more is at stake. Feedback within a single enquiry — few informants, several years, the same team returning, material gathered by an informant between visits — precedes any publication and no date catches it. Where the corpus was fixed that way, the ground states the gathering situation as well. Whether it does so honestly is settled at the merge and not by a shape; naming that limit is better than a guard that pretends to check it."@en .

pm:independentAttestation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "unabhängige Bezeugung"@de,
        "independent attestation"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:range pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "A tradition attesting the correspondence for which no route of contact is documented, so that its testimony is not the same statement counted a second time."@en ;
    skos:note "The earlier definition called this the only kind of attestation that makes a correspondence a finding. It is not: it is the kind that makes the count honest. A correspondence carried by reception is still a finding about what was carried; what it may not be is a tally of independent witnesses."@en .

pm:individualAwareness a pm:AwarenessSpace ;
    rdfs:label "Individuelles Bewusstsein"@de,
        "Individual awareness"@en ;
    skos:definition "First-person singular awareness bounded to a single subject."@en .

pm:methodOrientation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung der Methode"@de,
        "method orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:MethodOrientation ;
    skos:definition "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of method. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other: a passage, an edition, and the counter-search."@en ;
    skos:note "No domain is declared, deliberately. The axis is stated of whatever a source states it of — a transmission as a whole, a single practice, a stage within one — and a declared domain would force the level before the material has settled it."@en .

pm:normDistinctionDeclined a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Abgelehnte Normunterscheidung"@de,
        "Norm distinction declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source holds that the distinction does not apply to its path: nothing is kept and nothing is broken, because it denies that anything needs to be renounced, purified or transformed."@en ;
    skos:note "The value exists because a path that declines the axis is not the same as one whose position on it is unknown, and a scale without it would force the first to be recorded as the second. It is asserted only where a source says so."@en .

pm:ordersClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ordnet Behauptung"@de,
        "orders claim"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Systematizing ;
    skos:definition "A claim that this ordering act groups. The claims keep their own attestation; only the grouping belongs to the compiler."@en .

pm:originatedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "aufgestellt von"@de,
        "originated by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Originating ;
    skos:definition "The named author with whom the claim begins."@en .

pm:positedIn a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gesetzt in"@de,
        "posited in"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Positing ;
    skos:definition "Where the claim was put forward, given as an identifier rather than as a bibliographic reference: an issue number, a draft designation. Deliberately not dcterms:source, because what is named here is not a work a reader can obtain and must never be counted as one."@en .

pm:prerequisiteStrength a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Stärke der Voraussetzung"@de,
        "prerequisite strength"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:range pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    skos:definition "How firmly the source sets the prerequisite."@en .

pm:principalCorpus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "principal corpus"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "The works in which this transmission is chiefly carried, named so that a later reader can find them."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A source carries a passage that was read, with its place and its edition; this names a corpus that was not read. Using dcterms:source here would let a registration claim a textual knowledge it does not have. The literature rule applies all the same: a work, never a URL."@en .

pm:priorStep a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "vorausgesetzter Schritt"@de,
        "prior step"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Presupposing ;
    skos:definition "The earlier step the later one is said to require."@en .

pm:protocolUpdated a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Protokollstand"@de,
        "protocol updated"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date ;
    skos:definition "The date on which the count was last carried forward. A protocol without this date claims a scrutiny it does not document."@en .

pm:readVia a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gelesen über"@de,
        "read via"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work in which the passage was actually read, given as a bibliographic reference, where that work is not the one carrying the claim."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately separate from dcterms:source, for the same reason pm:evidenceFrom is. dcterms:source names what a reader must obtain to check the claim; this names what the writer of the node had in front of him. A record that fused them would report second-hand reading as first-hand and would lose the one distinction that makes an unreachable source admissible at all."@en,
        "Required wherever pm:attestedBy is pm:mediatedAttestation. Carries a bibliographic reference and never a URL, enforced by pm:ReadViaIsLiteratureShape."@en .

pm:receivedFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "übernommen von"@de,
        "received from"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Reworking ;
    rdfs:range pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "The transmission the claim was taken over from. The work and the place stay in dcterms:source; this names the transmission, so that the depth of a chain can be counted."@en .

pm:reworkedClaim a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bearbeitete Behauptung"@de,
        "reworked claim"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Reworking ;
    skos:definition "The claim that was taken over, as it stands in the tradition performing the reworking."@en .

pm:selfBoundaryDeclined a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Abgelehnte Selbstgrenze"@de,
        "Self boundary declined"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source holds that the question is malformed for its path: there is no bounded self that could either be dissolved or preserved, so neither end state is what it aims at."@en ;
    skos:note "As with pm:normDistinctionDeclined, this is a positive claim about a source and not a place to put an undetermined case."@en,
        "The value was first written as the single place for everything that is neither dissolution nor separation, and the material has since shown that too coarse: a marriage is not a non-position, and a non-position is not a claim about which level the question belongs to. The two clearest of those have their own values below. What remains here is the plain form — the source denies that there is a bounded self to be dealt with either way — and the case where a source instead declares the whole level of the question superseded, which is a further distinction still to be made when a second instance of it appears."@en .

pm:separativeTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Absonderndes Ziel"@de,
        "Separative telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the bounded self is to be strengthened and kept as its own absolute, and treats its dissolution as what is to be avoided."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from self-assertion, ambition or the pursuit of power, none of which say anything about an end state. What places a path here is a claim about where it ends, not about how its practitioners behave."@en .

pm:telicOrientation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung des Zieles"@de,
        "telic orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:TelicOrientation ;
    skos:definition "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of the end state. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a source claims one end state for a stage and another for the whole, both are stated on their own nodes rather than averaged. An averaged orientation reports a path no source teaches, in the same way an averaged reading reports a text no witness gives."@en .

pm:tests a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "prüft"@de,
        "tests"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The claim this protocol attempts to fail."@en .

pm:transgressiveMethod a pm:MethodOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Übertretende Methode"@de,
        "Transgressive method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative means take what the surrounding order prohibits and use the force of the prohibition itself as the vehicle. The transgression is prescribed, not incidental, and it is bounded by the rite that prescribes it."@en ;
    skos:note "Not the same as licence. A path is transgressive on this axis only where a source sets the breach as a means; a practice that merely fails to observe a norm is not oriented by it at all."@en .

pm:transmissionPath a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Übertragungsweg"@de,
        "transmission path"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Converging ;
    skos:definition "A documented route of historical contact between the traditions in which the correspondence appears."@en ;
    skos:note "The earlier definition here said that the presence of a route downgrades the correspondence from convergence to reception. That was a judgement wearing the clothes of a definition, and it is withdrawn. A route says how a claim travelled, not whether anything was in it. Survival through many hands is selection, because most claims do not survive it, and a record that treats transmission as noise throws away the only evidence it has about what kept being worth copying."@en,
        "What the route does decide is narrower and stays in force: an agreement reached along it may not be counted as independent attestation, because the same statement would be counted twice. Both may stand on one node, and on several they already do. The route bars a particular way of counting; it does not devalue the correspondence."@en .

pm:undividedOutcomes a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "nicht geschiedene Ausgänge"@de,
        "undivided outcomes"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Testing ;
    skos:definition "The differing outcomes that the claim treats alike, named so that the charge of saying nothing is itself checkable."@en ;
    skos:note "Required wherever the state is pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate, enforced by pm:UndividedOutcomesRequiredShape. The burden falls on the examiner for the same reason it does at pm:independenceGround: a verdict that a claim says nothing is easy to reach and hard to contest, and one that names no pair of outcomes it fails to separate is an opinion about the claim rather than a finding about it."@en .

pm:unitiveTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Vereinigendes Ziel"@de,
        "Unitive telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the bounded self is dissolved into, or united with, something that exceeds it, and treats the persistence of the separate self as what is to be given up."@en .

pm:universalAwareness a pm:AwarenessSpace ;
    rdfs:label "Universelles Bewusstsein"@de,
        "Universal awareness"@en ;
    skos:definition "Awareness whose reach is unbounded across all possible subjects."@en .

pm:withinTradition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "within tradition"@en ;
    rdfs:range pm:Tradition ;
    skos:definition "Associates a process instance with the tradition in which it is situated."@en .

pm:workOpened a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Werk geöffnet"@de,
        "Work opened"@en ;
    skos:definition "A work of the corpus has been obtained and read and its edition is settled, but no passage has yet been entered as a node."@en .

pm:yieldsCapacity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bringt Vermögen hervor"@de,
        "yields capacity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:range pm:Conceptualizing ;
    skos:definition "The capacity a yielding claim arrives at. That the tradition says so is the claim; that the practice produces it is not asserted."@en .

pmp:AndeanDreamDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagen durch Schlafen auf dem Kleidungsstück des Fragenden"@de,
        "Divining by sleeping on the questioner's garment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the moscoc" ;
    skos:definition "The diviner by dreams. Someone comes to ask whether he will recover or die, or whether a lost horse will be found. If the questioner is a man the diviner asks for the band from his head, or his bag, or his cloak, or some other part of his clothing; if a woman, for her sash or the like. He takes it home, sleeps on it, and answers according to what he dreams. Where the question is about love, he asks for the hair or the clothing of the person he is to divine about."@en ;
    skos:note "The identifier carries the name of the tradition because the plain one was already taken by a practice of another transmission in this graph. That is a property of a flat identifier space and not of the practice; see prima-materia#275."@en,
        "The instrument is a possession of the person the question is about, not of the questioner, wherever the two differ. That distinction is stated in the source and is the only place in this file where the procedure names what it is a procedure on."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Zweiteilung älter ist als ihre Berichterstatter"@de,
        "Whether the two-way division is older than the reports of it"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that the naming of two Śākta branches as a right-hand and a left-hand ritual reports a division the Indian material carries, rather than imposing one it does not."@en ;
    skos:note "No case answering the condition has been found so far, and two passages read for other purposes tell against it. The Kulārṇava gives a ladder of seven steps, not a pair, and ranks the left above the right; the Mahānirvāṇa divides practitioners into three dispositions rather than two paths. Neither was sought as a counterexample, which is what makes them worth recording here — they came out of a search for something else."@en,
        "The modern scholarship that argues the same point — the construction of the category by nineteenth-century orientalism — has not been read and is therefore not cited at pm:evidenceFrom. Naming it unread would make this protocol look better supported than it is. See prima-materia#71."@en,
        "What this protocol does not establish. It has not searched the Indian corpus exhaustively, and absence of a case in two texts is not absence in the corpus. The state is procedure-without-cases and not cases-without-deviation, and the difference matters: the first says the search has begun, the second would say it has been carried."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A pre-colonial Indian source that presents dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them. One such passage refutes the claim that the division was imposed rather than found." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-29" ;
    pm:tests pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature .

pmp:Biqi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verschließen des Atems"@de,
        "Closing off the breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8" ;
    skos:definition "A retention counted in heartbeats: the breath is drawn in through the nose and held, then released in the smallest quantities through the mouth, the count being raised only by degrees."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmp:BrahmaviharaBhavana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Kultivierung der göttlichen Verweilungen"@de,
        "Cultivation of the divine abidings"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IX (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate raising and sustaining of a chosen affective state as a meditation subject in its own right."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:ChannelPurification a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Reinigung der Kanäle"@de,
        "Purification of the channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "A preliminary in which the channels are cleared by visualisation before the heat practice proper; the tradition's own form of the notion that a blockage is a place the energy does not reach."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:FireAndWaterRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Ritus mit Feuer und Wasser bei einer Geburt"@de,
        "The rite of fire and water at a birth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The whole work of fire and water at a birth is committed to the specialist who holds it as his office, and who among these people ordinarily bears the name and the office of physician."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is a statement about an institution: the office exists, it has a name, and the rite is not performed by the family. That is the part a later reader can check, and it is why the passage is entered as a practice rather than as an item of belief."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmp:HuacaFeast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fest der Huaca"@de,
        "The feast of the huaca"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "The senior specialist gives notice when the time comes, the chicha is brewed, and he goes through the households collecting the offerings. On the appointed day he goes with his assistants to the principal huaca and makes the offering, then to the lesser huacas and the ancestor bodies. On his return nobody sleeps that night: they sing, dance and tell tales, and children are beaten if they fall asleep. This night is called the pacaricuc, and from it the fast begins — no salt, no chilli, no sleeping with wives — lasting five days in some places and longer in others according to different traditions. Three such feasts are held in the year, tied to the Pleiades, to the first rains and to the maize harvest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:InuitMeatForkDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrsagung mit der Fleischgabel"@de,
        "Divination with the meat fork"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "A procedure used to decide whether a sick person will live: the meat fork is set up in the cooking pot, and the way it comes to rest is read as the answer."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmp:InuitReturnFailureExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob das Wiederaufleben in der berichteten Fassung überhaupt scheitern kann"@de,
        "Whether the return of the dead can fail at all as the tradition reports it"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a dead man over whom the words were spoken can come back among the living, held here to record that the one reported failure was assigned to a broken prohibition and that no condition for failing the claim itself is available."@en ;
    skos:note "No falsifying condition is given, and this is the permitted omission: the state says outright that none has been devised. Stating one here would misreport the situation, because the obstacle is not that nobody has looked but that the claim as transmitted has no failing case by construction. Whether a condition can be recovered by fixing the interval and the announcement in advance is an open question and belongs to whoever takes it up."@en,
        "The asymmetry with the protocol above is worth naming. Both claims come from the same narrator on adjacent pages; one is universal and therefore refutable by a single case, the other is hedged by a clause that absorbs every case. The difference is in the form of the claim and not in the strength of the testimony behind it."@en,
        "The passage is unusual in reporting a failure at all. What it does with the failure is the finding: the attempt is said to have miscarried because a third person had sewn new caribou skin where that is forbidden, so the practitioners and the claim are both left untouched. Success shows the power, failure shows the breach, and no outcome is left over that could count against the claim."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-31" ;
    pm:tests pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath .

pmp:InuitSecondSightExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Vorherwissens-Behauptung ihre eigene Allgemeinform übersteht"@de,
        "Whether the foreknowledge claim survives its own universal form"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a named person always knows in advance when anything remarkable is going to happen. The claim is examinable because it is universal and because the tradition itself counts the announcement, not the interpretation, as the thing that happens."@en ;
    skos:note "One confirming case is related in the same passage and no failing case is. The case does not count against the condition and does not count for it either: it was told after the event it is said to have foretold, by the wife of the man it is told about, and a dream of a friend eaten by his kin is a description that the encounter afterwards fitted rather than a prior announcement of that encounter."@en,
        "The remainder of the volume was not read in the run that opened this protocol, and it is precisely there that a failing case would be found if one is recorded at all. The state is therefore procedure-without-cases and not cases-without-deviation: the search has begun and has not been carried."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/inuit-angakkuq.ttl" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "One recorded remarkable event in the bearer's presence that he did not announce beforehand. The claim says always, so a single unannounced case refutes it as stated. A weaker form, that such announcements occur more often than chance would give, would require announcements collected before the events they name, with the failures kept alongside the hits." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-31" ;
    pm:tests pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight .

pmp:Karakia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Karakia"@de,
        "Karakia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 10-11, and the vocabulary entry karakia, p. 108" ;
    skos:definition "An address to the spirits of dead ancestors, invoked in genealogical order and closing in a petition that the invoked power give the address its effect. The witness insists that the ordinary rendering as charm is wrong and that invocation comes nearer."@en ;
    skos:note "The genealogies are said to be preserved because they form the ground-work of the invocation, so that the descent recited and the efficacy claimed are the same fact for this tradition rather than two."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:LungReading a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Lesen an der Lunge des getöteten Tieres"@de,
        "Reading the lungs of the killed animal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the calparigu" ;
    skos:definition "The office named first in the earlier report: those who see the fortune and outcome of what is asked of them. For this they killed birds, lambs and llamas, and blowing through a certain vein into the lungs found in them signs by which they said what was to happen."@en ;
    skos:note "The one procedure in this file that reads a variable natural object rather than a count. It is the Andean counterpart of the guinea-pig inspection that the later account gives under two other names, and the two together make entrail reading the best-witnessed technique of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:NavajoSacredSong a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Navajo-Kultgesang"@de,
        "Navajo sacred song"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The songs belonging to a rite, held by the priest of the rite and his assistants, containing expressions that are obsolete or without current meaning and that have therefore to be carried by memory rather than by sense."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmp:PostMortemForWitchcraftSubstance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Leichenschau auf die Hexereisubstanz"@de,
        "The post-mortem for witchcraft substance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 3-4" ;
    skos:definition "The belly of a dead man is opened and the substance looked for. A post-mortem in which none is found clears his paternal kin of the suspicion, and cases so decided are quoted afterwards by that kin in their defence."@en ;
    skos:note "A procedure inside the transmission with an outcome that could have fallen otherwise, applied to a claim about a class of persons, and reported as having been used to settle actual accusations. It is the second reason this tradition was opened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:Pranayama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Prāṇāyāma"@de,
        "Prāṇāyāma"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.49-53" ;
    skos:definition "The regulation of the breath as a distinct limb of practice, following posture and preceding the withdrawal of the senses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmp:SeparativeEndStateExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der absondernde Endzustand überhaupt geprüft wurde"@de,
        "Whether the separative end state has been examined at all"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment of the path, held here to record that no procedure for failing it has been devised."@en ;
    skos:note "No pm:falsifiedBy is given, and the omission is the permitted one: the state says outright that no procedure has been devised. Everywhere else its absence would let a claim drift from unexamined to examined-and-unsupported."@en,
        "The asymmetry is worth naming because it is easy to mistake for evidence. States of dissolution have measurable correlates and a literature; the end state claimed on the other side has neither instrument nor operationalisation. That is a fact about the instruments. Whoever infers from it that one path is better attested than the other has read the reach of the measuring apparatus as a finding about the paths. See prima-materia#73."@en,
        "This node exists to prevent a confusion, not to report a result. Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted, and that is a statement about the means of examination and not about the claim. A record that left the protocol out entirely would say the same thing by silence; a record that reported the claim as unsupported would say something else and something false."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:noProcedureDevised ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-29" ;
    pm:tests pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration .

pmp:SiguiMaterialRecord a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bewahrung des Sigui in Holz und Faser"@de,
        "Keeping the Sigui in wood and fibre"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 277-279" ;
    skos:definition "The keeping of the great mask carved for each Sigui in a rock shelter beside those of the preceding ceremonies, and of the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each Sigui in the hogon's house. Both accumulate as series, and the article treats the series as the firmer evidence that a ceremony took place at all."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one part of the account that leaves an object a later worker could count and date independently of any informant. The article reports nine masts at Ibi in 1931 with traces of three more, and eight or fourteen ferment-holders at Sanga and Arou. Held as a practice here and as a protocol at pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination in examinations/dogon-sirius.ttl."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmp:SiguiReckoning a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Auszählen des Sigui-Abstands"@de,
        "Reckoning the interval between two Sigui"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 275-277" ;
    skos:definition "The counting of the sixty-year interval by means that are stated to be mnemonic rather than astronomical: thirty biennial millet-beer drinkings, each marked with a cowry laid down by the eldest; a millet-porridge figure above the sanctuary door in which a point is added each year; and a painted line of chevrons called sigi lugu, count of the Sigui, each tooth standing for twenty years."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorders report that the rule is theoretical and that the practice departed from it: between the Sigui of the beginning of the century and 1931 only one drinking was held, and the cowries for the missing thirty years were laid down together in a single heap afterwards. The reckoning that is described as regular is thus reconstructed in arrears, and the article says so."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmp:Sitkari a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sītkārī"@de,
        "Sītkārī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.54-56 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the breath is drawn in through the mouth with the tongue between the lips, producing a hissing sound, and is not let out the same way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:Ujjayi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ujjāyī"@de,
        "Ujjāyī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.51-53 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the mouth is held closed and the breath is drawn through both nostrils so that it grazes the throat and sounds as it passes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:Whakahokitu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Whakahokitu"@de,
        "Whakahokitu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 35" ;
    skos:definition "The forms of makutu employed to turn back the working of another tohunga, that is, a counter-operation of the same kind rather than a protection of a different kind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:Whakanoa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Aufheben des Tapu"@de,
        "Making noa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 27" ;
    skos:definition "The rite by which a person or place under tapu is made common again, in the case witnessed by the eating of food cooked in an oven opened for the purpose and distributed to everyone present."@en ;
    skos:note "The rite the recorder describes as an eyewitness was already carried out with a Christian morning service inside it, and he remarks that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial. The passage is the clearest single reason the contact route of this tradition is entered as disputed rather than absent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:ZuniPrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Zuni-Festgebet"@de,
        "Zuni fixed prayer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616" ;
    skos:definition "A prayer of stereotyped phrasing, held by an appointed keeper for each kiva and taught over four nights after each planting of prayer sticks, the instructor intoning while the pupils join in by degrees and afterwards reciting it back to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The teaching method is itself a check: the pupil returns the prayer to the keeper, so that a divergence is caught before it is carried into the ceremony. Because the phrasing is stereotyped the witness names the sequence, and not the wording, as the hard part of learning a long prayer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmt:Ajivika a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ājīvika-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ājīvika transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A lost renunciant transmission teaching a fixed destiny through which every being passes, known only through the reports of its opponents."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as the limiting case: a tradition with no surviving corpus of its own. Whatever it yields will be an ascription by hostile witnesses and must be recorded as one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted corpus; the doctrine is reported in the Buddhist and Jain canons, chiefly Sāmaññaphala Sutta and the Bhagavatī Sūtra" .

pmt:Alvar a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ālvār-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ālvār transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tamil devotional transmission of the twelve Ālvār, in which the relation to the deity is sung in the first person and the states it passes through are named."@en ;
    skos:note "The earliest of the devotional corpora and the one on which the later theory of devotional states rests. Worth registering ahead of the others for the same reason the Gauḍīya file was opened: the states are named, not only described."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Nālāyira Divya Prabandham, in particular the Tiruvāymoḻi of Nammāḻvār" .

pmt:Amazonian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Amazonische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Amazonian transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lowland South American transmission of songs used to direct the effect of a plant preparation, and of a body that can be exchanged for another."@en ;
    skos:note "No corpus in its own language and no writing before contact. What exists is transcribed song and ethnographic report, which this project cannot yet admit as a source. Registered so that the exclusion is visible rather than silent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted written corpus; the published icaro transcriptions and the ethnographic record stand in its place" .

pmt:AnalyticalPsychology a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Analytische Psychologie"@de,
        "Analytical psychology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Jung's transmission of an objective psyche and of active imagination as a procedure for engaging figures encountered in it, with a record kept of the engagement."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as a tradition and not as science on the rule stated at the head of this file: its central claims rest on testimony, including the author's own record of his experiment, and not on procedures that could have come out otherwise. Where it does produce evidence, that evidence enters at an examination node like any other."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Jung, Liber Novus; Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos; Jung, Mysterium Coniunctionis" .

pmt:Anthroposophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Anthroposophie"@de,
        "Anthroposophy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Steiner's transmission of a schooling of perception said to open supersensible cognition, presented as a discipline with stated stages and moral prerequisites."@en ;
    skos:note "The prerequisites are stated as conditions without which the exercises are held to be harmful, which makes them a prerequisite chain of the strong kind rather than an exhortation."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten; Steiner, Die Geheimwissenschaft im Umriss" .

pmt:ArabicAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Arabische Alchemie"@de,
        "Arabic alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the balance, of elixirs and of the generation of life by art, carried under the name of Jābir by a corpus larger than one man."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Jābirian corpus; al-Rāzī, Sirr al-Asrār" .

pmt:ArsNotoria a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ars Notoria"@de,
        "Ars Notoria"@en ;
    skos:definition "The medieval transmission of prayers and figures held to confer the liberal arts and a retentive memory upon a practitioner who keeps a stated schedule."@en ;
    skos:note "One of the few pre-modern western claims formulated so that it could fail: a named capacity, a named period, a named regimen."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ars Notoria in the medieval recensions; Liber Juratus Honorii" .

pmt:AshtarTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ashtar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Ashtar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of messages ascribed to a command or council of non-terrestrial beings charged with the oversight of the earth, received after the first by many transmitters who do not share a lineage."@en ;
    skos:note "The motif of a federation or confederation of worlds enters general circulation here and is afterwards found in corpora that never name Ashtar. That spread is a reception history and belongs in the graph as one, before anybody reads it as agreement between independent witnesses."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Van Tassel, the Ministry of Universal Wisdom publications; the published Ashtar message collections" .

pmt:Atharvavedic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Atharvavedische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Atharvavedic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative Vedic corpus: spells for healing, protection, harm, love and kingship, with the ritual manual that tells how each is employed."@en ;
    skos:note "The oldest Indian material the project would hold that is a working corpus rather than a doctrine."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā (Śaunaka recension); Kauśikasūtra" .

pmt:AustralianDreaming a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Australische Aboriginal-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Australian Aboriginal transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Australian transmission of an ancestral order laid into country, held in song and in the routes the song follows."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered and deliberately left at corpus named. There is no corpus in the project's sense, much of the material is restricted by rules that are still enforced, and a public-domain dedication of restricted knowledge would be a taking and not an opening. The gate here protects ownership, not authority, and the project's criterion does not apply to it unchanged."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "No transmitted written corpus; the ethnographic record stands in its place and much of it is restricted" .

pmt:Ayurvedic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Āyurvedische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Āyurvedic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Indian medical transmission, including the rasāyana section that claims restoration of vigour and extension of life by regimen."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Caraka Saṃhitā; Suśruta Saṃhitā; Vāgbhaṭa, Aṣṭāṅgahṛdaya" .

pmt:BalineseTantra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Balinesische Lontar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Balinese lontar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Balinese palm-leaf transmission of healing, protective diagrams and the composition of the person, at the far edge of the Indian sphere of influence."@en ;
    skos:note "Written, catalogued and barely used by comparative work. The contact question is genuinely open: how much is Indian import and how much is local is undecided in the literature."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Usada healing lontar; Kanda Pat; the Aji Saraswati texts" .

pmt:BalticSlavicCharm a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Baltische und slawische Besprechungstradition"@de,
        "Baltic and Slavic charm transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eastern European transmission of spoken charms for healing, protection and binding, fixed in writing only by nineteenth-century collectors."@en ;
    skos:note "The late fixation is the whole difficulty: the corpus is a collection and not a work, and the project has no attestation mode for that yet."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published zagovory collections; the Latvian dainas collections" .

pmt:BasharTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Bashar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Bashar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission received by Darryl Anka since 1984, teaching that acting on the strongest available excitement is the means by which one comes to occupy a different reality."@en ;
    skos:note "Unusually operative for a channelled corpus: the teaching is given as steps with stated conditions of failure, in front of audiences, on record."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change; the recorded session transcripts" .

pmt:Bon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Bön"@de,
        "Bön"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tibetan transmission that claims descent from Zhangzhung rather than from India, with its own dzogchen cycles and its own founder."@en ;
    skos:note "The independence claim is the point. If it holds, Bön is a witness beside Tibetan Buddhism rather than a variant of it; if it fails, the failure is one of the better documented cases of a tradition constructing its own antiquity. Either outcome is a finding."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud; the Bön dzogchen cycles in the published Bön canon" .

pmt:BoranKammatthana a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Borān kammaṭṭhāna"@de,
        "Borān kammaṭṭhāna"@en ;
    skos:definition "The esoteric Theravāda transmission of Southeast Asia, working with syllables placed in the body and a nimitta led along an interior path."@en ;
    skos:note "Largely displaced by the modern reform movements and therefore poorly represented in every general account of Buddhist meditation."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Yogāvacara manuals of Sri Lanka and Siam in the published editions" .

pmt:BurmeseWeikza a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Burmesische Weikza-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Burmese weikza transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Burmese transmission of an accomplished practitioner who prolongs life by alchemy, diagram and mantra in order to await a future buddha."@en ;
    skos:note "A living tradition whose claims are made about named contemporaries, which is rare and worth more to an examination than any ancient ascription."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published weikza and dhat-lonn literature in Burmese" .

pmt:Bwiti a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Bwiti"@de,
        "Bwiti"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Gabonese transmission of an initiation carried by a root preparation, in which the initiate is expected to meet the dead and to report the meeting."@en ;
    skos:note "The expected content of the experience is stated in advance, which makes the tradition unusually well suited to an examination of what such expectation determines."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa" .

pmt:Candomble a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Candomblé"@de,
        "Candomblé"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Brazilian transmission of the orixás, with an initiation that seats a spirit in the head of the initiate."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Bastide, Le candomblé de Bahia; Verger, Notes sur le culte des Orisa et Vodun" .

pmt:CastanedaToltec a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Castanedas toltekische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Castaneda's Toltec transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of a body of practice concerning attention, dreaming and the assemblage point, presented as the teaching of a Yaqui man of knowledge."@en ;
    skos:note "The fieldwork is not credible and the informant is not attestable, and the practices are nonetheless transmitted today by people who follow them. Both facts belong in the graph, which is why the tradition is registered rather than dismissed."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Castaneda, The Teachings of Don Juan; Castaneda, The Art of Dreaming" .

pmt:CayceReadings a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Die Cayce-Readings"@de,
        "The Cayce readings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of several thousand readings given in a sleep-like state, most of them addressed to a named person about a stated condition, and taken down stenographically."@en ;
    skos:note "The one corpus in this block built for examination without intending to be: individual readings, individual recipients, dated, indexed, and with outcomes that were sometimes followed up. Whatever it yields, it yields countably."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The indexed Cayce readings held by the Association for Research and Enlightenment; Sugrue, There Is a River" .

pmt:CelestialMasters a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Himmelsmeister"@de,
        "Celestial Masters"@en ;
    skos:definition "The earliest organised Daoist transmission, in which illness is treated as the consequence of fault and healing proceeds by confession and petition."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Xiang'er commentary on the Laozi; Zhengyi fawen; the Taiping jing" .

pmt:ChaldeanOracles a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chaldäische Orakel"@de,
        "Chaldean Oracles"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fragmentary hexameters that the later Platonists treated as revealed, and on which their theurgic practice was built."@en ;
    skos:note "The graph already holds the theurgy that rests on this text without holding the text."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Chaldean Oracles, transmitted in fragments through Proclus, Damascius and Psellos" .

pmt:Chan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chan"@de,
        "Chan"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission holding that awakening is sudden and that the mind is not to be polished but recognised."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Platform Sūtra; Linji lu; Biyan lu" .

pmt:ChaosMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chaosmagie"@de,
        "Chaos magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The late twentieth century transmission treating belief as a tool to be adopted and discarded, and holding that the symbolic system employed is arbitrary if the technique is sound."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that content is interchangeable and only technique carries effect is directly examinable, and if it held it would reorganise every convergence question the project asks."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Spare, The Book of Pleasure; Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut" .

pmt:ChineseDivination a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinesische Divinations- und Siedlungslehre"@de,
        "Chinese divinatory and siting transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of change-based divination, of fate calculation from birth data and of the siting of dwellings and graves."@en ;
    skos:note "Claims here attach to datable outcomes, which makes them among the few in the corpus that could be falsified without a laboratory."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhouyi with the Xici commentary; Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing; Guo Pu, Zangshu" .

pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinesischer medizinischer Kanon"@de,
        "Chinese medical canon"@en ;
    skos:definition "The canonical Chinese account of qi, the channels and the spirits resident in the organs, framed as a clinical rather than a soteriological doctrine."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Huangdi neijing (Suwen and Lingshu); Nanjing" .

pmt:ChristianTheosophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Christliche Theosophie"@de,
        "Christian theosophy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Böhme's transmission of a divine self-disclosure through opposition, reported as having been seen rather than reasoned."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Böhme, Aurora; Böhme, De signatura rerum" .

pmt:ContacteeTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Contactee-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Contactee transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mid twentieth century transmission of teachings ascribed to visitors from other worlds, reported first as bodily meetings and later as messages received without one."@en ;
    skos:note "The shift from a claimed physical encounter to a claimed interior reception happens inside this literature and is datable. It is the hinge between the older revealed corpora and the later channelled ones."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Adamski, Inside the Space Ships; Menger, From Outer Space to You; van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer" .

pmt:CopticMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Koptische Zaubertradition"@de,
        "Coptic magical transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "Christian Egyptian operative texts in which biblical names, angelic invocation and older Egyptian procedure stand side by side without apology."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Coptic magical papyri and ostraca in the standard collections; the Testament of Solomon as its adjacent Greek witness" .

pmt:CourseInMiracles a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Kurs in Wundern"@de,
        "A Course in Miracles"@en ;
    skos:definition "A scribed corpus in three parts, teaching that the perceived world is a projection of guilt, and carrying a workbook of three hundred and sixty-five daily exercises in fixed order."@en ;
    skos:note "The workbook is a stated curriculum with an order and a duration, which is rare in this block and puts the corpus closer to the manual traditions than to the revealed cosmologies."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "A Course in Miracles: Text, Workbook for Students, Manual for Teachers" .

pmt:Druze a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Drusische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Druze transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A closed transmission teaching the transfer of the soul into a new body at death, with an internal literature restricted to the initiated."@en ;
    skos:note "A case where the gate the project exists to remove is still shut and still enforced. Registration says so rather than pretending the corpus is available."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rasāʾil al-Ḥikma" .

pmt:Dzogchen a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Dzogchen"@de,
        "Dzogchen"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tibetan transmission of a primordially free awareness, with a practice whose stages are counted by visions appearing under stated conditions of posture, light and darkness."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Nyingthig cycles; Longchenpa, Seven Treasuries" .

pmt:EgyptianRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pharaonisch-ägyptisches Ritual"@de,
        "Pharaonic Egyptian ritual"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of heka as an operative power held by gods, kings and the transfigured dead, carried in mortuary and temple literature."@en ;
    skos:note "The transformation spells are a capacity list in the strict sense: each states what the speaker becomes and what that state permits."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Pyramid Texts; Coffin Texts; Book of the Dead; Papyrus Westcar; the demotic magical papyrus of London and Leiden" .

pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Äthiopisch-orthodoxe Zaubertradition"@de,
        "Ethiopian Orthodox magical transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Geʿez transmission of protective scrolls, of names held to bind, and of a canon that preserves works lost everywhere else."@en ;
    skos:note "African, literate, canonical, and already present in the graph at one remove: the fullest witness of the Enochic book the graph holds is the Ethiopian one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Lefāfa Ṣedeq; the asmāt scroll corpus; the Geʿez canon" .

pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Europäische Volksmagie"@de,
        "European folk magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The vernacular transmission of blessings, charms and cheap printed handbooks for healing, protection and the recovery of stolen goods."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Merseburg charms; Lacnunga; Bald's Leechbook; Romanusbüchlein; Sechstes und Siebtes Buch Mosis; Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund" .

pmt:FinnicIncantation a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Finnische Zaubersprüche"@de,
        "Finnic incantation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Finnish and Karelian transmission of the tietäjä, in which power over a thing follows from knowing and reciting its origin."@en ;
    skos:note "Kalevala is a nineteenth-century compilation and belongs in the record as an ordering act, not as the corpus. The corpus is the collected incantations."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot; the loitsurunot collections" .

pmt:FourthWay a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Der Vierte Weg"@de,
        "The Fourth Way"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Gurdjieff's teaching: a claim that ordinary waking is a form of sleep, and a work on attention conducted in the middle of ordinary life."@en ;
    skos:note "The graph already holds the Fourth Way anthropology as an origination, because material from it circulates under ancient ascription. Registering the transmission does not touch that finding: the tradition transmits, and the pedigree remains unsupported."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous; Gurdjieff, Beelzebub's Tales to His Grandson" .

pmt:GoldenDawn a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"@de,
        "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"@en ;
    skos:definition "The late Victorian transmission that welded kabbalistic, grimoire and Egyptian material into a graded initiatory curriculum with examinations."@en ;
    skos:note "The syncretism is documented in its own papers, which makes it the best available record of how a modern system is assembled from older parts and then presented as one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The cipher manuscripts and knowledge lectures; Regardie, The Golden Dawn" .

pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Griechisch-ägyptische Alchemie"@de,
        "Graeco-Egyptian alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The earliest alchemical transmission, in which the operation on matter is reported together with dreams that interpret it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zosimos of Panopolis, the Visions and the authentic memoirs" .

pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Griechische und demotische Zauberpapyri"@de,
        "Greek and demotic magical papyri"@en ;
    skos:definition "The working handbooks of late antique Egypt: procedures with materials, hours, purity requirements, spoken formulae and stated signs of success."@en ;
    skos:note "The densest procedural corpus available to the project, and the one most likely to carry falsifying conditions, because a handbook has to tell its user when the working has failed."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Papyri Graecae Magicae and Papyri Demoticae Magicae, including the Mithras Liturgy at PGM IV.475-829" .

pmt:HaitianVodou a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Haitianisches Vodou"@de,
        "Haitian Vodou"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Haitian transmission of spirits who arrive in a named person and speak, with a liturgy that assigns each an appearance, a rhythm and a manner."@en ;
    skos:note "Deren's account is a first-person report of being mounted by a spirit written by the observer herself, which is a rare form: participant testimony with the observation intact."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Métraux, Le vaudou haïtien; Deren, Divine Horsemen" .

pmt:Hasidism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chassidismus"@de,
        "Hasidism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The eastern European transmission of devekut as a continuous attachment attainable within ordinary life, and of contemplation as its means."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Toledot Yaakov Yosef; Keter Shem Tov; Shneur Zalman, Tanya" .

pmt:HawaiianCosmogony a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hawaiische Kosmogonie"@de,
        "Hawaiian cosmogonic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Hawaiian transmission of a chanted genealogy of the world, recited as a continuous descent from darkness to the living."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Kumulipo in the Hawaiian text with Beckwith's edition" .

pmt:Hekhalot a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hekhalot- und Merkava-Literatur"@de,
        "Hekhalot and Merkavah literature"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Jewish transmission of an ascent through palaces guarded by gatekeepers, entered by seals and passwords, and of an adjuration by which knowledge is obtained without study."@en ;
    skos:note "The Sar ha-Torah material states a learning capacity plainly enough to be tested: knowledge of Torah acquired by ritual rather than by study, in a stated time."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hekhalot Rabbati; Hekhalot Zutarti; Sar ha-Torah; Šiʿur Qomah; 3 Enoch" .

pmt:Hermetic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hermetische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Hermetic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Graeco-Egyptian transmission of an ascent through the spheres in which the ascending one sheds a vice at each, and of a rebirth reported as having happened to the speaker."@en ;
    skos:note "The Discourse on the Eighth and the Ninth is an initiation with a sequence, a moment of change and a demand that the initiate confirm what he now sees. It has no equivalent in what the graph currently holds."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Corpus Hermeticum; Asclepius; the Stobaean excerpts including Korē Kosmou; Nag Hammadi VI,6" .

pmt:HittiteRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hethitisch-anatolisches Ritual"@de,
        "Hittite and Anatolian ritual"@en ;
    skos:definition "Anatolian purification and substitution rituals transmitted under the names of the practitioners who dictated them, most of them women."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The CTH ritual corpus, including the rituals of Tunnawiya, Maštigga and Ambazzi" .

pmt:Hoodoo a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hoodoo"@de,
        "Hoodoo"@en ;
    skos:definition "The African American transmission of workings for protection, luck, love and revenge, carried by practitioners without a priesthood or a central corpus."@en ;
    skos:note "The Hyatt collection is thousands of practitioner statements taken down verbatim with the speakers identified only by place. As a body of first-person practice testimony it has no equal in the project's reach."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hyatt, Hoodoo, Conjuration, Witchcraft, Rootwork, five volumes" .

pmt:Huna a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Huna"@de,
        "Huna"@en ;
    skos:definition "An early twentieth century system of three selves and directed vital force, presented as the secret teaching of Hawaiian priests."@en ;
    skos:note "Constructed by an outsider from theosophical and New Thought material and rejected by Hawaiian scholars as a representation of anything Hawaiian. Registered as the transmission it actually is, so that the ascription can be entered as false without the material vanishing from the record."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles" .

pmt:Illuminationism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Illuminationismus"@de,
        "Illuminationism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Suhrawardī's transmission of knowledge by presence and of an intermediate world of images held to be neither material nor mental but a place with its own standing."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that the imaginal world is a place and not a faculty is an ontological commitment the graph will have to sort against every tradition that treats vision as internal."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Suhrawardī, Ḥikmat al-Ishrāq; Mullā Ṣadrā, al-Asfār al-Arbaʿa" .

pmt:InnerAsianTengrism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Innerasiatische Tengri-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Inner Asian sky transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Turkic and Mongol transmission of a sky power that confers mandate and fortune, and of practitioners who address it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Secret History of the Mongols; the Orkhon inscriptions; the published Manas cycle" .

pmt:InsularCeltic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Inselkeltische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Insular Celtic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Irish and Welsh transmission of poetic knowledge as an acquired faculty, with named divinatory procedures and the ecclesiastical prohibitions attached to them."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibitions are in the same manuscripts as the procedures, so procedure and counter-testimony arrive together."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The legal and glossarial material on imbas forosnai, teinm laída and díchetal do chennaib; the immrama; the Book of Taliesin" .

pmt:IslamicLetterMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Islamische Buchstaben- und Namensmagie"@de,
        "Islamic letter and name magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative transmission of the science of letters and of the divine names, with squares, talismans and hours of working."@en ;
    skos:note "The ascription of the best known handbook to al-Būnī is contested, and the corpus grew after him under his name. The dispute is the first node this tradition should carry."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Šams al-maʿārif corpus ascribed to al-Būnī; Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm" .

pmt:IsmailiCosmology a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ismailitische Kosmologie"@de,
        "Ismaili cosmology"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Shia transmission of a graded emanation and of an inner meaning disclosed by initiation, together with the encyclopaedic Neoplatonism of the Brethren of Purity."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sijistānī, Kashf al-Maḥjūb; Nāṣir-i Khusraw, Jāmiʿ al-Ḥikmatayn; Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ" .

pmt:Jain a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jainistische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Jain transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Jain transmission of restraint, austerity and the shedding of karmic matter, with its own catalogue of powers said to arise along the way."@en ;
    skos:note "A second capacity list formulated in the same region and period as the yogic one and not derived from it. Within the Indian sphere this is the closest thing to a control case."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ācārāṅga Sūtra; Umāsvāti, Tattvārthasūtra; Hemacandra, Yogaśāstra" .

pmt:JapaneseZen a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Japanischer Zen"@de,
        "Japanese Zen"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of seated practice as enactment rather than means, together with a literature on the illnesses that practice itself produces."@en ;
    skos:note "Hakuin's account of meditation sickness is a self-warning of the strongest kind the project can hold: a text prescribing a practice and describing its casualties, with a remedy."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Dōgen, Shōbōgenzō; Hakuin, Yasenkanna; Hakuin, Orategama" .

pmt:JewishPracticalMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jüdische Praxismagie"@de,
        "Jewish practical magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The operative Jewish transmission of adjurations, amulets and bowls, distinct from the speculative literature and older than it in its written witnesses."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer ha-Razim; Ḥarba de-Moshe; the Cairo Genizah magical fragments; the Aramaic incantation bowls" .

pmt:Kabbalah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Kabbala"@de,
        "Kabbalah"@en ;
    skos:definition "The medieval and early modern Jewish transmission of the sefirot as the structure of emanation, and of the human act as bearing on it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer ha-Bahir; Zohar; Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim; Vital, Ez Ḥayyim and Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim" .

pmt:Kalacakra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Kālacakra"@de,
        "Kālacakra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tantric transmission of a sixfold yoga whose completion is marked by a sequence of signs appearing in a darkened retreat."@en ;
    skos:note "The sign sequence is stated in fixed order and is therefore comparable with the Dzogchen visions and with the phosphene literature, which is a convergence question the graph cannot currently pose."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Vimalaprabhā; Sekoddeśa and its commentary" .

pmt:KeylonticScience a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Keylontic Science und die MCEO-Lehren"@de,
        "Keylontic Science and the MCEO teachings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of a detailed geometry of subtle anatomy together with a claimed ancient lineage of guardianship, delivered through a living transmitter and taught in graded courses with their own technical vocabulary."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims to keep apart. That it transmits is a fact: texts, a transmitter, courses, students, successors, and it is registered on that ground alone. That it descends from an ancient priesthood is an ascription with a modern author and a modern date, attested nowhere outside the corpus, and it enters as a pm:Originating node when it enters. The material is under active copyright, so nothing is reproduced; the definitions here are the compiler's own paraphrase, as everywhere in this graph."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ashayana Deane, Voyagers I and Voyagers II; the published MCEO Freedom Teachings course materials" .

pmt:KongoNkisi a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Kongolesische Nkisi-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Kongo nkisi transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Kongo transmission of composed objects charged to act, and of the land of the dead as a place with a stated geography."@en ;
    skos:note "The Laman notebooks were written in Kikongo by Kongo authors describing their own practice, which makes them one of the few sources in this file that is not an outsider's account."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Laman notebooks in Kikongo; Laman, The Kongo" .

pmt:KoreanMuism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Koreanischer Musok"@de,
        "Korean muism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Korean transmission of the mudang, whose office is entered through a sickness resolved only by accepting it, and whose ritual is a negotiation carried out in the voice of the dead."@en ;
    skos:note "Chiefly oral. The corpus is a collection of transcribed ritual songs, which the project has no attestation mode for yet."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The published muga song transcriptions; Akiba and Akamatsu's collected materials" .

pmt:LakotaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lakota-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Lakota ritual transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Lakota transmission of the seven rites, of the vision sought under fasting, and of the pipe as the instrument of address."@en ;
    skos:note "The best known account passed through two editors, and the difference between what was said and what was printed is itself documented. The tradition should enter the graph together with that dispute, not before it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Brown, The Sacred Pipe; Neihardt, Black Elk Speaks; DeMallie, The Sixth Grandfather, as the record of the mediation" .

pmt:LatinAlchemy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lateinische Alchemie"@de,
        "Latin alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The western alchemical transmission, in which the stages of the work are described at once as operations and as states of the operator."@en ;
    skos:note "The double reading is not a modern imposition and not a settled fact either. It is a dispute the corpus carries in itself and the graph should record as one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Turba philosophorum; Rosarium philosophorum; Aurora consurgens" .

pmt:LatinMysticism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lateinische Mystik"@de,
        "Latin mystical transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The western Christian transmission of graded interior states, with criteria for telling one state from another and warnings against taking consolation for progress."@en ;
    skos:note "Teresa's dwellings and John of the Cross's nights are the western material closest to a stage map with failure modes, and the failure modes are stated more plainly than the attainments."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Richard of St Victor, Benjamin Major; Meister Eckhart, German sermons; The Cloud of Unknowing; Teresa of Ávila, Las Moradas; John of the Cross, Subida del Monte Carmelo and Noche oscura" .

pmt:LucumiSanteria a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Lucumí"@de,
        "Lucumí"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Cuban transmission of the orichas, carrying the Yoruba divinatory corpus in a shortened form alongside Catholic imagery."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to Ifá is a documented case of a corpus surviving a forced transfer with measurable loss, which is examinable rather than merely regrettable."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Cabrera, El Monte; the published libretas" .

pmt:Mandaean a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mandäische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Mandaean transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A living gnostic transmission with its own scripture and priesthood, teaching an ascent of the soul past stations of examination."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ginzā Rabbā; Drāšā d-Yahia; Qolastā" .

pmt:Manichaean a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Manichäische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Manichaean transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded on Mani's own account of a companion who appeared to him and disclosed what he taught."@en ;
    skos:note "The Cologne Mani Codex carries a first-person account of the syzygos, which is rare for late antiquity and rarer still with a name attached."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kephalaia; Cologne Mani Codex; the Coptic psalms and the Turfan fragments" .

pmt:MayaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Maya-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Maya ritual transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Maya transmission of creation, of the calendar as a moving structure of obligation, and of divination by counting seeds."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Popol Vuh in the Ximénez manuscript; the Books of Chilam Balam; the Dresden Codex; the Ritual of the Bacabs" .

pmt:Mesmerism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mesmerismus"@de,
        "Mesmerism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of an animal magnetism held to pass between bodies and to be directed by passes of the hand, together with the induced states its practitioners described."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as a tradition because it transmits by testimony and lineage. Its examination by the royal commission is evidence and belongs at an examination node, not here. The pair is the cleanest illustration the project has of the two predicates."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mesmer, Mémoire sur la découverte du magnétisme animal; Puységur, Mémoires pour servir à l'histoire du magnétisme animal" .

pmt:MesopotamianExorcism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mesopotamische Beschwörungskunst"@de,
        "Mesopotamian exorcistic craft"@en ;
    skos:definition "The scribal craft of the āšipu: a trained office with its own curriculum, treating affliction as an assault to be countered by named procedure."@en ;
    skos:note "The oldest procedural corpus in writing anywhere, and older than every tradition the graph currently holds. Whatever it shares with the Greek and Jewish material is therefore a question of derivation running one way only."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Maqlû; Šurpu; the exorcist's manual KAR 44; Ludlul bēl nēmeqi; Enūma Anu Enlil" .

pmt:ModernVipassana a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Moderne Vipassanā-Bewegung"@de,
        "Modern vipassanā movement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The twentieth century Burmese reform transmission of insight practice as a lay technique detachable from monastic life, taught in fixed courses."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered separately from the canon and the commentaries because it is a distinct transmission with its own founders and manuals, and because its relation to the older material, in particular its treatment of absorption, is contested inside the tradition itself."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Manual of Insight; Ledi Sayādaw, the Manuals of Buddhism" .

pmt:ModernWicca a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Moderne Wicca"@de,
        "Modern Wicca"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mid twentieth century transmission of an initiatory craft with a coven structure, a seasonal calendar and an inherited book copied by hand."@en ;
    skos:note "Presented at its founding as the survival of an old religion. The construction is documented in detail, so the tradition and the refutation of its pedigree can be held together without either being lost."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Gardner, Witchcraft Today; the Book of Shadows in the published recensions" .

pmt:Nath a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Nāth-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Nāth transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lineage of Gorakhnāth, teaching a body perfected by practice and a state in which mind is said to be absent rather than concentrated."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Siddha-Siddhānta-Paddhati; Gorakṣaśataka; Amanaska" .

pmt:Neidan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neidan, innere Alchemie"@de,
        "Neidan internal alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission that relocates the alchemical work into the body, with a staged refinement of essence, breath and spirit and an embryo said to form and depart."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian; Zhong-Lü chuandao ji; Xingming guizhi" .

pmt:NeoConfucianCultivation a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neokonfuzianische Selbstkultivierung"@de,
        "Neo-Confucian self-cultivation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of quiet sitting and of the investigation of things as a discipline of attention that claims no transcendent object."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered as a control condition. A contemplative practice with the same postures and much of the same phenomenology, and without the metaphysical claim, is what any examination of the others needs to have alongside them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhu Xi, Zhuzi yulei on jingzuo; Wang Yangming, Chuanxilu; Mencius 2A.2 on the flood-like qi" .

pmt:Neoshamanism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neoschamanismus"@de,
        "Neoshamanism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The modern transmission of a journey undertaken to a drumbeat and taught in workshops, presented as a technique abstracted from many indigenous traditions at once."@en ;
    skos:note "The abstraction claim is the point of interest: it asserts that a core method survives the removal of every particular tradition. The graph can hold that assertion beside the traditions it claims to abstract from, which is exactly what it was built to do."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Harner, The Way of the Shaman" .

pmt:NewThought a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "New Thought"@de,
        "New Thought"@en ;
    skos:definition "The American transmission holding that mental states determine bodily and material conditions, and that the determination can be exercised deliberately."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is stated in a form that invites counting, and it is the ancestor of most of what circulates today as manifestation."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Quimby, The Quimby Manuscripts; Emma Curtis Hopkins, Scientific Christian Mental Practice; Troward, The Edinburgh Lectures on Mental Science" .

pmt:NorseSeidr a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Altnordische Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Old Norse ritual transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Norse transmission of runic and sung operations and of a divinatory practice performed from a raised seat with a stated arrangement of singers and questioners."@en ;
    skos:note "Hávamál names eighteen effects in list form, which is a capacity catalogue in the same shape as the siddhi lists. Written down by Christians centuries after the practice, so the contact question is a question about the recording hand."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hávamál; Grímnismál; Snorri, Ynglinga saga; Eiríks saga rauða" .

pmt:Onmyodo a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Onmyōdō"@de,
        "Onmyōdō"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese office of yin-yang divination and calendrical prohibition, practised by a licensed bureau of the state."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Hoki naiden; the onmyō provisions of the Engishiki" .

pmt:PaloMonte a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Palo Monte"@de,
        "Palo Monte"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Cuban transmission of Kongo derivation, working through a charged vessel and a pact with a named spirit of the dead."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Cabrera, Reglas de Congo" .

pmt:Pancaratra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pāñcarātra"@de,
        "Pāñcarātra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Vaiṣṇava tantric transmission, in which the practitioner's body is dissolved and rebuilt by placement before any worship may proceed."@en ;
    skos:note "The body-construction sequence is a prerequisite chain of unusual explicitness and belongs in the graph for that reason before any doctrine of the school does."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Jayākhya Saṃhitā; Lakṣmī Tantra; Sātvata Saṃhitā" .

pmt:PropheticKabbalah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Prophetische Kabbala"@de,
        "Prophetic Kabbalah"@en ;
    skos:definition "Abulafia's transmission of letter permutation, breath and head movement as a technique for reaching a state he calls prophecy."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered apart from Kabbalah because it is a manual and not a doctrine. On the level of stated procedure it is the closest western counterpart to the Yogasūtra that exists, including its warnings about what befalls the unprepared."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Abulafia, Or ha-Sekhel; Abulafia, Ḥayyei ha-Olam ha-Ba" .

pmt:RaMaterial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ra-Material"@de,
        "The Ra material"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of one hundred and six sessions received between 1981 and 1984 by a group of three working in fixed roles, teaching a cosmology of densities, a choice between service to others and service to self, and a harvest at the close of a cycle."@en ;
    skos:note "The best documented reception in the block. Questioner, instrument and scribe are named, the sessions are numbered and dated, the working conditions are described including the instrument's physical decline, and the transmission continued under a different contact after this one ended. The cosmological claims are largely undecidable; the claims about the conditions of contact and about what the work cost the instrument are not, and that is where an examination would begin."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Elkins, Rueckert and McCarty, The Law of One, Books I to V; the L/L Research session transcripts" .

pmt:Raelism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Raëlismus"@de,
        "Raëlism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded on Claude Vorilhon's reported meeting of 1973, holding that humanity was made by a non-terrestrial people and reading scripture as the record of that making."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered because it performs the characteristic move of the block in its clearest form: it rereads received texts as accounts of contact. That reinterpretation is a claim about the older corpora and belongs in the graph beside them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Vorilhon, Le livre qui dit la vérité; Vorilhon, Les extra-terrestres m'ont emmené sur leur planète" .

pmt:Rasashastra a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Rasaśāstra"@de,
        "Rasaśāstra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Indian alchemical transmission of mercurial preparations held to make the body proof against decay, with named substances, processes and durations."@en ;
    skos:note "Among the most testable claims available anywhere in the corpus, and testable in two directions at once: the promised outcome and the toxicity of the means."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rasārṇava; Govindabhagavatpāda, Rasahṛdayatantra; Vāgbhaṭa, Rasaratnasamuccaya" .

pmt:Reiki a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Reiki"@de,
        "Reiki"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of a healing energy conveyed by the hands, transmitted through an attunement conferred by a teacher in a stated lineage."@en ;
    skos:note "Attunement is a transmission claim in the strict sense: the capacity is said to pass from person to person by a discrete act. That is unusually well suited to examination, because the tradition itself distinguishes those who have received it from those who have not."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Usui Reiki Ryōhō Hikkei; Hayashi, Reiki Ryōhō Shishin" .

pmt:RenaissanceMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Gelehrte Magie der Renaissance"@de,
        "Renaissance learned magic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The early modern attempt to give magic a philosophy: sympathies, images, memory as an operative art, and angelic communication kept as a record."@en ;
    skos:note "Dee's diaries are a working log with dates, which makes them a different kind of witness from the treatises around them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ficino, De vita libri tres; Agrippa, De occulta philosophia; Bruno, De umbris idearum; Dee, Monas Hieroglyphica and the spiritual diaries" .

pmt:Rosicrucian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Rosenkreuzerische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Rosicrucian transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission begun by three anonymous manifestos that announced an order and a knowledge, and that produced both by being read."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition whose founding documents describe a body that did not exist before them. Of interest to the project precisely as a documented case of a transmission created by its own announcement."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Fama Fraternitatis; Confessio Fraternitatis; Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz" .

pmt:Samkhya a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sāṃkhya"@de,
        "Sāṃkhya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The enumerative transmission of the evolutes of nature and of a witnessing principle distinct from them, on which the yoga of the graph already rests."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Īśvarakṛṣṇa, Sāṃkhyakārikā; Yuktidīpikā" .

pmt:SantNada a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sant- und Nāda-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Sant and inner-sound transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The north Indian transmission of an inner sound held to be audible under instruction, and of union without image or rite."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Kabīr granthāvalī; Ādi Granth; Nāda-bindu Upaniṣad" .

pmt:SethMaterial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Das Seth-Material"@de,
        "The Seth material"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission received by Jane Roberts from 1963, teaching that reality is constructed by belief and that the constructing can be observed and altered by the one who does it."@en ;
    skos:note "States its central claim in a form that asks for a procedure: beliefs are to be examined and changed, and the change is said to show. The sessions were numbered and dated from the start."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Roberts, Seth Speaks; Roberts, The Nature of Personal Reality; The Early Sessions" .

pmt:Shangqing a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shangqing"@de,
        "Shangqing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Daoist transmission of visualised journeys, of deities resident in the body, and of nocturnal encounters recorded as they were received."@en ;
    skos:note "The Zhen'gao is a dated record of revelations with the recipient named, which places it among the few pre-modern first-person corpora available anywhere."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao; Huangting neijing jing; Dadong zhenjing" .

pmt:Shingon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shingon"@de,
        "Shingon"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese esoteric transmission claiming buddhahood in the present body, achieved through the coordination of gesture, speech and visualisation."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kūkai, Sokushin jōbutsu gi; Kūkai, Hizō hōyaku" .

pmt:Shinto a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shintō"@de,
        "Shintō"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of kami, of purification as the central operation, and of formal address preserved in the imperial liturgies."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kojiki; Nihon shoki; the norito of the Engishiki" .

pmt:Shugendo a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Shugendō"@de,
        "Shugendō"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Japanese transmission of mountain austerity, in which power is held to follow from ordeals performed at named places in a fixed order."@en ;
    skos:note "The self-mummification practice gives the project something almost nothing else does: a stated procedure with a documented outcome and documented failures, both counted."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Shozan engi; the Shugendō shūyō himitsu shū" .

pmt:SiberianShamanism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sibirische Schamanenüberlieferung"@de,
        "Siberian shamanic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tungus and Sakha transmission of an office entered through an illness that resolves only on acceptance, with a costume, a drum and a stated itinerary."@en ;
    skos:note "The region the word shaman was taken from. The graph uses the category and holds no witness from its source."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus; the published Olonkho epics; Anisimov's field records" .

pmt:SolomonicGrimoire a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Salomonische Grimoire-Tradition"@de,
        "Solomonic grimoire transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of ritual handbooks for the constraint of spirits, carrying preparation, circle, seal, adjuration and dismissal as a fixed sequence."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Clavicula Salomonis; Lemegeton; Heptameron; the Book of Abramelin" .

pmt:Spiritism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Spiritismus"@de,
        "Spiritism"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission founded by Kardec on answers obtained through mediums and arranged as a doctrine, with survival and communication asserted as facts open to verification."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered because of how it states its claims. A tradition that submits itself to examination in its own founding text is the natural first object of the project's examination layer, and the largest single body of nineteenth-century investigation is attached to it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kardec, Le Livre des Esprits; Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums" .

pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Spiritistisches automatisches Schreiben"@de,
        "Spiritualist automatic writing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The nineteenth century transmission of extended texts produced by a writer who reports not composing them, and issued as scripture rather than as message."@en ;
    skos:note "The earliest layer of the block and the template for all of it: a named person, a dated production, a printed volume, and the claim that the author is elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Newbrough, Oahspe; Davis, The Principles of Nature, Her Divine Revelations, and a Voice to Mankind" .

pmt:Srivaisnava a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Śrīvaiṣṇavische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Śrīvaiṣṇava transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission descending from Rāmānuja, which gives the devotional relation a scholastic form and disputes within itself over whether the devotee acts or is acted upon."@en ;
    skos:note "The internal dispute of the two schools, over whether surrender requires an act of the devotee or none, is a candidate for a pm:Disputing node with two attested sides inside one transmission."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rāmānuja, Śrībhāṣya and Gadyatraya; Vedānta Deśika, Rahasyatrayasāra; Piḷḷai Lokācārya, Śrīvacanabhūṣaṇa" .

pmt:Srividya a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Śrīvidyā"@de,
        "Śrīvidyā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the goddess as sound and diagram, with an initiatory sequence tied to the levels of a single mantra."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Yoginīhṛdaya; Nityāṣoḍaśikārṇava; Bhāskararāya, Setubandha" .

pmt:Swedenborgian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Swedenborgische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Swedenborgian transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A transmission resting on one man's sustained reports of conversation with the dead and with angels, kept as a dated journal over decades."@en ;
    skos:note "Quantitatively the largest body of first-person reports available to the project, and dated throughout, which makes internal consistency checkable in a way most such material is not."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale" .

pmt:SyriacAsceticism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Syrische Askese"@de,
        "Syriac ascetic transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The East Syriac transmission of solitary prayer, of the stages of stillness and of the wonder held to interrupt prayer rather than complete it."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Isaac of Nineveh, Ascetical Homilies; John of Dalyatha, Letters; Evagrius, Kephalaia Gnostica in the Syriac recension" .

pmt:TamilSiddhar a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tamilische Siddhar-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Tamil Siddhar transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tamil transmission of bodily perfection, rejuvenation regimens and a deliberately obscure poetic idiom that resists the commentary it invites."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Tirumūlar, Tirumandiram; the Bogar and Agastyar corpora; the kāya-kalpa literature" .

pmt:Thelema a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Thelema"@de,
        "Thelema"@en ;
    skos:definition "Crowley's transmission of a received book, of the discovery of one's own will as the central task, and of a practice curriculum with a demand for recorded results."@en ;
    skos:note "The insistence on keeping a magical diary and treating claims sceptically is a methodological demand made from inside a tradition, and belongs in the graph as such."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Crowley, Liber AL vel Legis; Crowley, Magick in Theory and Practice; Crowley, The Vision and the Voice" .

pmt:Theosophy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Theosophie"@de,
        "Theosophy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The modern transmission of root races, planes and masters, presented as the recovery of a single ancient doctrine underlying all religions."@en ;
    skos:note "The pedigree claim is the tradition's own central assertion and is contested; it belongs in the graph as a claim about origin and not as a fact about transmission. Much of what circulates today as the doctrine of older traditions entered general circulation here."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Blavatsky, Isis Unveiled; Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine; Leadbeater, The Chakras" .

pmt:TheravadaCommentarial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Theravādische Kommentarhandbücher"@de,
        "Theravāda commentarial manuals"@en ;
    skos:definition "The manual layer of Theravāda: the objects of concentration, the marks by which each absorption is recognised, and the higher knowledges said to follow from mastery."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered apart from the canon because the canon states that the attainments occur and the manuals state how to reach them and how to tell whether one has."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga; Upatissa, Vimuttimagga; Paṭisambhidāmagga" .

pmt:Tiantai a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tiantai"@de,
        "Tiantai"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of calming and contemplation, with a systematic account of the obstacles, illnesses and demonic states that arise in practice."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Zhiyi, Mohe zhiguan; Zhiyi, Xiao zhiguan" .

pmt:UgariticRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ugaritisch-levantinisches Ritual"@de,
        "Ugaritic and Levantine ritual"@en ;
    skos:definition "Late Bronze Age Levantine ritual and incantation, including the address to the deified dead."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The KTU corpus, including the Rapiuma texts and the serpent incantations" .

pmt:UrantiaBook a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Das Urantia-Buch"@de,
        "The Urantia Book"@en ;
    skos:definition "A single large revealed cosmology published in 1955, presenting itself as a series of papers by named celestial authors and describing a graded administration of the universe."@en ;
    skos:note "The human circumstances of its production were withheld on purpose by the group that published it, which leaves its provenance the weakest in a block whose provenance is otherwise its strength."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Urantia Book" .

pmt:VaisnavaSahajiya a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Vaiṣṇava-Sahajiyā-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Vaiṣṇava Sahajiyā transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Bengali transmission that takes the devotional relation as a bodily practice, holding that the states named by the aesthetic theory are to be produced rather than contemplated."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered because it is the one station of this sphere that turns a doctrine of states into a procedure, which is what SPEC §14 puts first. Its corpus is small, partly clandestine, and the attribution of several works is contested; that is to be settled before the file is opened, not after."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Vaiṣṇava Sahajiyā padāvalī; Ānanda-bhairava; Vivarta-vilāsa attributed to Ākiñcana-dāsa" .

pmt:Varkari a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Vārkarī-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Vārkarī transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Marathi devotional transmission of the pilgrimage to Paṇḍharpūr, carried in the songs of its poet-saints rather than in a treatise."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Jñāneśvarī; the abhaṅga of Tukārām, Nāmdev and Eknāth" .

pmt:VedicRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Vedische Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Vedic ritual transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The solemn ritual and its exegesis, in which efficacy rests on stated equivalences between the act, the body and the world."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa; Aitareya Brāhmaṇa; the Śrautasūtras" .

pmt:Vodun a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Fon-Vodun-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Fon vodun transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Fon transmission of vodun as powers received into a person, with an initiation understood as a death and a return under a new name."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Maupoil, La géomancie à l'ancienne Côte des Esclaves; Herskovits, Dahomey" .

pmt:Waidan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Waidan, äußere Alchemie"@de,
        "Waidan external alchemy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Chinese transmission of elixirs compounded from mineral substances, with an explicit catalogue of the capacities each elixir is said to confer."@en ;
    skos:note "Ge Hong's inner chapters list attainments in the same enumerated form as the siddhi lists and were compiled without access to them. The comparison is one of the sharpest tests the project can set itself."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian; Zhouyi cantong qi" .

pmt:Yangsheng a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Yangsheng-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Yangsheng nourishing-life transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The early Chinese transmission of breath circulation, guided stretching and dietary regimen for the prolongation of life, with illustrated postures."@en ;
    skos:note "The Mawangdui and Zhangjiashan manuscripts are dated by their burial, which puts the breath material before any documented contact with Indian practice. This is the single most valuable registration in the file for the independence question."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Mawangdui manuscripts, including Daoyin tu and Quegu shiqi; the Zhangjiashan Yinshu; Huangting jing" .

pmt:Yarsan a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Yarsanische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Yarsan transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "An Iranian transmission teaching successive bodily lives as a purification, carried in Gorani verse."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Kalām-e Saranjām" .

pmt:Yazidi a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jesidische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Yazidi transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Kurdish transmission carried chiefly in hymn and recitation, teaching successive returns of the soul."@en ;
    skos:note "The two books circulated as its scripture are held by most scholarship to be forgeries of the early twentieth century, which makes the corpus question itself the first thing to decide."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Qewls (hymns) in the published collections; the disputed Mišefa Reš and Kitêba Cilwe" .

pmt:Yogacara a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Yogācāra"@de,
        "Yogācāra"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of consciousness-only doctrine together with the most detailed Indian description of meditative signs and the stages at which they appear."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Asaṅga, Yogācārabhūmi, particularly the Śrāvakabhūmi; Vasubandhu, Triṃśikā" .

pmt:Zoroastrian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Zoroastrische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Zoroastrian transmission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Iranian transmission of a dualist cosmology, of xvarənah as a conferred radiance, and of a soul journey undertaken and reported."@en ;
    skos:note "Ardā Wīrāz Nāmag is a substance-induced journey with a named traveller, a preparation and a report, and therefore closer to a protocol than most of what the graph holds."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:corpusNamed ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Avesta (Gāthās, Yasna, Vīdēvdād); Bundahišn; Dēnkard; Ardā Wīrāz Nāmag" .

pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schmuck und Kosmetik"@de,
        "Adornment and cosmetics"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of bracelets and ornaments, of antimony and the painting of the eyelids, of precious stones and colouring tinctures."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Kopplung eines Zielzustands an einen starken Affekt verschiebt den Koppelnden"@de,
        "Coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the one who couples"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that binding an intended state to a strong affect shifts what the person attends to, how ready they are to act, how they carry themselves, and where their threshold for risk lies."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately the weak reading. The neighbouring claim of the same section holds that the coupled state acts outside its carrier; that one is not examined here and nothing below bears on it. Separating them is the point: the weak reading is testable and the strong one is not, and running them together would let evidence for the first be read as evidence for the second."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#141" .

pmc:Ahwal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zustände"@de,
        "States"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:definition "Conditions that descend on the wayfarer as a gift and pass, distinguished in the handbooks from the stations precisely by not being attainable through effort."@en ;
    skos:note "No prerequisite claim is entered for these. The tradition holds that they cannot be worked for, which is a claim about the limits of any ordering and not an omission in this record."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:Araqiel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Araqiel"@de,
        "Araqiel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the earth."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Armaros a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Armaros"@de,
        "Armaros"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Hermani"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named in the teaching list as the one who disclosed the undoing of spells."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Aufstieg, der in einer Bewegung jedes Zentrum durchläuft"@de,
        "The ascent that passes through every centre in one movement"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "The power is imagined rising in the form of lightning, at each centre in turn and step by step, upward as far as three fists, and at the end of that ascent stands the great awakening."@en ;
    skos:note "The two phrases that carry this are praticakram, at each centre, and kramāt kramam, step by step. Recorded because it settles two questions at once. It gives a body measure in the base text, three fists for the upward reach, where a modern draft gives an arm's length; and it makes the passage through several centres a single exercise, where the same draft allows one centre per session and calls a change of centre a break. See prima-materia#38 and prima-materia#40."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:Astrology a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astrologie"@de,
        "Astrology"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of terrestrial import from the position of the heavenly bodies."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abwenden des Zorns der Huacas und der Ahnen"@de,
        "Turning aside the anger of the huacas and the ancestors"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "What the confession is made for: the faults of a man are held to anger his ancestors and his huacas, and the anger to be the reason he falls sick. The confession, the blowing of the powders, the rubbing with the pardon-stone and the washing of the head are done so that the anger is turned aside and the sickness with it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:BeautyItself a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schönheit selbst"@de,
        "Beauty itself"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 211c-212a" ;
    skos:definition "The final object of the ascent, seen only by one who has climbed by way of the intervening rungs."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:BeautyOfOneBody a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schönheit eines Körpers"@de,
        "The beauty of one body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 210a-211c" ;
    skos:definition "The first object of the ascent, from which the climb begins."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Giftorakel bringt die Benennung des Hexers hervor"@de,
        "The poison oracle yields the naming of the witch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 35-36 and pp. 141-143" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch .

pmc:Cakra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Cakra"@de,
        "Cakra"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)" ;
    skos:definition "A centre of the subtle body, described in the source with its seed syllables and its position along the central channel."@en ;
    skos:note "The count varies across the older tantras, which give four, five or six. The tradition does not assign the centres the psychological qualities that modern presentations attach to them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:CompletionStage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vollendungsstufe"@de,
        "Completion stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the two stages, to which the six yogas belong."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:ConfessionValidityTest a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Test, der entscheidet, ob eine Beichte galt"@de,
        "The test that decides whether a confession held"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the account of the confession before the ichuri" ;
    skos:definition "Concealing a fault in confession is held a great fault, and the confessor makes great efforts to find it out, by different ceremonies in different places. In some, the penitent first calls on the hills around, the plains, the condors that fly, the owls, saying that he wishes to confess his faults; he says them holding a small bead of shell fixed on a thorn between two fingers of the right hand, the thorn raised; when he has finished he gives it to the confessor, who drives the thorn into his cloak and presses until the bead breaks, and looks at how many parts it broke into. If it broke into three the confession was good; if into two it was not good, and he tells him to confess his faults again. In other places a small bundle of the grass called ichu, from which the confessor's name is taken, is divided in two and straws are drawn one from each side until it is seen whether they come out even, and then the confession was good, or odd, and it was bad. In others they divine it by the blood of guinea pigs."@en ;
    skos:note "A falsifying condition stated by the transmission, applied by its own practitioners to its own act, with a stated outcome and a stated remedy: the confession is void and must be made again. It is not a test of whether the specialist is right; it is a test of whether the layman held something back, so its target is the penitent and not the instrument. That is the opposite arrangement from the one this graph holds for the Zande poison oracle, and the difference is worth more than the resemblance."@en,
        "The same passage records what the procedure is when it fails to persuade: in one village the penitent's hands were tied behind him and squeezed with a cord until he told the truth; a man said before the visitor that his confessor beat him with a stick and another that he was beaten with a rope. A validity test with a coercive fallback is not an oracle, and both are in the same paragraph."@en,
        "The three variants are three different randomising devices — a break count, a parity count and an inspection of blood — attached to one function. That the function is stable across three devices, and that a fourth device with the same parity mechanism is used for divination proper elsewhere in the same corpus, is the finding here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:Constellations a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Sternbilder"@de,
        "The constellations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the fixed groupings of stars."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:CoolingAndQuenching a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kühlung und das Stillen von Hunger und Durst"@de,
        "Cooling and the quenching of hunger and thirst"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The state ascribed to the cooling retention: fever, bile, hunger, thirst and the effect of poisons are said to be put down."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:CourseOfTheMoon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Lauf des Mondes"@de,
        "The course of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the moon's path, the calendrical rather than divinatory form of lunar knowledge."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unterscheidung von bitter und süß"@de,
        "Discernment of bitter and sweet"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The disclosure of the distinction of bitter from sweet, given in the text alongside the secrets of wisdom."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dogon kannten den Begleiter des Sirius"@de,
        "The Dogon knew the companion of Sirius"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1950" ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274, 282 with its first footnote, and 287" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a people without instruments held, as transmitted knowledge, the movements and characteristics of a star that cannot be seen: its period around Sirius, its extreme density, and its priority in creation. The claim is put forward in an article of nineteen fifty which sets the question of how such knowledge could exist and expressly declines to answer or even to pose it."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination and not as a dispute, because a dispute needs two sides that each say something about the matter. Here one side says nothing: no transmission carries the identification, and the tradition file holds only the seed-star. The identification begins in the work named below."@en,
        "The decisive sentence is the one the reception never quotes. On p. 274 the recorders write that the question of how men without instruments know the movements and certain characteristics of practically invisible bodies has not been decided, nor even raised. That sentence grants the premise and withholds the enquiry in one move, and everything the claim later became rests on the granted premise."@en,
        "The later form of the claim, in which the knowledge is explained by contact with visitors from the Sirius system, begins in a work of nineteen seventy-six that has not been read at the wording in this run and is therefore not entered. See prima-materia#251."@en,
        "The second decisive sentence is a footnote. Where the article gives the orbital period as some fifty years, the note observes that this period is very close to that of the companion of Sirius and cites a French astronomical periodical of nineteen thirty-one for it. The identification is therefore the recorders' and is made in the recorders' own apparatus; the body of the text never says Sirius B. What the informants are reported as saying and what the note makes of it are separable on the page, and separating them is what this node exists for."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "ancient Dogon astronomy: a pre-contact African tradition holding the existence, period and density of Sirius B, cited both as evidence of a lost indigenous science and as evidence of extraterrestrial contact" ;
    pm:originatedBy "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen" .

pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Holz, das sich mit dem Sonnenlauf dreht"@de,
        "Wood that turns with the sun's course"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48" ;
    skos:definition "The qualifying material of the drum. It must be a pine, fir or birch grown in a particular place and turning directly according to the sun's course, which the recorder defines: the grain of the wood, running from the bottom of the tree to the top, winds itself from the right hand to the left. A tree so grown is held acceptable to the sun."@en ;
    skos:note "A second condition on the same material stands in the same passage: the piece must be taken from the root, cleft asunder and hollowed on one side, and the skin is stretched over the hollow. It is entered here rather than as a node of its own because it names no criterion by which a piece could fail, only a manner of working."@en,
        "The direction is stated twice in this file in frames that do not obviously agree. Here the sun's course is the grain winding from the right hand to the left as it rises; at pmc:SunCourseRule it is the ring travelling round towards the right hand. Whether a spiral seen along a rising trunk and a ring seen on a flat skin describe one direction or two is not decidable from the passages, and is left standing rather than harmonised."@en,
        "The recorder gives the sun-acceptability as his own conjecture — 'from this perhaps they believe' — not as something told to him. The requirement is reported; its reason is his."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:EmmeYaTolo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Emme ya tolo, der Weibliche-Hirse-Stern"@de,
        "Emme ya tolo, the Female Sorghum star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 287-288" ;
    skos:definition "A second companion said to run a wider course in the same direction and the same time as the seed-star, four times lighter and larger than it, called the sun of the women, and named as the seat of the female souls of all beings living or to come. It is said to have a satellite of its own, the star of the Women."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fasten des Herzens"@de,
        "Fasting of the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhuangzi 4" ;
    skos:definition "A graded emptying in which listening passes from the ears to the heart-mind and from the heart-mind to the qi, until only emptiness remains for the way to gather in."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:EnergyTakenFromAPersonThroughTheLeftHand a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Energie lässt sich aus einer Person über die linke Hand aufnehmen"@de,
        "Energy can be taken from a person through the left hand"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that something passes from one person to another during contact, and that it enters through one named hand rather than the other."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims in one sentence, and they are not equally reachable. That something passes is the general transfer claim of the section; that it enters through the left hand and not the right is a statement about sides, and a statement about sides is the kind that can be checked."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#328" .

pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Energie lässt sich aus Kälte aufnehmen"@de,
        "Energy can be taken in from cold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that exposure to cold is a source from which the practitioner draws, rather than a condition he works against."@en ;
    skos:note "The narrowest of the draft's uptake claims and the only one that names a measurable boundary condition, which is why it is entered first."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#133" .

pmc:EnergyTakenFromWind a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Energie lässt sich aus Wind aufnehmen"@de,
        "Energy can be taken in from wind"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that moving air is a source from which the practitioner draws, beyond what breathing it supplies."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#134" .

pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gerade Rumpf als Merkmal einer Sitzhaltung"@de,
        "The straight trunk as the mark of a seat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.21 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A seat defined by sitting evenly with the body straight; the straightness is part of what the posture is, not a separate instruction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Genauigkeit der Rezitation ist als Bedingung ihrer Wirksamkeit gesetzt"@de,
        "The exactness of the recitation is set as the condition of its efficacy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
        "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 615-616",
        "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), sections 41-42, pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "Three transmissions make the same claim about spoken ritual language: the effect is not obtained unless the words are rendered as they stand. Two of them also say what a lapse does. One holds that an error, and equally a hesitation, is fatal to the efficacy of the address. Another holds that an error in a song may be fatal to the ceremony and that in some cases a single syllable does irreparable harm. The third requires the publicly chanted parts to be letter perfect and, in the passages read, states no consequence."@en ;
    skos:note "A defect in the vocabulary shows at this node and is recorded rather than worked around. pm:contactRoute is a property of a single tradition, but contact is a relation between two. The value pm:contactRouteAbsent as the registry uses it means that no route runs to what the graph already held at the time of registration; it cannot answer whether a route runs between two registered traditions that are later brought together in one correspondence, which is exactly the question a convergence asks. See prima-materia#101 for the vocabulary defect and prima-materia#102 for the registry entries it affects."@en,
        "The counter-search did not produce a tradition that denies the claim. What it produced is of a different kind and belongs here: the recorded versions of the same Māori address differ from one another across the eighteen-forties to the early nineteen-hundreds, and which rendering was valid was disputed among Māori at the time. A doctrine of one correct wording and a transmission that visibly carries several are not a contradiction, but they are the point at which the claim becomes examinable instead of edifying. The place that says so has not been read at the wording and stands at prima-materia#110."@en,
        "Three witnesses agreeing on the form of a rule is a weaker finding than it looks unless the alternative is stated. The alternative exists and is common: traditions in which the sense of a prayer carries it and the wording may be varied, and traditions in which improvisation is the mark of the competent practitioner. That the three named here all fall on the fixed side is what the correspondence asserts. Where the rule holds and where it is absent has not been surveyed; the spheres left unsearched stand at prima-materia#116, the Indic parallel at prima-materia#109."@en,
        "What the node claims and what it does not. Independent attestation is asserted for the two North American transmissions with respect to the Polynesian one and with respect to everything else this collection holds; no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the periods in which any of these corpora were fixed. It is not asserted between those two: they are neighbours in one region, and a route between Pueblo and Athabaskan ceremonialism is neither absent nor established here. The Māori witness is therefore the third leg and not a third independent one, because its own contact route is entered as disputed."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:MaoriExactRecitation,
        pmc:NavajoSongExactness,
        pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness ;
    pm:independenceGround "Not a claim of priority in time. The two American corpora were fixed in eighteen ninety-seven and, from fieldwork of the nineteen-twenties, in nineteen thirty-two, both after the Polynesian witness was printed in eighteen eighty-two. What carries the independence is that no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the period in which any of the three corpora were fixed, so agreement between them cannot be one statement counted twice. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide: both American corpora were gathered by ethnographers working in a discipline to which the rule of exact recitation was already familiar, and neither passage says whether the rule was volunteered in the field or asked for. The second is the gathering question and it stands open." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:NavajoCeremonial,
        pmt:ZuniRitual .

pmc:Ezeqeel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ezeqeel"@de,
        "Ezeqeel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named in the Ethiopic teaching list as the one who disclosed the knowledge of the clouds."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:FirstWeek a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die erste Woche"@de,
        "The first week"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4" ;
    skos:definition "The purgative part of the exercises, given over to the consideration of sin."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:FiveHindrances a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die fünf Hemmnisse"@de,
        "The five hindrances"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt, whose abandoning the discourse describes immediately before entry into the first absorption."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Gadreel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gadreel"@de,
        "Gadreel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who led Eve astray and disclosed the weapons of death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:GreaterMysteries a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die großen Mysterien"@de,
        "The greater mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C" ;
    skos:definition "The rite at Eleusis, held in the month Boedromion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EleusinianMysteries .

pmc:HauOfTheGift a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Hau der Gabe"@de,
        "The hau of the gift"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271" ;
    skos:definition "The account, given by a named Māori authority in a letter, that what comes back from a third party for a valuable passed on is the hau of the valuable first received, and that keeping it back for oneself would be fatal to the keeper."@en ;
    skos:note "Held at mediated attestation because the letter is an archival manuscript and was read in the study that prints and collates it, not in the archive. The published version of 1909 could not be opened in this run either; the paths tried are recorded in prima-materia#106. This node is therefore an open post and is to be raised to textual attestation when either the manuscript or the 1909 printing is read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "Manuka Hēnare, \"Ko te hau tēnā o tō taonga…\": The words of Ranapiri on the spirit of gift exchange and economy, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Ordnung der Affekte nach gemessener Stufe"@de,
        "The ranking of emotions by measured level"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1995" ;
    dcterms:source "D. R. Hawkins, Power vs. Force (1995)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that emotional and mental states occupy fixed positions on a single numbered scale, ascending from shame through fear, anger and pride to reason, love, joy, peace and enlightenment, and that the position can be established by testing."@en ;
    skos:note "An earlier form of this note held that no transmission ranks affects on a single ordered scale. That was withdrawn: the ground for it had been a search across the holdings of this graph, which says what has been entered here and not what the transmissions say. The search that had not been carried out finds one at once. Rūpa Gosvāmin ranks the five primary forms of devotional love in a single ascending order in the sixteenth century, in the verse itself, and traditions/gaudiya-vaisnava.ttl now holds it. The finding of this node is therefore not that ranking affects is modern. It is narrower and survives: the particular scale below, with its count, its order and its claim of measurability, begins here."@en,
        "The diagnostic markers, given so that any later list presenting itself as old can be measured against a dated first appearance. The count: seventeen steps. The order of the lower band: shame, guilt, apathy, grief, fear, desire, anger, pride. The turn at the middle: courage, then neutrality, then willingness in eleventh place. The upper band: acceptance, reason, love, joy, peace, enlightenment. A list that shares the count, the relative order of shame, guilt, fear and anger, and the eleventh position of willingness has not arrived at them independently."@en,
        "The nearest older relative is pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling, and the comparison sharpens rather than dissolves this node. That transmission supplies the vibration vocabulary and an axis of self-reference on which a feeling rises or falls, and it was read at the wording for this purpose. It supplies no ranking of unlike feelings against one another. The single numbered order is therefore not inherited, and the question of where it does come from is open in prima-materia#12."@en ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A perennial map of consciousness, reported as the distilled agreement of the mystical traditions." ;
    pm:originatedBy "David R. Hawkins" .

pmc:HealingByTouch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heilung durch Berührung"@de,
        "Healing by touch"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" ;
    skos:definition "The restoration of a withered hand and of sight, ascribed to the touch of a ruler in Alexandria."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:HeatWithoutForcefulBreath a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Hitze entsteht ohne den kraftvollen Atem"@de,
        "The heat arises without the forceful breath"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the gentler form of the practice, without the forceful breathing, produces the same effect as the form the tradition prescribes."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the point where the method draft parts company with the transmission it draws on. The transmission prescribes the forceful breath; the draft says it can be left out. That is a claim about the draft's own variant and belongs to nobody else, which is why it is posited and not sourced."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#318" .

pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Dem Mann der Erzählerin wird das zweite Gesicht zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The narrator's husband is said to have the gift of second sight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29" ;
    skos:definition "The narrator states of her husband Padloq, whom she calls by a name she glosses as the one who keeps her alive, that he has the gift of second sight and always knows when anything remarkable is going to happen. The case she gives is a dream, on a journey from Iglulik to Tununeq, that a friend of his had been eaten by his nearest kin; on that journey they met the woman who had eaten her own family."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is universal in form, which is what makes it examinable: he always knows. One confirming case is related and no failing case is; the case is retrospective and was told after the meeting it is said to have foretold. The protocol stands at examinations/inuit-second-sight.ttl."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:InuitSecondSight ;
    pm:ascribedTo "Padloq, the narrator Takornâq's husband" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Benennung dessen, der gerade behext"@de,
        "Naming the one who is bewitching at this moment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4 and pp. 35-36" ;
    skos:definition "What the poison oracle is chiefly consulted for: to learn whether witchcraft threatens an undertaking or a person's health, and if so which neighbour is at this moment exercising it, so that he may be approached and asked to withdraw it. The question is never whether a man is a witch but whether he is now bewitching the questioner."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:IfaForeknowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vorauswissen über den Ausgang einer Sache"@de,
        "Foreknowledge of how a matter will fall out"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the oracle is consulted for: to learn beforehand how a matter stands and will fall out. The answer is delivered through the channel of parables, and the account of eighteen ninety-four reports that nothing of importance is undertaken without the consultation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:IfaSixteenMothers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die sechzehn Figuren, Mütter genannt"@de,
        "The sixteen figures called mothers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The sixteen figures the casting can produce, named mothers in the account of eighteen ninety-four. A figure formed by taking one column from one mother and one column from another is named a child."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederfinden verlorener Dinge"@de,
        "The recovery of lost things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to bring back an object that has been lost, named in the narrative for iron and steel knives, which were rare at the time and often lost."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitSecondSight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zweites Gesicht"@de,
        "Second sight"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to know in advance that something remarkable is about to happen, reported in the narrative as a standing property of a person rather than as the outcome of an exercise."@en ;
    skos:note "The English term is the translator's. What the node carries is the claim reported under it and not the term's own history in Scottish and Scandinavian usage; whether the Inuktitut word behind it has that range is not decidable from the portion read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Verbot, auf Oqshoriaq neues Karibufell zu nähen"@de,
        "The prohibition on sewing new caribou skin on Oqshoriaq"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29" ;
    skos:definition "A prohibition attached to a named place: garments of new caribou skin may not be sewn on the island of Oqshoriaq."@en ;
    skos:note "The prohibition is local and specific, which distinguishes it from the general taboo system the later chapters of the volume set out. Whether it is a case of that system or a rule of its own cannot be decided from the portion read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:Kasdeja a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kasdeja"@de,
        "Kasdeja"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the striking of spirits, the killing of the embryo and the serpent's bite."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:KevalaKumbhaka a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Alleiniges Anhalten"@de,
        "Retention alone"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. The manual calls it the accomplished form of the practice and says nothing in the three worlds is beyond one who holds it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:KhuddikaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geringe Verzückung"@de,
        "Minor rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The least of the five degrees of rapture, described as able to raise the hairs of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage that attests piloerection as a recognised concomitant of a meditative state, not as its object."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:KindledInnerFire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das entfachte innere Feuer"@de,
        "The kindled inner fire"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A heat said to arise when the raised wind reaches the region of fire, lengthening its flame, until the sleeping power is warmed through and straightens."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:KnowledgeOfClouds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wolkenkunde"@de,
        "Knowledge of the clouds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of weather and import from cloud formations."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Leiter der dreißig Stufen"@de,
        "The ladder of thirty steps"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30" ;
    skos:definition "An ascent in thirty steps, running from renunciation and exile through the active virtues to stillness, prayer, freedom from the passions and love, and passing in its course from human labour to what is given."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:LesserMysteries a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die kleinen Mysterien"@de,
        "The lesser mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C" ;
    skos:definition "The preparatory rite at Agrai, held in the month Anthesterion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EleusinianMysteries .

pmc:LightKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Leichte Kenntnis"@de,
        "Light knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274" ;
    skos:definition "The first stage of a secret knowledge, named with a Bambara expression. The account states that the rules of the Sirius system revealed to initiates at this stage are simplified in some parts and complicated in others, in order to turn attention away from calculations that remain more secret still."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:LightOfKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Leuchten der Erkenntnis"@de,
        "The light of knowledge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28" ;
    skos:definition "What is said to arise, on the wearing away of impurity, and to continue up to discriminative discernment."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:LoosingOfSpells a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lösen von Beschwörungen"@de,
        "Loosing of spells"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The undoing of a binding laid by another, held in the text as a distinct art from the laying of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit der Karakia"@de,
        "The efficacy of the karakia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 11-12" ;
    skos:definition "The effect an invocation is held to obtain, which the closing petition asks the invoked ancestor to grant."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:Maqamat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Stationen"@de,
        "Stations"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ" ;
    skos:definition "Stages of the path attained by effort and held by the one who reaches them, enumerated in the handbooks in a set order."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:Metalworking a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Metallverarbeitung und Waffenherstellung"@de,
        "Metalworking and weapon-making"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the metals of the earth and of working them into blades, shields and body armour."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:MideForesight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Blick in das Künftige"@de,
        "Looking into futurity"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to see what is to come, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the pictographic chart by lines drawn upward from the eyes."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie"@de,
        "The efficacy of the ceremony"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The effect a chantway is held to obtain, which the witness says an error in a song may destroy."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:NeidanThreeStages a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Stufen des inneren Elixiers"@de,
        "The three stages of the inner elixir"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "Refining essence into breath, breath into spirit, and spirit back into emptiness."@en ;
    skos:note "A dating warning belongs on this node. The sequence is routinely attributed to the Cantong qi or to the Wuzhen pian, but neither carries it as a fixed numbered progression; the Wuzhen pian sets only a precedence of vital force before inner nature, and of doing before non-doing. The earliest ordered witness located for the full triad is the Yuan-period Jindan dayao."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Drei Wege in das Amt"@de,
        "Three ways into the office"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "By succession, the son inheriting from the father, and where the heir is too young the nearest kinsman standing in until he is fit. By election, where succession fails, the other ministers choosing whom they judge fittest with the agreement of the lords. Or by a man taking the office himself, which is said to be ordinary among the old for the lesser offices of diviner and healer, and to be done for a living."@en ;
    skos:note "The third way is entered with the others because the recorder gives it the same weight and because it is the one a later reader would drop. A transmission that admits self-appointment for its lesser offices is saying where its own boundary is soft."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wonach das Orakel befragt wird"@de,
        "What the oracle is asked for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the consultation is undertaken for: to be told whatever the questioners wish to know, with no restriction of subject stated in the passage."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die beiden Winde werden zugleich in entgegengesetzte Richtungen getrieben"@de,
        "The two winds are driven in opposite directions at once"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.50 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The apāna is raised from below while the prāṇa is pressed down from the throat, and the two are brought together in the region of the navel."@en ;
    skos:note "The manual assigns direction to the two winds, not to the two phases of breathing. Both directions run in the same operation and against each other, so a scheme that reads inhalation as upward and exhalation as downward, or the reverse, has no purchase here. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:OracleRankOrder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rangordnung der Orakel"@de,
        "The graded order of the oracles"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164" ;
    skos:definition "Where more than one oracle is consulted the lesser is always consulted before the greater, in the order rubbing-board, termites, poison. A verdict of the termites is not put to the rubbing-board for confirmation, and a verdict of the poison oracle is not put to the termites. On all important matters the decision of the termites must be corroborated by the poison oracle."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is a rule about the direction in which confirmation may run and not merely a ranking by esteem. It is what makes the cheaper oracles usable at all: a man narrows the field with the termites and spends poison only on the final decision."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ParalysisIsTheRegularShutdown a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lähmung ist die reguläre Abschaltung der Motorik am Schlafübergang"@de,
        "The paralysis is the regular shutdown of movement at the threshold of sleep"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that what is called sleep paralysis is the ordinary switching off of motor control that happens as sleep is entered, and therefore a regular part of the architecture of sleep rather than a disorder."@en ;
    skos:note "The weaker of the two claims in this file and separated from the other on purpose. This one places the event and calls it regular; pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice adds that it is universal and that only its noticing is rare. The two are examined apart because they fail apart, and one of them does."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#137" .

pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lähmung liegt bei jedem vor, selten ist nur ihr Bemerken"@de,
        "The paralysis is present in everyone and only its noticing is rare"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the motor paralysis at the threshold of sleep is a regular feature of every sleeper rather than a disorder of some, and that it becomes noticeable only where waking awareness outlasts the body's entry into it."@en ;
    skos:note "Split deliberately from the weaker claim that sleep paralysis is the regular shutdown of motor control at the sleep transition, which is prima-materia#137 and stands on its own. This one adds the two things that make it testable: a universality and an explanation of why a universal thing is rarely reported."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#138" .

pmc:Pneuma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pneuma"@de,
        "Pneuma"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8" ;
    skos:definition "A mixture of air and fire that pervades bodies and, by its tension, holds them in cohesion; a principle of the world rather than a capacity of a practitioner."@en ;
    skos:note "The associated doctrines are that of tension and that of total mixture, by which two bodies occupy the same place throughout."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:StoicPhysics .

pmc:PneumaticVehicle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das pneumatische Seelenfahrzeug"@de,
        "The pneumatic vehicle of the soul"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:definition "A subtle vehicle carried by the soul, whose purity is held to decide whether what appears in a dream is clear and true or clouded."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:PoTolo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pô tolo, der Digitaria-Stern"@de,
        "Pô tolo, the Digitaria star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 284 and 287" ;
    skos:definition "A star named after the fonio grain, said to be white where Sirius is seen red, to be the first thing created, to be the egg of the world out of which everything visible and invisible developed, and to consist of a metal called sagala. It is called at once the smallest of all things and the heaviest, weighed in the account at four hundred and eighty donkey loads, so that all earthly beings together could not lift it."@en ;
    skos:note "The number four hundred and eighty is glossed in the article itself as the product of the base eighty and the count of tens in the older base sixty, and is said to be used as the symbol of the greatest of numbers. A reader who takes it for a measured mass is reading against the note printed beneath it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:PoToloAsAxis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Same-Stern als Achse der Welt"@de,
        "The seed-star as the axis of the world"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 287" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the orbit of the seed-star lies at the centre of the world, that without its movement no heavenly body could hold its place, and that it orders the position of Sirius in particular, which is called the most disordered of the stars."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:Prana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Prāṇa"@de,
        "Prāṇa"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 1.11.5",
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)" ;
    skos:definition "Vital breath, presented as that on which the parts of the person rest and out of which beings come forth, in the image of a wheel resting on its hub."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Upanisadic .

pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herzensgebet"@de,
        "The prayer of the heart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart" ;
    skos:definition "The continuous inward prayer that gives the tradition its practice."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Zurückhalten des herabfließenden Nektars"@de,
        "The retention of the descending nectar"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.77 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The stopping of a fluid held to fall from above and to be consumed below, whose loss the manual makes responsible for ageing and death."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wiedergabe der beiden Śākta-Zweige als rechtshändiges und linkshändiges Ritual"@de,
        "The rendering of the two Śākta branches as a right and a left hand ritual"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1832" ;
    dcterms:source "H. H. Wilson, Sketch of the Religious Sects of the Hindus, Asiatic Researches XVII (Calcutta 1832), the sections headed Dakshinas, or Bhaktas and Vamis, or Vamacharis" ;
    skos:definition "The English naming of the dakṣiṇācāra and vāmācāra divisions of the Śākta sect as the followers of a right hand and a left hand ritual, together with the characterisation of the second as impure, as running contrary to what is usual, and as something its adherents will not publicly avow."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an ordering act because the naming is regularly credited to Blavatsky, who wrote in 1877 and in 1888. It stands in Wilson forty-five years earlier, in the volume of the Asiatic Researches for 1832, in those terms and with the moral charge already attached. Whoever first coined it, it was not she, and a record that repeats the later attribution reports a reception as an origin. See prima-materia#57."@en,
        "No pm:ordersClaim is given, because the division it orders is not yet in the graph as a claim of its own: that would need a passage in a tantra rather than a passage in Wilson. See prima-materia#53."@en,
        "The same section carries a ranking the naming does not survive. Wilson translates a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava that grades the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava and the Śaiva to the right hand Śākta, then sets the left hand above the right, the Siddhānta above that, and the Kaula above all. In the material being ordered, the left is therefore the higher of the two; the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses the valuation. The verse is not located in an edition of the Kulārṇava here and is not held as a claim of that work. See prima-materia#56."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:compiledBy "Horace Hayman Wilson" .

pmc:Romanca a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Haaraufrichtung"@de,
        "Horripilation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bhagavadgītā 1.29",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "The raising of the body hair, counted among the eight involuntary signs and treated as a mark of an inner state, not as something to be produced for its own sake."@en ;
    skos:note "Attested outside the dramaturgical treatise in the epic corpus, where trembling and horripilation are named together as signs of an overwhelming state."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SanskritDramaturgy .

pmc:RootCutting a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wurzelschneiden"@de,
        "Root-cutting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The gathering and preparation of plant material for pharmacological or operative use."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Rtsa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kanäle"@de,
        "Channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The channels of the subtle body through which the winds are held to move; Tsongkhapa's presentation counts one hundred and twenty principal ones."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:Ruach a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ruach"@de,
        "Ruaḥ"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
        "Sefer Yetzirah 6:7 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)" ;
    skos:definition "Breath or spirit, named as the first of the ten emanations, from which air issues, from air water, and from water fire."@en ;
    skos:note "The citation was extended from 1:9-10 to 1:9-12 when the passage was reopened: in this numbering the first emanation is breath, the second breath from breath, and only the third and fourth carry water from breath and fire from water. The earlier range stopped two verses short of the sequence the definition states. The numbering is recension-dependent throughout, and the long and Saadia versions divide the same material otherwise."@en,
        "The text also has the patriarch stirring the letters with breath, which is the closest the source comes to breath as an operative medium; it remains an act of formation, not a conduction through the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SeferYetzirah .

pmc:Saktipata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Herabkunft der Kraft"@de,
        "Descent of power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra" ;
    skos:definition "The transfer of power from one who has it to one who does not, graded in the Kashmirian exegesis into nine degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "The sources give transfer by glance, by touch and by word. The specifically manual modality of the modern draft is not attested here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:SecondWeek a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Woche"@de,
        "The second week"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4" ;
    skos:definition "The part given over to the life of Christ up to Palm Sunday, and to the following of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:Shamsiel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schamsiel"@de,
        "Shamsiel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the sun."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfLightning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Blitze"@de,
        "Signs of the lightning flashes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from lightning, given in place of astrology in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfShootingStars a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Sternschnuppen"@de,
        "Signs of the shooting stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from meteors, given in place of the knowledge of the clouds in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheEarth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Erde"@de,
        "Signs of the earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from terrestrial phenomena."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheMoon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen des Mondes"@de,
        "Signs of the moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from the moon, given in place of its course in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheStars a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Sterne"@de,
        "Signs of the stars"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from the stars, given in place of the constellations in the Aramaic-Greek witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SignsOfTheSun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zeichen der Sonne"@de,
        "Signs of the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The reading of import from the sun."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Sila a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sittlichkeit"@de,
        "Virtue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three trainings, on which the handbook is built as a commentary on the verse about the tangle."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:SpellCasting a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beschwörung"@de,
        "Spell-casting"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The binding of persons or events by spoken formula."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:StrikingOfSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schlagen der Geister und der Leibesfrucht"@de,
        "Striking of spirits and of the womb"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The disclosure of harmful operations: the striking of spirits and demons, the killing of the embryo in the womb, the serpent's bite and the stroke of the noonday heat."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SunCourseRule a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Regel des Sonnenlaufs"@de,
        "The rule of the sun's course"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 474 and 477" ;
    skos:definition "The single rule by which the drum is read. If the ring travels round towards the right hand, with the course of the sun, health, fortune and increase of men and beasts are promised. If it goes the contrary way, towards the left, sickness and all the evils of ill success are expected. The later recorder gives the same rule in weaker terms on the negative side: with the sun it augured a favourable event, against the sun a doubt then arose of a prosperous one."@en ;
    skos:note "One further reading is given by the later recorder alone, from a named practitioner: that if the copper bird laid on the skin falls from the drum to the earth, it is a sign that the person enquired of will soon die. That is a third outcome beside the two directions, and it is entered here because a binary instrument with a third outcome is not binary."@en,
        "The difference between them is not nothing. The earlier makes the contrary turn a prediction of evil; the later makes it only a doubt. Whether that is a change in the practice, a difference between regions, or the difference between a compiler stating a rule and a missionary reporting what a man told him is not decidable from the two passages."@en,
        "Two recorders, ninety years apart, one compiling from priests' reports in Uppsala and one living among the people, give the same reading rule tied to the same physical direction. It is the best-attested single claim in this file."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:Tapu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Tapu"@de,
        "Tapu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 25-26" ;
    skos:definition "The state of being sacred and thereby prohibited, held to be communicated from an atua or a sacred person to whatever they touch, and from that object onward to whatever it touches in turn."@en ;
    skos:note "The re-transmission by contact is the part later theory generalised. A record that carries only the prohibition and not the transmissibility loses exactly what the nineteenth-century comparativists picked up and turned into an impersonal magical fluid. See prima-materia#103 and prima-materia#104 for the originations that are not yet entered."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:Tawba a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Umkehr"@de,
        "Turning back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations" ;
    skos:definition "Repentance, placed first among the stations in the handbook enumeration."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Sufism .

pmc:TestingOfThoughts a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Prüfung der Gedanken"@de,
        "The examination of thoughts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 334",
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22" ;
    skos:definition "A four-part examination of an inner movement, set out in the image of the money-changer who tests a coin: for the metal, for the stamp, for the lawfulness of its provenance, and for its weight."@en ;
    skos:note "Ignatius adds the rule that the whole course of a train of thought is to be examined, since a bad end reveals the origin, and that a consolation arriving after the fact is to be examined well before it is believed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "John Cassian, transmitting Abba Moses; supplemented by Ignatius of Loyola" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A movement fails the test if its metal is false, its stamp is not that of right teaching, its provenance is unlawful, or it weighs light of the fear of God and heavy of human vanity; and it fails if the end of the train of thought is bad, however good its beginning." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-28" ;
    pm:tests pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:Theologike a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Theologikē"@de,
        "Knowledge of God"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    skos:definition "The third of the three parts, the knowledge of God."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tummo bringt innere Hitze hervor"@de,
        "Tummo yields inner heat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The one pairing in this file that names a capacity with an outward, measurable side. Any later protocol has a clearer target here than anywhere else in the collection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Tummo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:InnerHeat .

pmc:Vespasian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vespasian"@de,
        "Vespasian"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81" ;
    skos:definition "The Roman emperor to whom the Alexandrian healings are ascribed by two independent historians."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:Watchfulness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wachsamkeit"@de,
        "Watchfulness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Philokalia I, Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters",
        "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness" ;
    skos:definition "The guarding of the heart and the attention to what arises in it, treated as the groundwork on which the prayer of the heart is built."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hesychasm .

pmc:WeaponsOfDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Waffen des Todes"@de,
        "The weapons of death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "Shield, breastplate and the sword for slaughter, disclosed as instruments of killing rather than as craft."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte sollen das Wiederaufleben ermöglichen"@de,
        "Words spoken at birth are said to make the return after death possible"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that those at whose birth such words were uttered can, if they die, come to life again and return among people."@en ;
    skos:note "The interval between the act and its effect is a whole life, which puts the claim beyond any protocol that could be run on it and is worth recording as a property of the claim rather than as a defect of the record."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath .

pmc:WorldPathTraversal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Durchquerung des Weltenpfads"@de,
        "The traversal of the path of worlds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 57 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "The whole is contemplated in the form of the path of worlds and the other paths, in sequence, in its coarse, subtle and supreme condition, until the mind dissolves; and the same method carried to the limits of the universe is said to bring the great awakening."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the old form of the claim that attention placed in a centre opens an assigned layer. The layers here are worlds, bhuvana, and they belong to a cosmology written into the body, not to a list of psychological qualities. The distance between the two is the whole point of the mapping node above. See prima-materia#39."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:WritingWithInk a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schreiben mit Tinte"@de,
        "Writing with ink"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "The art of fixing speech in ink on paper, treated in the text as a transgression rather than a gift."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:XingqiDepth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tiefe als erste Stufe"@de,
        "Depth as the first stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:definition "The opening stage of the inscription: the breath is taken deeply, whereupon it gathers."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:Ziqel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ziqel"@de,
        "Ziqel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher named in the Aramaic-Greek teaching list as the one who disclosed the signs of the shooting stars. The position he occupies is held by Ezeqeel in the Ethiopic; no identification of the two is asserted here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirksamkeit des Zuni-Gebets"@de,
        "The efficacy of the Zuni prayer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615" ;
    skos:definition "The effect the fixed prayer is held to obtain when it is delivered as required."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pm:MeaningCluster a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bedeutungs-Cluster"@de,
        "Meaning Cluster"@en ;
    skos:definition "A bounded subgraph that carries emergent meaning irreducible to its individual triples. Designed for vector embedding as a unit."@en ;
    skos:note "Implements the Sphoṭa principle (Bhartṛhari): meaning resides in the whole utterance, not in isolated phonemes. The subgraph is the phoneme-analog; the cluster is the sphoṭa."@en .

pm:Symbolizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Symbolisieren"@de,
        "Symbolizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of one form standing for another within awareness."@en .

pm:casesWithoutDeviation a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Fälle ohne Abweichung"@de,
        "Cases without deviation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cases have been recorded and show no deviation from the stated base rate."@en .

pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Behauptung unterscheidet nicht"@de,
        "Claim does not discriminate"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cases have been recorded, they show no deviation, and the claim is nonetheless uninformative: it is worded so that outcomes which differ from one another all satisfy it equally."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinction that decides which of the two applies is not how strong the evidence is but whether an outcome exists that the claim would have ruled out. If the claim covers a result and its opposite alike, it discriminates nothing and belongs here, however well the cases fit."@en,
        "The fifth state and the least obvious. It is not a milder pm:casesWithoutDeviation. There the claim said something and the cases bore it out; here the cases bear it out because the claim did not say enough to be borne out against anything. Recording such a protocol as cases without deviation would enter a success where an examination in fact found the claim empty, and a reader counting confirmations would count this one."@en,
        "This state exists because a case forced it. A claim that coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the threshold for risk is confirmed by the evidence and useless to whoever acts on it, because fear and anger are both strong and move that threshold in opposite directions. See prima-materia#141."@en .

pm:conjugalTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Bräutliches Ziel"@de,
        "Conjugal telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the bounded self is neither dissolved nor kept apart but carried into a union of two that preserves both parties to it, so that what is transformed persists as one side of a relation rather than as an absolute or as nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from pm:separativeTelos for the same reason from the other side: what persists here is not its own absolute but a party to a bond, and it is transformed on the way rather than fortified."@en,
        "Distinct from pm:unitiveTelos, and the distinction is not verbal. A dissolution has one term at the end; a marriage has two. A record that files a bridal end state as unitive reports the disappearance of something the source keeps."@en .

pm:individuatingTelos a pm:TelicOrientation ;
    rdfs:label "Individuierendes Ziel"@de,
        "Individuating telos"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path claims that the creature's proper movement is to hold itself as itself against a dissolution that would end it, while denying that the movement is towards difference or towards becoming an absolute; what is sought is the creature's own being and not its distinction from others."@en ;
    skos:note "Between the two older values and reducible to neither. It refuses dissolution, so it is not unitive; and it refuses the striving after difference, so it is not separative. The distinction is fine and load-bearing: a scheme that reads every refusal of dissolution as self-deification cannot state it, and that misreading is common enough to be worth a term."@en .

pm:protocolledPractice a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Protokollierte Praxis"@de,
        "Protocolled practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "A practice repeated under a stated procedure that records its failures alongside its successes."@en .

pmp:BabalawoPupilage a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Lehrzeit des Anwärters auf das Amt des Babalawo"@de,
        "The pupilage of a candidate for the office of babalawo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo" ;
    skos:definition "A candidate for the office is expected to be the pupil of a babalawo known for wide knowledge of the religion and for skill in divining, for at least three years, and the course is divided into three parts covering those years. In the first the candidate learns the names of all the signs and the marks that stand for them and shows his command of them in practice; in the second he learns the traditions attached to them."@en ;
    skos:note "What the third year requires was not recovered from the portion of the extract delivered in this run. The gap is a property of the retrieval and not of the work, and it is recorded so that the next reader opens the appendix at that place rather than repeating the whole."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der bestätigende zweite Test am Giftorakel"@de,
        "The corroborative second test at the poison oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-140" ;
    skos:definition "A second question, put with a second fowl, in which the oracle is told what its first answer was and asked to confirm it by the opposite outcome. The first test is called bambata sima and the second gingo. If the first fowl died the second must survive, and if the first survived the second must die."@en ;
    skos:note "The two tests may be made at one seance where the poison suffices, and are often separated by weeks or months. The transmission names six situations in which a single test is accepted instead, among them the case where the answer came decisively and the case where a lesser oracle had already been asked; but two tests are said to be indispensable in any question concerning the relations between two persons, and especially where a legal issue is involved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:BengeTaboos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tabus vor der Berührung des Orakelgifts"@de,
        "The taboos kept before touching the oracle poison"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 132-133" ;
    skos:definition "Whoever comes into contact with the poison abstains from sexual relations, from the flesh of the elephant, from fish, from two glutinous vegetables named in the source, and from smoking hemp. Sexual abstinence is set at five or six days and abstinence from the forbidden foods at three or four, though the source records that the period is not fixed and that men give different estimates."@en ;
    skos:note "A man asked to operate who has not kept the sexual taboo says that he has eaten the first of the two vegetables, and everyone understands the euphemism. The source adds that he may use the same words when he simply does not want the work, which makes the phrase a refusal as well as a confession."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:DreamDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumdivination"@de,
        "Dream divination"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate use of dreams as a source of knowledge, treated in this literature as available to anyone and requiring no instrument beyond the sleeper."@en ;
    skos:note "The absence of an instrument and of an office is the point at which this tradition differs from the incubation cult: no temple, no priest, no fee."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmp:DreamYoga a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga"@de,
        "Dream yoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state" ;
    skos:definition "The training of awareness within the dream, set out in the treatise as a course of exercises in apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan rmi lam, transliterated milam. Older witnesses stand behind the fifteenth-century treatise: the dream-yoga manuals in the collected works of Gampopa (1079-1153) and the root verses ascribed to Tilopa. That ascription is traditional and only partly secured philologically. Note also that the list of the six is unstable between witnesses, and that the name six yogas is a western coinage for what the tradition calls six dharmas."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fasten vor dem Antritt eines höheren Amtes"@de,
        "The fast before entering a major office"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III" ;
    skos:definition "Whoever is to enter one of the greater offices fasts: a month, in other places six, in others a year, eating neither salt nor chilli, not sleeping with his wife, not washing and not combing. In some places he must not bring his hands to his body, and one who kept it said that in the time of his fast he scratched himself with a little stick."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:FireReading a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Lesen am Verbrennen von Fett und Coca"@de,
        "Reading the burning of fat and coca"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc" ;
    skos:definition "Those who burned llama fat and coca in the fire and saw in the shapes and the marks made at the time of the burning what was to come, and told it to whoever consulted them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:FourteenfoldMastery a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Beherrschung des Geistes in vierzehn Weisen"@de,
        "Control of the mind in fourteen ways"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.2 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "A numbered course of mastery over the meditation objects, among them the order of the kasiṇa and its reverse, skipping the absorption, skipping the object, and the definition of the object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:InuitSpiritCalling a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Geisterrufen"@de,
        "Spirit calling"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28" ;
    skos:definition "The session in which a shaman calls up his helping spirits and puts a question to them, held in the household on the occasion that requires it rather than at a fixed time."@en ;
    skos:note "The portion read gives the occasions and the outcomes and not the procedure. Nothing was found here about the posture, the words, the darkness or the binding that the later chapters of this volume are known to describe, and none of that is asserted by this node."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte"@de,
        "The words spoken at a birth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The uttering of efficacious words over a person at birth, said to determine that the person will be able to come to life again after dying."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmp:Khecari a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Khecarī"@de,
        "Khecarī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.32-33 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A seal in which the tongue is turned back and set above the palate while the gaze rests between the brows; the manual describes a months-long preparation of the tongue before the position can be held."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:Makutu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Makutu"@de,
        "Makutu"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31" ;
    skos:definition "The art of bringing the resentment of another person's own family atua down upon them, worked upon something that has been part of them or has touched them: a portion of spittle, or what is left of their food."@en ;
    skos:note "The operation runs through the same transmissibility by contact that tapu runs through, which is why the two belong in one file and not in two."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einweihung in den ersten Grad"@de,
        "Initiation into the first degree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 164" ;
    skos:definition "The entry rite of the society: instruction under a paid teacher, often carried over years, gifts to the officiating priests, a feast, and the shooting of the shell into the candidate by the officiating priests."@en ;
    skos:note "The report says candidates sometimes spend a number of years in preparation and impoverish themselves in the payment of fees and the preparation of the feast. The cost is stated as a fact of the institution and not as a warning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmp:MocharTheHuaca a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mochar: der Akt der Anrede"@de,
        "Mochar: the act of address"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "The act the whole transmission turns on. The priest sits on the ground, extends the left hand towards the huaca, names it, and makes with the lips the sucking sound that is what the word properly means; he says that he comes and brings what its children and creatures offer it, asks it to receive them and not be angry and to give life and health and good fields, pours chicha before it and sometimes over it, smears it with the blood of guinea pigs or llamas, and burns or blows the rest of the offerings."@en ;
    skos:note "The recorder states expressly that to have mochado a huaca does not mean to have seen it or knelt before it, because the adoration does not consist in that. It consists in having given the offering to the priest at the appointed place and joined the invocation with the left hand raised and open. That correction is his, and it is the most useful sentence in the chapter: it says that the act is participation and not sight, which is what makes the next node possible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:Mulabandha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wurzelsiegel"@de,
        "The root seal"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.61-63 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A seal made by pressing the perineum with the heel and contracting the anus, by which the downward wind is drawn upward."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Trinken des Ololiuhqui, um es zu befragen"@de,
        "Drinking the ololiuhqui in order to question it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos II and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "A seed resembling a lentil is drunk; the recorder says the draught takes away the judgement, being very vehement. Those who drink it consult it as an oracle for everything they wish to know. Some hold the drinking of the seed for such consultations as their office, and such a specialist bears his own name."@en ;
    skos:note "Three things stand in the passage and are kept apart here: that the substance is drunk, that the drinker is questioned as an oracle, and that there is an office for it. Only the third makes this a transmission rather than a usage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Übung der Glieder"@de,
        "Practice of the limbs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28" ;
    skos:definition "The performance of the eight limbs, taken together as one course of practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmp:Preliminaries a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vorbereitungen"@de,
        "The preliminaries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patrul Rinpoche, Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung (19th century)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The general and the tantra-specific preliminaries, placed before the training in either stage."@en ;
    skos:note "A dating warning belongs here. The preliminaries are set before the training in the fifteenth-century treatise, but the sharpest codification of them as an obligatory course is nineteenth-century, in Patrul Rinpoche's manual. Their presence in the older text does not license reading the later regime back into it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung des Reibbrettorakels"@de,
        "Consulting the rubbing-board oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 170-171",
        "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries" ;
    skos:definition "Two pieces of wood, the upper rubbed across the moistened surface of the lower. Sticking gives one answer and smooth running the other, and a lid that runs from side to side or alternately sticks and runs is taken as a refusal to answer. The instrument is the cheapest and most frequently used of the oracles and the one least trusted."@en ;
    skos:note "The earliest witness describes the same instrument two generations before the monograph, under the name borru, made from the wood of a tree he names, and reports that it was consulted before any undertaking of importance and hidden from the Muslim traders. The two descriptions of the mechanism agree; the names do not, and the divergence stands on the dispute node at the end of this file."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:SaktipataTransmission a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Übertragung der Herabkunft der Kraft"@de,
        "Conferring the descent of power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII" ;
    skos:definition "The initiatory act by which the descent of power is conferred."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmp:Sitali a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Śītalī"@de,
        "Śītalī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "A retention in which the breath is drawn in over the tongue, protruded past the lips, and released through the nostrils."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:Tummo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Tummo"@de,
        "Tummo"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The generation of inner heat, presented in three components: the channels, the syllables, and the heat meditation itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmp:Xingqi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Zirkulation des Qi"@de,
        "Circulating the qi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143" ;
    skos:definition "A breath practice inscribed in forty-five characters on a twelve-sided jade block, setting out the deliberate movement of qi through the body."@en ;
    skos:note "The two axes of disagreement belong to the claim and are recorded with it. Dating: Harper places the inscription in the late fourth or early third century BCE, Needham in the middle of the sixth. Reading: Harper takes the opening two characters as the title and begins the text at the second line, where Roth counts and construes differently. A citation that names only the inscription conceals both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmt:AdvaitaVedanta a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Advaita Vedānta"@de,
        "Advaita Vedānta"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)" ;
    skos:definition "The non-dual commentarial tradition on the Vedānta corpus, represented here by the commentary on the Brahmasūtra."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept separate from the Upaniṣadic file although it comments on that corpus. A commentary is a later act of reading and states things its base text does not: what is held here is the commentator's argument, and a record that filed it under the older heading would date a ninth-century position to the age of the verses it expounds."@en,
        "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Greek or Chinese material; a correspondence with either would count as a finding, and none is asserted yet. Edition: the Thibaut translation of 1890 in the Sacred Books of the East, named on the node. Number of citable passages: one, read in the introductory section on superimposition."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:Madhyamaka a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Madhyamaka"@de,
        "Madhyamaka"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)" ;
    skos:definition "The middle-way school of Buddhist philosophy founded on the verses of Nāgārjuna and its commentarial literature."@en ;
    skos:note "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: to the Śaiva and Śākta material a route exists and is not in dispute, so a correspondence with it would be reception and not a finding; towards the Greek or the Western esoteric material none is documented. Edition: the verses are read in Stcherbatsky's translation of 1927, named on the node, which carries the commentary of Candrakīrti alongside them; the Sanskrit has not been consulted. Number of citable passages: one."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:SeferYetzirah a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sefer Jetzira"@de,
        "Sefer Yetzirah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Book of Formation and its commentaries, in which the world is formed through letters, number and breath."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:StoicPhysics a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Stoische Physik"@de,
        "Stoic physics"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8" ;
    skos:definition "The Stoic doctrine of a fiery breath that pervades matter and holds it together."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition of Galen the fragment was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The section numbering of De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis differs between the older editions and the critical one, so V.3.8 locates the passage only in the latter."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:ValentinianGnosis a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Valentinianische Gnosis"@de,
        "Valentinian gnosis"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Valentinian school of the second century, known in this file through the account of an opponent and not yet through its own writings."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution governs everything read out of this file at present. What is held here comes from a refutation: the heresiologist reports the doctrine in order to defeat it, selects what serves that end, and is under no obligation to state it as its holders would. Every node therefore carries pm:thirdPartyAscription and not textual attestation, and none of them may be read as a Valentinian self-statement until the same claim is found in a text of the school itself."@en,
        "The three decisions that precede a tradition file, stated rather than left implicit. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Indian or Chinese material, so a correspondence with either would count as a finding rather than as reception — but no such correspondence is asserted here yet. Edition: the account is read in the Keble translation of 1872, named on each node; the Nag Hammadi witnesses are not yet read and their translation is not yet chosen. Number of citable passages: one. That is thin, and deliberately so, on the model of traditions/daoist.ttl — a file grows in passages, and a heading without passages under it would be a claim about the corpus that the corpus does not carry."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmc:Agape a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liebe"@de,
        "Love"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84" ;
    skos:definition "Called the offspring of apatheia and the keeper of the door to the contemplation of created things."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:Apatheia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apatheia"@de,
        "Apatheia"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81" ;
    skos:definition "Freedom from the disturbance of the passions, called the flower of ascetic labour."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:Asana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sitzhaltung"@de,
        "Posture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The seat, treated in the first chapter as the first limb of the practice."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:Azazel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Azazel"@de,
        "Azazel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Asael"@en ;
    skos:definition "A leader among the Watchers, named in the Book of the Watchers as the one who disclosed metallurgy, weaponry and adornment."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Baraqel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel"@de,
        "Baraqel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Baraqijal"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher whose teaching concerns celestial signs; the two recensions differ on which."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Huhn muss sterben und das andere leben"@de,
        "One fowl must die and the other live"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139, the four cases lettered A to D" ;
    skos:definition "The rule by which an oracular verdict stands or falls: across the two tests one fowl must die and the other must live. If both die or both live the verdict is contradictory and therefore void, and the matter must be put to the oracle again on another occasion."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a falsifying condition stated by the transmission itself, applied by its own practitioners, and printed with four worked cases. It is the reason this tradition was opened before others in the registry. What the transmission does with the condition when it is met is a separate matter and stands on its own node below."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:Cetopariyanana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins"@de,
        "Knowledge encompassing another mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the state of another consciousness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ClearLightOfSleep a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das klare Licht des Schlafs"@de,
        "The clear light of sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep" ;
    skos:definition "A luminosity held to arise at the passage into sleep, which the practitioner is trained to recognise and hold rather than to pass through unconscious."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan 'od gsal, Sanskrit prabhāsvara. The state is not that of a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; it is a luminosity placed at the moment of falling asleep, and its purpose in the sources is the recognition of emptiness and the preparation for dying, not the acquisition of a faculty."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:Dibbacakkhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Auge"@de,
        "Divine eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Sight beyond the range of the physical eye."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Dibbasota a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Ohr"@de,
        "Divine ear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Diksa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Initiation"@de,
        "Initiation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII" ;
    skos:definition "The rite by which a person is admitted to the practice, set out in the tantra and reproduced with commentary in the Kashmirian exegesis."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:DivyaCaksus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Auge"@de,
        "Divine eye"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26" ;
    skos:definition "Perception of what is distant, hidden or obstructed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:FourthJhana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vierte Vertiefung"@de,
        "The fourth absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The absorption from which the handbook has the supernormal powers proceed, and which it calls their natural plane."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:GenerationStage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Erzeugungsstufe"@de,
        "Generation stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the two stages of the highest tantra, treated before the completion stage in the training."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:InuitHiddenThings a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die verborgenen Dinge"@de,
        "Hidden things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19, 24" ;
    skos:definition "What is not open to ordinary sight: the state of the sick, the whereabouts of what is lost, the presence of the dead among the living. The tradition treats this as a domain that some persons perceive and that others are ill advised to form opinions about."@en ;
    skos:note "Relevant to prima-materia#127, which asks whether a perception of the distant, hidden or concealed is attested outside the spheres already held. The passage here attests the domain and one case of its perception; it does not attest a procedure for acquiring it, and that part is not in the portion read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wiederaufleben nach dem Tod"@de,
        "Coming to life again after death"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to certain persons of coming to life again after death and returning to their place among people, held to belong to those over whom particular words were spoken at their birth."@en ;
    skos:note "The narrative gives the being a name in a phonetic transcription that the digitised text renders unreliably; the spelling is therefore not carried into this node, and the term is to be taken from a clean copy of the page before it is asserted anywhere. The claim itself does not depend on the spelling."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kenntnis der Merkmale des Same-Sterns"@de,
        "Knowledge of the characteristics of the seed-star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282 and 284" ;
    skos:definition "The knowledge of what the seed-star is made of, how heavy it is and how it moves, said in the account to have been unavailable to men while the star's existence was known but its characteristics unrevealed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:Kokabel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kokabel"@de,
        "Kokabel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the stars; the two recensions differ on whether it is the constellations or the signs of the stars."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:Kundalini a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kuṇḍalinī"@de,
        "Kuṇḍalinī"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)" ;
    skos:definition "A power held to rest coiled at the base of the subtle body and to rise through it when roused."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:Manomayakaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geistgemachter Körper"@de,
        "Mind-made body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "A second body, formed and complete in its faculties, said to be produced from this one; the classical form of the claim the modern draft calls exteriorisation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:MaoriExactRecitation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wiederholung ohne Fehler und ohne Zögern"@de,
        "Repetition without error or hesitation"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11" ;
    skos:definition "The rendering of an invocation in which neither a mistake nor a pause occurs. The tradition sets the two on the same footing: hesitating is treated as failing, not as taking care."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Maori .

pmc:MideDistantHearing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hören dessen, was in großer Ferne geschieht"@de,
        "Hearing what happens at a great distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to hear what is transpiring at a great distance, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the chart by lines drawn from the ears."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MideDistantTouch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Berühren in der Ferne, zum Guten wie zum Bösen"@de,
        "Touching at a distance for good or ill"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity to touch friends and enemies at a distance, however remote, for good or for evil, marked in the chart by lines drawn from the hands; alongside it the ability to traverse all space, marked by lines from the feet."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MideSoulSeizing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Herausrufen des Schattens und sein Festhalten"@de,
        "Calling forth the shadow and holding it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree to call forth the shadow, that is the soul, and to retain it within his grasp at pleasure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:MideThoughtReading a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lesen der Gedanken und Absichten anderer"@de,
        "Reading the thoughts and intentions of others"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree, marked in the chart by the shell placed at the top of the head."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:NadiPurification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reinigung der Kanäle"@de,
        "Purification of the channels"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The clearing of impurity from the channels, at which the breath practice is said to aim, and whose accomplishment the text says shows outwardly in the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:NavajoSongExactness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Genauigkeit der gesungenen Worte"@de,
        "Exactness of the sung words"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24" ;
    skos:definition "The requirement that the songs be known in a most exact manner, down to the syllable, no important mistake being tolerated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NavajoCeremonial .

pmc:Penemue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Penemue"@de,
        "Penemue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the distinction of bitter and sweet and the art of writing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:PoToloOrbit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bahn des Pô tolo um Sirius"@de,
        "The course of pô tolo around Sirius"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 281-283" ;
    skos:definition "The course the seed-star is said to run around Sirius: perpendicular to the horizon, of a period of some fifty years counted double to a hundred because Sigui are paired as twins, with the star turning on itself once a year. Sirius is said to brighten when the seed-star is nearest and the seed-star to scintillate so that an observer believes he sees several stars when it is farthest."@en ;
    skos:note "The article calls the same course the figure of circumcision and excision: the near and far points of the orbit are the foreskin and the knife. The astronomical reading and the ritual reading are the same drawing, and the account gives no priority to either."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:Samadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sammlung"@de,
        "Concentration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the three trainings, named in the handbook as the condition for the six direct knowledges."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Sariel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sariel"@de,
        "Sariel"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:definition "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the moon; the Ethiopic gives its course, the Aramaic-Greek witness gives its signs under the name Sahriel."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SeeingWitchcraft a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen der Hexerei und das Weissagen"@de,
        "Seeing witchcraft and prophesying"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the witch-doctors: to see what is unseen, to tell who the witches are, to see the emanations of witchcraft floating about as small lights invisible to the uninitiated, and to resist a fatigue that the source says could not otherwise be borne."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:Shemihazah a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schemichasa"@de,
        "Shemihazah"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 6:3, 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    skos:altLabel "Amezarak"@en,
        "Semjaza"@en ;
    skos:definition "The leader of the Watchers who binds the others by oath on Mount Hermon and is named as the teacher of spells and root-cutting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:SoulJourney a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seelenreise ohne den Körper"@de,
        "Journey of the soul apart from the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the soul leaves the body and returns to it, reported of Aristeas of Proconnesus and of Hermotimus of Clazomenae."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Xeper als das Wort der Selbsthervorbringung"@de,
        "Xeper as the word of self-generation"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1975" ;
    dcterms:source "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the Egyptian term for becoming, coming into being, personified in the scarab god and signifying self-generation and the dawn, names the transformation and evolution of the will, and that it is uttered as the word of a new age succeeding the age of indulgence."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination because of the shape of the claim rather than its content. The word is Egyptian and its lexical sense is not in dispute; what begins in 1975 is its use as the formula of a path, in a sequence of ages with named predecessors. The Egyptological reference is present in the source and does not make the doctrine ancient."@en,
        "This is the end state the milieu sets against dissolution, and it is worth recording in the author's own terms rather than in the summaries: not the preservation of an ego as ordinarily meant, but a self-generation modelled on a god who brings himself forth. A record that reports it as mere ego-preservation reports the criticism and not the claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A doctrine recovered from Egyptian antiquity, reported as the teaching the word is drawn from rather than as a formula first uttered in 1975." ;
    pm:originatedBy "Michael A. Aquino" .

pmc:XingqiDescent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg als Stufe der Folge"@de,
        "Descent as a stage of the sequence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:definition "The stage at which the gathered and extended breath goes downward, after which it settles and becomes firm; the sequence then turns and rises."@en ;
    skos:note "The inscription assigns direction to a place in a sequence, not to a phase of breathing. Descent follows gathering and extension; ascent follows sprouting and growing. The closing lines set the two directions against the two powers, the dynamism of heaven showing in what rises and that of earth in what descends. Nothing here binds a direction to inhaling or to exhaling. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe"@de,
        "Letter-perfect delivery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616" ;
    skos:definition "The requirement, set on the parts of the ceremony chanted aloud and in unison, that the words be rendered letter perfect."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ZuniRitual .

pm:presentationOrder a pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    rdfs:label "Darstellungsreihenfolge"@de,
        "Presentation order"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source only presents the material in a sequence. Any necessity is the reader's, and belongs to whoever read it that way."@en .

pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beichte während des Fastens"@de,
        "The confession made during the fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "During the fast everyone confesses to the holder of that office, both sitting on the ground, at places appointed in the open country. What is confessed is theft, ill-treatment of others, having more than one woman, adultery, and failure to attend to the huacas; inward faults are not confessed, and simple fornication is not held a fault at all. Powders of the offerings are laid on a flat stone and the penitent is made to blow them; a small stone called pasca, which means pardon, is rubbed on his head with ground white maize, and the head is washed in a stream or where two rivers meet, which is called tincuna."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also done outside the feasts, when a man is sick, because they understand that their ancestors and huacas are angry at their faults and that this is why they fall ill. Some purify themselves without telling anyone, by rubbing the head with the pasca and washing it in a river, saying that the water carries the faults away; and one man said the specialist took him and his wife to a large ditch, washed them, made them put on new clothes and leave the old ones on the road, saying that their faults stayed there and that whoever took the clothes would take them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmp:IfaCasting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Wurf der sechzehn Palmnüsse"@de,
        "Casting the sixteen palm nuts"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The operation by which a figure is obtained: sixteen palm nuts are held loosely in one hand and thrown through the half-closed fingers into the other. One nut left behind is marked with a double stroke and two nuts left behind with a single stroke, and the throw is repeated until two columns of four marks stand on the whitened board."@en ;
    skos:note "The mark is set by what remains in the hand and not by what the diviner judges, so the figure is not chosen. The source describes the mechanism and does not draw that consequence itself; the observation is recorded here and not asserted as the source's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einweihung in den vierten Grad"@de,
        "Initiation into the fourth degree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170" ;
    skos:definition "The last entry: further feasts, the personation of the bear spirit, the sacred drum and the chanted prayers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmp:NoaidiJourney a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reise bei stillliegendem Leib"@de,
        "The journey made while the body lies still"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56" ;
    skos:definition "The practitioner beats the drum and sings, others present singing with him in higher and lower notes. He then casts himself on the ground, grows black in the face and lies as if dead, the drum laid as near as possible on his head, sweating. Those present do not leave off singing for the whole time. When he awakes he relates all the circumstances of the business enquired after."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Essen der Medizinen der Hexendoktoren"@de,
        "Eating the medicines of the witch-doctors"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89 and pp. 96-97" ;
    skos:definition "Before a seance the performers eat some of their medicines. The medicine goes into the stomach and is shaken through the body by the dancing, where it is held to become an active agent. The whole apprenticeship consists in the slow transfer of the knowledge of which plants the medicines are made from, paid for in a long string of fees, and a plant is known by stalk and leaf and not by root, which is why laymen may sit at the communal meal without learning anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Gauḍīya-vaiṣṇavische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇava transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 2.5.113-115 (trans. Haberman 2003)" ;
    skos:definition "The Bengali Vaiṣṇava transmission descending from Caitanya, which takes over the aesthetic vocabulary of Sanskrit dramaturgy and applies it to devotion, treating the relations in which a devotee stands to the deity as rasa and ranking them."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run: the fifth wave of the Southern Quadrant and the third wave of the Western Quadrant of the Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu, at the wording, in the bilingual edition of two thousand and three, whose Sanskrit stands on the left page and the translation on the right. Left unread: the Eastern and Northern Quadrants entirely, and the remaining waves of the two quadrants opened. The Ujjvalanīlamaṇi and the Prīti-sandarbha were not opened; both are named in the notes of the edition read and would carry the fuller series of the expansions of love."@en,
        "The contact route is documented and not undecided, and it runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The rasa vocabulary of this corpus is taken over from the dramaturgical treatise: the same terms, the same distinction of foundational and transitory states, the same eight secondary rasa. An agreement between this file and traditions/sanskrit-dramaturgy.ttl therefore counts as reception and never as independent attestation. What is worth looking for is what this later station added, and it added the ranking: the dramaturgical treatise arranges its rasa by derivation, deity and colour and does not rank them."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu; Rūpa Gosvāmin, Ujjvalanīlamaṇi; Jīva Gosvāmin, Prīti-sandarbha" .

pmt:Upanisadic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Upaniṣadische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Upaniṣadic tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Upaniṣads as the speculative layer of the Vedic corpus."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Chāndogya 7.15.1 carries the edition it was read from and was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For 1.11.5 the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmc:AstangaLimbs a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Glieder"@de,
        "The eight limbs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29" ;
    skos:definition "Restraint, observance, posture, breath regulation, withdrawal of the senses, concentration, meditation and absorption, listed together in a single sūtra."@en ;
    skos:note "The sūtra names them and says nothing about traversing them in order."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unterscheidung der Herkunft"@de,
        "Discernment of origins"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 8",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 80" ;
    skos:definition "The examination of an inner movement for its origin, distinguished threefold as angelic, human or demonic."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment .

pmc:DistantSight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Sehen des Entfernten"@de,
        "Seeing what is at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47 and pp. 55-57" ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole transmission is consulted for: to know what is happening at a place too far off to be seen or reached, and to report it on returning."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DivyaSrotra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Göttliches Ohr"@de,
        "Divine ear"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    skos:definition "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:FourAbsorptions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die vier Vertiefungen"@de,
        "The four absorptions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "A graded series in which each stage is reached by letting go of a factor still present in the one before it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Iddhividha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Arten übernatürlicher Kraft"@de,
        "The kinds of supernormal power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Transformation by supernormal power, the first of the direct knowledges treated in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:InnerHeat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Innere Hitze"@de,
        "Inner heat"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "Heat held to be generated within the body by deliberate practice rather than received from outside it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:ParacittaJnana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins"@de,
        "Knowledge of another mind"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19" ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the contents of another consciousness, said to follow from saṃyama on the presented idea."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:ParakayaPravesa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eintritt in einen anderen Körper"@de,
        "Entering another body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)" ;
    skos:definition "The transfer of the seat of awareness into a body other than one's own."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmc:Physike a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physikē"@de,
        "Contemplation of nature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    skos:definition "The second of the three parts, the contemplation of created things."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:Piti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pīti"@de,
        "Pīti"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "Rapture as a bodily felt state, graded in the handbook into five degrees of increasing pervasion."@en ;
    skos:note "The five degrees are khuddikā, khaṇikā, okkantikā, ubbegā and pharaṇā."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:Praktike a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Praktikē"@de,
        "Ascetic practice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1" ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three parts of the way, consisting in the keeping of the commandments and the labour against the passions."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:Qi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Qi"@de,
        "Qi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)" ;
    skos:definition "The vital breath or energy held to circulate in the body and to be susceptible of deliberate direction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:Sigui a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sigui"@de,
        "Sigui"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274 and 280" ;
    skos:definition "A ceremony celebrated once in sixty years whose stated purpose is the renewal of the world. The star that governs its reckoning is named after it: sigi tolo, star of the Sigui."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:Theopemptos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der gottgesandte Traum"@de,
        "The god-sent dream"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "A dream held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and distinguished from the ordinary dream not by its content but by the state in which it arrives."@en ;
    skos:note "The state is described as the one in which sleep is departing and waking beginning, or as a condition between the two, accompanied by a voice, by a light, and by an inability to move the eyes. This is the closest description in the collection to what modern accounts call the hypnopompic state, and the immobility is named as part of the sign rather than as an obstacle. What arrives in it is a communication received, not a faculty exercised."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pm:Reworking a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bearbeiten"@de,
        "Reworking"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act, within one tradition, of taking over a claim from another and adding to it something the source it came from does not contain: a term, a consequence, a caution, a sign, a condition of failure."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately expensive: pm:addedElement points at a node, so the addition has to be entered before the reworking can be claimed. A station that added something and cannot say what added nothing that this graph can use. As with pm:Testing, absence is informative and is not to be stubbed: no instance means nobody has shown an addition, not that none was made."@en,
        "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which is an ordering act performed by an editor in the present and named as his. A reworking is an act inside a transmission, attested in a work like any other claim, and it belongs to the tradition that performed it."@en,
        "The class exists because reception is two things and the record could not tell them apart. Textual transmission is somebody copying a sentence, and it shows that the sentence was handed on. Operative uptake is somebody taking the instruction, carrying it out, and afterwards writing what was not in the exemplar. Only the second turns a chain of copies into a series of attempts, and only the second is proof against feedback: what makes the Dogon Sirius case weak is not that it is received but that nothing was added at the second station which was not already in the question."@en,
        "This is therefore the quantity worth counting, and it is not the number of independent witnesses. A tradition that takes over a claim about an effect and attaches a condition of failure to it has delivered more than three that pass it on word for word — and the addition is the material a pm:falsifiedBy is made from, so the examining layer comes out of the reception history rather than against it."@en .

pm:noProcedureDevised a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Verfahren ersonnen"@de,
        "No procedure devised"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted. This is a statement about the means of examination, not about the claim."@en .

pmp:Anapanasati a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ānāpānasati"@de,
        "Ānāpānasati"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Majjhima Nikāya 118" ;
    skos:definition "Mindfulness of in-breath and out-breath, set out as a sixteen-step progression in the discourse and as a meditation subject in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmp:BengeConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung des Giftorakels"@de,
        "Consulting the poison oracle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 136-138 and p. 153" ;
    skos:definition "A questioner addresses the oracle while a second man, the operator, administers a measured number of doses of the poison benge to a fowl. The address alternates: sentences framing the matter so that an affirmative answer is asked for and ending with the command that the oracle kill the fowl, then sentences framing it so that a negative answer is asked for and ending with the command that it spare the fowl. Whether the death of the fowl means yes or no is therefore decided by the form of the question and not by the death."@en ;
    skos:note "The operators are generally boys between twelve and sixteen, old enough to keep the food taboos and young enough to keep the sexual one. The consultation is held in public, and the bystanders may interrupt the questioner to have a point put that he has left out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmp:RunicDrumConsultation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung der Trommel"@de,
        "Consulting the drum"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48 and p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, their Language, Manners and Religion, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, Of the Magic Arts of the Laplanders, pp. 474-477" ;
    skos:definition "A bowl of hollowed pine, fir or birch with a skin stretched over it, painted with figures of the gods, the angels, the evil spirits, the associates of the noaidi, the sun, the dwellings, the sacred mountains and the beasts of the country. A ring or a brass cover is laid on the skin, the skin is struck with a hammer of reindeer horn, and the ring travels across the painted figures. Where it comes to rest, or which way it runs, gives the answer."@en ;
    skos:note "The two recorders describe the same instrument ninety years apart and their descriptions of the mechanism agree in every part that matters: the hammer of horn, the ring or cover laid on the skin, the painted field, the reading from where the ring goes. The later adds what the earlier does not: that the drum is not thought fit for use unless got by inheritance, and that women may not touch it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmt:EleusinianMysteries a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Eleusinische Mysterien"@de,
        "Eleusinian mysteries"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6" ;
    skos:definition "The Attic mystery cult of Demeter and Kore, with a lesser rite at Agrai in the spring and the greater rite at Eleusis in the autumn."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citation names the corpus in which the inscription is edited, which for an epigraphic source is the edition; what is not recorded is whether the text was read in that corpus or in a secondary report of it, and that is to be established before the reading of face C is relied on."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:Platonic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Platonische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Platonic tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 210a-212a" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Platonic dialogues and the ascent they describe from particular to intelligible beauty."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition the Symposium was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The Stephanus pagination is stable across the modern editions, so the citations locate the text; what is unknown is whether the Greek or a translation was read."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pm:MethodOrientation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung der Methode"@de,
        "Method Orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "How a path stands to the norm of the order it is transmitted in: whether its operative means keep that norm, deliberately break it, or decline the distinction. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."@en ;
    skos:note "Absence of pm:methodOrientation on a node means the axis was not determined for it. It does not mean the middle value, and it does not mean pm:normDistinctionDeclined, which is a positive claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other."@en,
        "Held apart from pm:TelicOrientation deliberately, and that separation is the whole reason both classes exist. The two vary independently: a path can take the forbidden as its vehicle and still aim at dissolution into what exceeds the self. A scheme with one axis cannot state that case, and the terms that carry one axis — right-hand path, left-hand path — therefore report a transgressive means as though it were a separative goal. See prima-materia#52."@en .

pm:PrerequisiteStrength a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Stärke der Voraussetzung"@de,
        "Prerequisite Strength"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "How firmly a source sets a prerequisite: as a necessity, as an instruction, or merely by the order in which it presents its material."@en ;
    skos:note "The three must not collapse. A list of eight limbs in the order given is not the same claim as a sentence saying that the eighth is unreachable without the seventh, and reading the first as the second is the commonest way a modern system acquires an ancient warrant it never had."@en .

pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Einweihung in den zweiten Grad"@de,
        "Initiation into the second degree"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168" ;
    skos:definition "The second entry: three feasts to the instructing and officiating priests, three chanted prayers to the named manidō, and a second shooting of the shell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmt:Hesychasm a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hesychasmus"@de,
        "Hesychasm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts" ;
    skos:definition "The Byzantine tradition of the prayer of the heart and of the vision of the uncreated light, defended in the fourteenth century against the objection that such a light must be created."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The Philokalia exists in Greek and in several translations whose contents and numbering differ, so a citation naming only the volume does not yet locate a text."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:SanskritDramaturgy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sanskritische Dramaturgie"@de,
        "Sanskrit dramaturgy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Nāṭyaśāstra VII" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Nāṭyaśāstra and the theory of aesthetic states that follows from it."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both citations the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter division of the Nāṭyaśāstra differs between the recensions, so the number seven does not by itself locate the passage on the bodily signs."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pm:AwarenessSpace a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bewusstseinsraum"@de,
        "Awareness Space"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pronominal reach of an awareness act, ranging from individual through dyadic and collective to universal."@en .

pm:ContactRoute a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg"@de,
        "Contact route"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a route of transmission is known between a tradition and those the graph already holds. It decides whether an agreement may be read as independent attestation or only as reception, and it is therefore a property of the tradition and not of the individual correspondence."@en .

pm:CoverageState a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Erschließungsstand"@de,
        "Coverage state"@en ;
    skos:definition "How far the corpus of a tradition has been carried into the graph. The scale exists so that a transmission nobody has opened and a transmission whose corpus is genuinely thin never read alike."@en .

pmp:HathaPranayama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Atemregulierung im Haṭhayoga"@de,
        "Breath regulation in Haṭhayoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The breath practice of the second chapter, directed at the clearing of the central channel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmp:Samyama a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Saṃyama"@de,
        "Saṃyama"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-3" ;
    skos:definition "The combined application of dhāraṇā, dhyāna and samādhi to a single object, presented as the general operator from which the capacities of the third chapter follow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:PatanjalaYoga .

pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Neuplatonische Theurgie"@de,
        "Neoplatonic theurgy"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "The late antique Platonic transmission concerned with the ascent of the soul and with the criteria by which a divine communication is told from a human one."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The De mysteriis citations name the edition they were read in. For Synesius, De insomniis neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened."@en,
        "The file records that this layer underlies the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory, so agreement among those three may not be counted as independent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:Sufism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Sufismus"@de,
        "Sufism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Islamic mystical path, systematised in the handbooks of the tenth and eleventh centuries."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both works the edition or translation the passages were read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. Neither citation carries a page or section number, and the printed editions of the Kitāb al-Lumaʿ and the Risāla divide their material differently, so the passages are to be reopened before anything in this file is cited by place."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pm:ExaminationState a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfstand"@de,
        "Examination State"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The standing of a protocol: how far the attempt to fail a claim has actually been carried."@en ;
    skos:note "The first two states and the third must never collapse into one another. A claim nobody has devised a way to examine is not a claim that has been examined and found wanting. A record that reports both as unsupported would not be reporting on the claims but on the reach of its own instruments."@en .

pm:Systematizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Systematisieren"@de,
        "Systematizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of imposing an order on material that did not carry it: a list, a sequence, a correspondence table. Named, dated, and attributed to the compiler who performed it, never to the source that is being ordered."@en .

pm:TelicOrientation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Ausrichtung des Zieles"@de,
        "Telic Orientation"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "What a path says becomes of the bounded self at its end: whether the self is to be dissolved into what exceeds it, strengthened and preserved as its own absolute, or whether the path declines the question. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."@en ;
    skos:note "The values name what a source claims, not what happens. Nothing on this axis is examined by holding it; where somebody takes up the examination, that is a pm:Testing node pointing at the claim, and the examination states of the two values are not symmetric."@en,
        "This is the axis the Western terms were built to carry, and the axis they carry badly, because they carry the other one at the same time. A record that wants to state where a path claims to end has to be able to state that without also stating how it proceeds. See prima-materia#52."@en .

pm:casesWithDeviation a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Fälle mit Abweichung"@de,
        "Cases with deviation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cases have been recorded and show a deviation from the stated base rate. What deviates is what the protocol measured, which is rarely the whole of what the claim asserts."@en .

pmt:GraecoRoman a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Griechisch-römisches Zeugnis"@de,
        "Graeco-Roman testimony"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Greek and Latin historiographical and doxographical reports of extraordinary capacities."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The book and chapter numbers are stable across the modern critical editions, which is why the loss is smaller here than elsewhere; it is still a loss, because it is unknown whether the passages were read in the original or in translation."@en,
        "The file records that a route to Indian material has been proposed for the older Greek transmission and not shown. Disputed is therefore the state of the question, not a hedge."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:NavajoCeremonial a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Navajo-Zeremonialüberlieferung"@de,
        "Navajo ceremonial transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), pp. 24-25" ;
    skos:definition "The Diné transmission of chantways: long ordered ceremonials for restoring a condition named as order, with the sequence fixed and its errors held to be consequential."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the first time in the introduction to the legends, sections 41 and 42 on the sacred songs, printed pages 24 and 25. The legends themselves and the ceremonial texts were not read, and the fuller ceremonial account of the night chant published in nineteen hundred and two was not opened in this run."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Wyman, Blessingway, with the recorded Navajo texts; Matthews, The Night Chant" .

pmt:ZuniRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Zuni-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Zuni ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 611-835" ;
    skos:definition "The Zuni transmission of fixed prayer, memorised verbatim and held to be ineffective if altered."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition that states its own falsifying condition: the words must be exact. Whether exactness is in fact required is examinable."@en,
        "Read for the first time in the introduction to the ritual poetry, pages 611 to 620 of the printed report. The body of the collection, the prayers themselves, was not read. The corpus stands in the language of its speakers with a facing translation, which is what makes it admissible here at all; the recorder is an outsider and the question of ethnographic first fixation raised at prima-materia#91 is not settled by this file."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteAbsent ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, with the Zuni texts" .

pm:Attesting a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bezeugen"@de,
        "Attesting"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act by which a claim entered the record. Instances of this class are modes of entry, not claims; a claim points at one of them through pm:attestedBy."@en ;
    skos:note "Orthogonal to pm:AwarenessContext, which describes the state the asserter was in rather than the route by which the assertion reached the record."@en .

pm:AwarenessContext a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bewusstseinskontext"@de,
        "Awareness Context"@en ;
    skos:definition "An epistemic mode in which a statement is asserted. Individual instances are defined in ontology/consciousness.ttl."@en .

pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Christliche Unterscheidung der Geister"@de,
        "Christian ascetic discernment"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John Cassian, Collationes I-II" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the practice of discerning the origin of inner movements, running from the desert monastic literature through the Byzantine ascetic corpus into the Latin spiritual exercises."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The gap is load-bearing here: Evagrius is transmitted in Greek and in Syriac with divergent chapter counts, the Collationes are numbered differently in the older editions, and the marginal numbering of the Spiritual Exercises belongs to a modern editorial layer and not to the autograph."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pm:Positing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Setzen"@de,
        "Positing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A claim held in the graph solely so that a protocol has something to address. It witnesses no transmission, claims no source, and belongs to no tradition."@en ;
    skos:note "The class exists because a protocol needs an object and because the claims most worth examining are often the ones no received work carries. It is deliberately narrow: an instance may exist only where a pm:Testing points at it, enforced by pm:PositingRequiresExaminationShape. Without that restriction the class would become the channel through which an unpublished draft enters the record after all, and the direction the project runs in would be reversed."@en .

pm:mediatedAttestation a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Vermitteltes Zeugnis"@de,
        "Mediated attestation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim stands in a received work at a passage that can be named, but the passage was read in a second work that quotes or reports it, because the first could not be obtained."@en ;
    skos:note "Admissible only where the work itself is out of reach — not digitised, out of print, held internally, in a language not commanded. Effort is not a reason: the mode exists so that unreachable material can enter at its true strength, not so that reachable material can enter cheaply. See SPEC §10 and SPEC §15."@en,
        "Every claim carrying this mode must name the intermediary with pm:readVia, enforced by pm:MediatedAttestationShape. dcterms:source keeps naming the work that carries the claim, because that is what a later reader must obtain; pm:readVia names what the writer of the node actually had in front of him. Collapsing the two would hide exactly the fact this mode exists to record."@en,
        "Weaker than pm:textualAttestation and stronger than nothing, and the record has to be able to say which of the two it has. A claim read through an intermediary carries that intermediary's selection, its translation and its silence; a reader who cannot see that the passage came second-hand cannot weigh any of it."@en .

pm:thirdPartyAscription a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Fremdzuschreibung"@de,
        "Third-party ascription"@en ;
    skos:definition "One party ascribes the capacity to another, who does not report it. Common for capacities said to be lost, since no bearer remains to be asked."@en .

pm:procedureWithoutCases a pm:ExaminationState ;
    rdfs:label "Verfahren ohne Fälle"@de,
        "Procedure without cases"@en ;
    skos:definition "A falsifying condition is stated and no case has yet been recorded against it."@en .

pmt:NahuaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Nahua-Ritualüberlieferung"@de,
        "Nahua ritual transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The central Mexican transmission of address to the powers in things, of the day count, of an animal companion given at birth, and of a seed drunk in order to be questioned."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run: the first treatise of the manual of sixteen twenty-nine, chapters one to seven, in the running text. The remaining eight treatises, which carry the incantations themselves and the calling back of the tonal, were not reached: the reproduction used delivers its running text only to that point. That is a property of the access path and not of the work, and the next run resumes there rather than at the beginning. See prima-materia#228."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the change is a correction rather than a finding. The registry recorded absent, which asserts that no route exists for the period in which the corpus was fixed. But this corpus was fixed a century after the conquest, by a Spanish priest holding an ecclesiastical office, among people who had been under mission for three generations, and the recorded material carries Christian elements in places. Whether what he wrote down transmits the pre-conquest practice, and in what proportion, is a question nobody in this graph has carried out. Undecided says that; absent said something stronger that was never earned."@en,
        "The edition is not fully stated, and that is a defect this file carries openly. The reproduction used gives the Spanish in an old orthography and does not name the edition it reproduces. Every source line below says so. Settling it is the first thing to do before more nodes are written on this tradition, because a later change of edition would touch all of them."@en,
        "The incantations were taken down in Nahuatl in order to be suppressed, so the words survive in the language they were spoken in inside a hostile frame. Both facts belong in the record."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sahagún, Florentine Codex, especially books VI and XI; Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas (1629); Codex Borgia" .

pm:contactRouteUndecided a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg unentschieden"@de,
        "Contact route undecided"@en ;
    skos:definition "The question has not been carried out. Registered explicitly rather than left absent, because an unstated contact route and a route shown to be absent are the difference between a witness and a candidate."@en .

pmt:DesertMonasticism a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Wüstenmönchtum"@de,
        "Desert monasticism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the ascetic literature of the Egyptian desert and its Byzantine continuation, in which the path is set out as an ordered ascent."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter numbering of the Praktikos and the step division of the Ladder both vary between editions, so the numbers here stand on nothing until the passages are reopened."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:Ifa a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Ifá"@de,
        "Ifá"@en ;
    dcterms:source "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa" ;
    skos:definition "The Yoruba divinatory transmission: an enumerated set of signs obtained by casting, each sign carrying narratives that a diviner is expected to hold by memory and to recite on the sign falling."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run: the section on Ifa in chapter II of the account of eighteen ninety-four, and its chapter IV for what that author says about the origin of the god; and the appendix of the volume of nineteen hundred and six, which reprints an extract on Ifa by a Yoruba bishop and a second extract by a Sierra Leonean author on geomancy. Left unread: the remaining chapters of both volumes, and the twentieth-century monographs named in the principal corpus, which are in copyright and available only as lending copies. The text of eighteen ninety-four was read in an electronic reproduction that carries no printed pagination, so the places are given by chapter and section and not by page."@en,
        "The closest thing to a written corpus that was never written. It is fixed, enumerated, internally ordered and transmitted through a pupilage of stated length, which is what made it the entry point chosen from the registry."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the correction is the main finding of the run that opened this file. The registry had recorded no route because none was looked for. One of the two extracts read here identifies the sixteen figures of the oracle with the apparatus of geomancy — twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, a judge and a further judge — which is the apparatus of the Arabic sand science and, through it, of material this graph already holds on its Mediterranean side. Until that identification is settled, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all."@en,
        "Two of the works cited in this file were read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints them. Neither extract carries an imprint there, so neither citation gives place or year. A separately published copy of the bishop's pamphlet was searched for and not found: under both the author's name and the confusable name of the Yoruba historian, in the large text archives and in a library catalogue aggregator, and in a bibliography of early Yorùbá books. The nodes resting on those extracts therefore carry pm:mediatedAttestation and name the appendix with pm:readVia, and they remain open posts."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Odù corpus as published in Bascom, Ifa Divination, and Abimbola, Ifá: An Exposition of Ifá Literary Corpus" .

pm:Converging a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Konvergieren"@de,
        "Converging"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A structural correspondence observed across traditions, held as a node so that it can carry its routes of transmission and its counterexamples instead of asserting universality."@en ;
    skos:note "Every instance is an ordering made by a compiler and carries pm:compilerInference, never textual attestation. No source says that the concepts correspond; the correspondence is the reading, and it is held as a node so that it can be argued with. Its sources are the sources of the concepts it brings together, and they attest those concepts and not their correspondence."@en,
        "Independence is also a weaker instrument than it looks, and this is the reason breadth is held beside it rather than under it. It guards against none of the three commonest sources of agreement: a shared physiology that produces the same experience wherever the same thing is done to a body, a structural necessity of the subject matter that makes any competent version converge, and the form of graded institutions, which resemble one another because institutions with grades do. What decides in the end is what can be reproduced, and independence is a way of sorting candidates for that, not a verdict."@en,
        "Most apparent universality in the Western esoteric record is diffusion from few sources. A graph that counts a citation chain as independent confirmation measures reception history and reports it as structure."@en,
        "That is a warning against one way of counting and not against reception itself. Reception history is a finding of its own: how many transmissions carry a claim at all, and how deeply the paths between them branch, are two separate numbers and neither cancels the other. Many witnesses on one cable is a different map from many witnesses on three, and both are more informative than the earlier arrangement, in which the first case was simply not shown. The one thing the graph must be able to do is say which of the two maps a reader is looking at: dense transmission and strong effect must never look alike, or the gate of authority returns in statistical dress."@en .

pmt:Maori a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Māori-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Māori transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882)" ;
    skos:definition "The Māori transmission of mana as an efficacy that attaches to persons and things, of tapu as its restriction, and of karakia as the form of address."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Shortland 1882, chapters II to V and the appendix, in the 1882 London edition. Chapters VI and VII, on claiming and naming land, were not read; nothing else of the principal corpus was opened."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the reason is a rule of this project rather than a judgement about Polynesia. pm:contactRouteAbsent asks whether a route was available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. This corpus was fixed in writing from the eighteen-forties onward, a generation after the first mission stations, by Māori writers and by European recorders working with mission orthography. Shortland himself reports a rite of removing tapu that was carried out with a Christian morning service inside it and adds that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial; that is not a suspicion about the material but an observation printed in the witness. The transmission is older than the contact; the corpus this graph can cite is not. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry, and prima-materia#108 for the source criticism that decides it."@en,
        "The word from which every western theory of an impersonal magical force was built. The graph uses the notion throughout and has never held the tradition it came from."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ngā Mōteatea; Best, The Maori; Best, Tuhoe" .

pm:contactRouteAbsent a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kein Kontaktweg"@de,
        "Contact route absent"@en ;
    skos:definition "No route is available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. Agreement with traditions already held is a candidate for independent attestation."@en .

pmt:Daoist a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Daoistische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Daoist tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of Chinese texts and practices concerned with the cultivation and circulation of qi."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately not documented. From the arrival of Buddhism in China the route is plain and the file's material lies mostly after it; for the earlier strata it is not, and the whole independence value of the Chinese material hangs on where that line falls."@en,
        "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citations of the jade inscription name the work they were read in. For Zhuangzi 4, Baopuzi neipian 8 and Chen Zhixu's Jindan dayao the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened, and for the Jindan dayao the juan division is itself edition-dependent."@en,
        "Thin on purpose, and the thinness is the finding. Of the material this tradition is credited with, two places could be run down to a text that names its own words: the jade inscription, which is the earliest datable witness located for a deliberate circulation of qi through the body, and the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, which states the manner of the breathing in terms an examination could take hold of. How early the inscription is remains contested, and the dispute node holds that. What is widely reported and was not run down to a citable passage stays out rather than being approximated: the absorption of solar, lunar and stellar qi (fuqi) and the abstention from grain (bigu) are recorded at prima-materia#17, not here."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:Dogon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Dogon-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Dogon transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-294" ;
    skos:definition "The Dogon transmission of a renewal of the world celebrated once in sixty years, of the ways by which the interval is counted, and of a seed-star said to be the smallest and the heaviest of things."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Griaule and Dieterlen 1950 in full, pp. 273-294 of the first printing, in the digitised page images of the journal. Nothing else of the principal corpus was opened: neither Dieu d'eau, nor Masques dogons, nor Le renard pâle, nor Les âmes des Dogon. The Sirius article was chosen first because it is the single text the outside reception rests on."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent. The reason is in the article and not in a later judgement about it: on p. 282 the recorders attach to the fifty-year orbit a footnote observing that this period lies very close to that of the companion of Sirius, and they cite a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for it. Whatever the informants said, the account that reached print was written by readers of European astronomy, and the central enquiry ran from 1946 to 1950 in a region under French administration with a mission station in it. A route to the material this graph already holds therefore exists at the point where the corpus was fixed, and it runs through the recorders. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry."@en,
        "The registration named van Beek's re-study as part of the principal corpus. That was wrong under SPEC §14: a field re-study is a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise, which makes it evidence and not testimony. It has been removed from the corpus here and stands at pm:evidenceFrom on the examination node instead."@en,
        "The tradition most widely reported outside itself, and reported wrongly. Registered in this graph from the start as the standing warning of the whole registry, because a body of teaching that circulated for decades as ancient African astronomy could not be found by later workers among the people it was ascribed to."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Griaule, Masques dogons; Griaule, Dieu d'eau; Griaule and Dieterlen, Le renard pâle; Dieterlen, Les âmes des Dogon; Griaule and Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius" .

pm:Disputing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bestreiten"@de,
        "Disputing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about a claim, carried on within a tradition, between traditions, or among the scholars who edit their texts."@en ;
    skos:note "Without this class a disagreement can only be recorded by silently preferring one side, or by leaving the claim out because it is contested. Both losses are worse than the disagreement. A dispute node names what is at issue and cites each side, and a dispute with only one source cited is not a dispute."@en .

pmt:TibetanTantric a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Tibetisch-tantrische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Tibetan tantric tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Indian tantric completion-stage practices in Tibet, of which the six yogas of Nāropa are the best documented cycle."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Tsongkhapa and Bar do thos grol citations name the translation they were read in. For Patrul Rinpoche's Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:SaivaSakta a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Śaiva- und Śākta-Tantra"@de,
        "Śaiva and Śākta tantra"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Śaiva and Śākta tantric corpus and its Kashmirian exegesis."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution governs everything read out of this tradition. What is old is a set of subtle centres carrying seed syllables, and the older tantras vary in how many they count. The fixed system of seven centres with spectral colours and assigned psychological qualities belongs to the twentieth century and to no tantra; it is held in this file as pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping so that it is available as what it is, and it must not be read back into the material it was built on."@en,
        "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Vijñānabhairava citations name the numbering they were read in. For the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa, the Tantrāloka, the Tantrasāra and the Mālinīvijayottaratantra the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those passages are to be reopened, and the chapter divisions of the Kashmirian exegesis are edition-dependent."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:InuitAngakkuq a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Inuitische Angakkuq-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Inuit angakkuq transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-32" ;
    skos:definition "The Inuit transmission of an office whose holder is said to perceive what is hidden, to call up helping spirits and to act through them, acquired through solitude and privation and exercised for a household rather than for an institution."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, stated rather than assumed. No route is proposed by anyone between the Iglulik Inuit and the literate transmissions this graph already holds, and none is available for the doctrinal content. The corpus was nevertheless fixed in nineteen twenty-one and twenty-two, well after contact: the narrative itself names whalers, and the region was under mission influence at the time of the recording. The value here is therefore contact-route-disputed and not contact-route-absent, following the correction made for the Māori entry, and the tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence until the dispute stands as its own node. See prima-materia#101 and prima-materia#102."@en,
        "Read in the opening of the volume: the account of the working method, the first meeting near Repulse Bay, the old singer Ivaluardjuk, and the autobiography of Takornâq, printed pages ten to thirty-two. The chapters on the shamans, on the spirits and on the taboo system, which carry the terms this tradition is usually reported by, were not reached in this run. The limit is one of the access path and not of the work: the volume is digitised and public, and the portion delivered by the tool in use broke off at printed page thirty-two. See prima-materia#99 and prima-materia#100."@en,
        "The material read is testimony of an unusual kind for this graph. It is not a doctrinal corpus but a life story told in the first person by a woman who is herself said to have practised, recorded by an ethnographer who spoke a related language and worked without an interpreter. What that buys is a speaker attached to each claim; what it costs is that almost every capacity is ascribed by the narrator to somebody else, which is why the nodes below stand on third-party ascription and not on first-person report."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos; Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Caribou Eskimos" .

pmt:PatanjalaYoga a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Pātañjala-Yoga"@de,
        "Pātañjala Yoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Yogasūtra of Patañjali and its commentarial literature."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Only Yogasūtra III.38 carries the edition it was read from; it was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For every other citation in this file, and for the Yogabhāṣya, the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable: the session that entered them did not record it, and it cannot be reconstructed from the wording of the nodes. It is stated here rather than guessed, because an edition entered by inference would be a claim about the compiler's work that is not true. Until the passages are reopened, the verse numbers in this file stand on the received four-pāda division and nothing further."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:SamiNoaidi a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Samische Noaidi-Überlieferung"@de,
        "Sámi noaidi transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 45-47" ;
    skos:definition "The Sámi transmission of a drum read by the travel of a ring across painted figures, of an office entered through a threefold seizure, and of a journey to a distant place undertaken while the body lies as if dead and from which a token is brought back."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run, both at the wording: the compilation of sixteen seventy-four in its Oxford English edition, chapter VIII on the religion and chapter XI on the magical ceremonies; and the account of Danish Lapland of seventeen sixty-seven in the English translation printed in a collection of eighteen hundred and eight, its chapter on the magic arts. Left unread: the remaining thirty-three chapters of the first and the remaining twenty-two of the second, and the treatise on the pagan religion of the Finns and Lapps printed with the second."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as undecided, and the ground is inside the practice rather than around it. By seventeen sixty-seven a named practitioner marked himself and his drum with the sign of the cross, said the Lord's Prayer over it and invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel before striking it. That is a route to the Christian material this graph already holds, visible in the operation itself and not inferred from proximity. It says nothing about the route to the Siberian material or to the Norse, which is the question the registry entry meant and which is still not carried out; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers, so it holds the stronger. See prima-materia#101."@en,
        "The recorder of seventeen sixty-seven applies source criticism to his own material, and it is entered here because it is rare and because it bears on how the rest of this file is to be read. Of the confessions of witchcraft in the Norwegian trials he says that it is probable they were extorted, partly by violence and fear, and that it appears from the law books that some were put to the torture before they would confess. He then separates that material from what he goes on to report of the Sámi themselves. The separation is his and it is the reason the trial material is not in this file."@en,
        "The two are of different kinds and that is why both were opened. The first was written by a professor at Uppsala who never went to Lapland, on the commission of the Chancellor of Sweden, out of the written reports of parish priests whom he names in the text — so what it carries is at one remove and its author says so. The second was written by a missionary who lived among the people he describes and who reports named practitioners in their own words. The first is a compilation with a stated method; the second is first-hand and confessional. Where the two agree, the agreement is worth something; where the first stands alone, it stands on reports it names but this graph has not seen."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Schefferus, Lapponia, in the English edition The History of Lapland; Leem, Beskrivelse over Finmarkens Lapper; the eighteenth-century missionary manuscripts of Skanke and Randulf; Jessen, De religione paganorum Fennorum et Lapponum" .

pm:Cautioning a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Warnen"@de,
        "Cautioning"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act, within a tradition, of warning against a capacity, a practice, or the pursuit of either."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node rather than as commentary, because what a tradition warns against is part of what it teaches. A record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports the tradition it claims to hold."@en .

pm:statedNecessity a pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    rdfs:label "Ausdrücklich gesetzte Notwendigkeit"@de,
        "Stated necessity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source says what fails to happen without the prior step: that the later step is not possible, or does not arise, or turns harmful."@en .

pm:Originating a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Urheben"@de,
        "Originating"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of first putting forward a claim that no transmission carries, in a work that can be named and dated, where the claim afterwards circulates as though it were old."@en ;
    skos:note "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which arranges material that was already there and names what it arranges. An origination arranges nothing: the claim begins with it. Holding the two apart is what keeps the record from reporting an invention as an ordering of something ancient, which is the commonest way a modern construction acquires a warrant."@en,
        "The circulating ascription is the point of the class, not an addendum to it. A node that names only the author and the work is a bibliography; a node that also names what the claim is taken for is the finding, and it is the kind of finding this project exists to make."@en .

pm:compilerInference a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Schluss des Bearbeiters"@de,
        "Compiler inference"@en ;
    skos:definition "A conclusion drawn by an editor or compiler that the source does not state."@en ;
    skos:note "Admissible as attestation of an ordering act, never of the claim being ordered. Enforced by pm:AttributionShape."@en .

pmt:Midewiwin a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Midewiwin"@de,
        "Midewiwin"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 143-300" ;
    skos:definition "The Anishinaabe medicine society, with graded degrees and a mnemonic record incised on birchbark."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the report of nineteen ninety-one for the first time, pages 143 to about 180 of the printed text: the account of the society, of the initiation, of the four degrees and of the pictographic charts. The song scrolls, the medicine formulas and the extensive appended vocabularies were not read. The recorder worked among the Ojibwa in eighteen eighty-seven to eighteen eighty-nine; his chief informants are named in the report, the principal one a second-degree priest of Mille Lacs first initiated about eighteen thirty."@en,
        "The birchbark scrolls are a written transmission by their own means, which puts this tradition closer to the project's source rule than most of the registry it came from."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as disputed and not as absent. What is disputed is the age of the society itself, not a route to any tradition this graph holds: one side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, the other points to a dated scroll fragment from before any documented European contact in the region. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all. Note that the axis of the dispute and the axis a convergence asks about are not the same axis; see prima-materia#101."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The birchbark scroll record; Hoffman, The Midewiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa" .

pm:contactRouteDisputed a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg strittig"@de,
        "Contact route disputed"@en ;
    skos:definition "A route has been proposed and is contested in the scholarship. Agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation until the dispute is entered as its own node."@en .

pm:Process a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Prozess"@de,
        "Process"@en ;
    skos:definition "The root class of all ongoing acts modelled by prima-materia. Every substantive class in the ontology is a subclass of pm:Process."@en .

pmt:Andean a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Andine Überlieferung"@de,
        "Andean transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, the summary of what is worshipped" ;
    skos:definition "The Andean transmission of huacas as powers that are addressed and answer, of camac as the making and animating that a huaca is invoked by, and of the graded specialists who speak with them, fast for them and hear confession before them."@en ;
    skos:note "Left unread: chapters VIII to XIII and XV to XX of the extirpation account, which are about the causes of idolatry and the conduct of the visitation rather than about what is transmitted; the whole of the report on the Incas apart from the section on the specialists; and the rest of the chronicle of sixteen fifteen. The Quechua prose of Huarochirí, which is the one document of this transmission written in its own language, was not opened at all: neither the manuscript nor any of its three standard translations could be reached in full text, which is a property of the works and is recorded as its own issue."@en,
        "Read in this run, all three at the wording: the account of the extirpation of idolatry of sixteen twenty-one, chapters I to VII and XIV, in a digitised copy of the first edition; the report on the fables and rites of the Incas of about fifteen seventy-five, in the critical edition of two thousand and ten, at the folios given; and the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom in the autograph chronicle of sixteen fifteen, in the transcription published with the manuscript by its holding library, at the author's own page numbers."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent. The registry's absent was not wrong about the Andes before fifteen thirty-two; it was wrong about the corpus. Every word of this transmission that can be read was written down after the conquest, and two of the three recorders wrote in order to destroy what they described. The categories they carried in — sin, confession, penance, priesthood, the devil — are the categories under which the material is reported, and the confession complex entered below is exactly where that matters most. Whether a validity test for a confession is Andean, or Christian confession seen through an Andean procedure, or an Andean procedure seen through Christian confession, is not decidable from these three witnesses, and the entry says so rather than settling it by silence."@en,
        "The first edition of the extirpation account carries folio numbers that its digitisation does not reproduce reliably, so the places below are given by chapter and by the passage within it. A modern transcription of the same text was held alongside to disambiguate the reading of the sixteen twenty-one type; the two give the same wording at every place used here. No place rests on the transcription alone."@en,
        "The third recorder states his own provenance and it cuts both ways. He writes that he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. That makes him a witness from inside the transmission who learned much of what he reports while helping to extinguish it, which is a stronger warrant than either priest for the terminology and a weaker one for the practice."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Huarochirí manuscript; Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú; Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas; Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno; Polo de Ondegardo, Los errores y supersticiones de los indios; Cobo, Historia del Nuevo Mundo, book XIII" .

pm:Yielding a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Hervorbringen"@de,
        "Yielding"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made within a tradition, that a named practice produces a named capacity."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node and not as an edge for the same reason pm:Attributing is: only a node can carry its own source, its own mode of attestation, and a protocol that tries to fail it. A bare edge would make every such claim look alike, whatever it rests on, and would leave nothing for pm:Testing to point at. The question of which capacities a practice actually develops is answerable only where the pairing itself is addressable."@en .

pm:Attributing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Zuschreiben"@de,
        "Attributing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The act of ascribing a capacity, property, or deed to a subject. A lost faculty is never a finding; it is an ascription made in a work, and the work is what the graph can hold."@en .

pmt:AzandeOracle a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Azande-Orakelüberlieferung"@de,
        "Azande oracular transmission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, the list of Zande terms, pp. 226-228" ;
    skos:definition "The Zande transmission of witchcraft as an inherited substance in the body, of a graded set of oracles by which it is located, and of a corroborative second question by which an oracular verdict is held valid or void."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance rather than on demand. The transmission states a condition under which its own principal oracle delivers no verdict at all, names eight causes to which a failure is attributed, and prints two consultations at length so that a reader may count the outcomes for himself. Very little else in this graph names a failure condition its own practitioners apply."@en,
        "Read in this run: the abridged edition of nineteen seventy-six in full for chapters I, III, V, VI, VIII, IX and X and for the appendix of terms and the appendix on dreams. Left unread in that edition: chapters IV, VII, XI, XII and XIII, on whether witches are conscious agents, on the place of witch-doctors in the society, on magic and medicines, on the association for communal magic, and on the situation of death; and the third appendix. The unabridged edition of nineteen thirty-seven was not opened: it is in copyright and stands in the large archives only as a lending copy or as a search-only index, which is a property of the work and not of the search. Every place below is therefore given from the abridged edition, and the abridgement is named in each reference because an abridgement is a selection and a later reader must be able to see that he is looking at one."@en,
        "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the correction is not a finding but the removal of one: the registry had recorded no route because none had been looked for. What a search finds is on both sides. Against a route: the poison oracle stands already in the earliest account, taken down at the first sustained European contact, so it cannot have entered with that contact. For a route: the same monograph places the belief in a witchcraft substance in the body among many peoples of Central and West Africa and calls Zandeland the north-eastern limit of its distribution, which is an areal connection to the West African sphere in which this graph already holds a transmission; and the corridor from the Islamic north was open from the eighteen-sixties, since the earliest witness travelled in with an ivory company out of Khartoum. Neither has been carried out for the oracular material in particular. Until it is, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation."@en,
        "Two further witnesses were opened for the counter-search and are cited where they carry something: the travel account of eighteen seventy-three, which is the earliest European description of these oracles and predates the anthropological literature by two generations, and the Belgian ethnography of nineteen twenty-one, which describes the same transmission from the other side of the colonial border and sixteen years earlier than the monograph. Both were read at the wording in full-text reproductions of the printed editions named in the references."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteUndecided ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande; de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande; Larken, An account of the Zande, in Sudan Notes and Records; Lagae, Les Azande ou Niam-Niam" .

pmt:Hathayoga a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Haṭhayoga"@de,
        "Haṭhayoga"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā of Svātmārāma and the manuals around it."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Every verse cited in this file was read in the Sanskrit of the widely circulated vulgate, in the recension that the 1915 edition and translation of Sinh carries, and each citation now says so. The critical edition drawn from the manuscript tradition numbers differently in places; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute holds what is at issue."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pmt:Theravada a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Theravāda"@de,
        "Theravāda"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Pāli canon and its commentarial literature, of which the Visuddhimagga of Buddhaghosa is the systematising handbook."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Visuddhimagga citations name the translation they were read from, and Dīgha Nikāya 2 was reopened for this purpose. For Majjhima Nikāya 118, Dīgha Nikāya 11 and Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8 the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pm:Testing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Prüfen"@de,
        "Testing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "An ongoing attempt to fail a claim under a stated condition. A protocol that names no condition under which the claim would be refuted is not a test of it."@en ;
    skos:note "An instance exists only where somebody has taken the examination up, and its absence is therefore informative rather than empty: it says that no procedure has been taken up, not that the claim has been examined and found wanting. A stub for every claim in the collection would double the graph and say nothing the absence does not already say."@en .

pm:prescribedOrder a pm:PrerequisiteStrength ;
    rdfs:label "Angeordnete Reihenfolge"@de,
        "Prescribed order"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source instructs that one comes before the other, without saying what follows from disregarding it."@en .

pm:placesEntered a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Stellen eingetragen"@de,
        "Places entered"@en ;
    skos:definition "At least one passage has been read in a named edition and entered as a node. This is the state in which a tradition begins to witness."@en .

pmt:Enochic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Henochische Überlieferung"@de,
        "Enochic tradition"@en ;
    dcterms:source "G.W.E. Nickelsburg, 1 Enoch 1 (Hermeneia, 2001), introduction" ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Enoch literature: the Book of the Watchers, the Astronomical Book, the Book of Dreams, the Epistle and, in the Ethiopic canon alone, the Book of Parables."@en ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered .

pm:Presupposing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Voraussetzen"@de,
        "Presupposing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The claim, made within a tradition, that one step of a path presupposes another: that a practice, state or capacity is not reached except by way of something else."@en ;
    skos:note "A node and not an edge, for the same reason as pm:Attributing and pm:Yielding. A prerequisite claim differs enormously in what carries it, and the difference is not visible on an edge. It matters whether a text sets an order or a reader inferred one from the order of the chapters."@en .

pm:Practicing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Praktizieren"@de,
        "Practicing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "An operative procedure enacted within a tradition."@en .

pm:contactRouteDocumented a pm:ContactRoute ;
    rdfs:label "Kontaktweg belegt"@de,
        "Contact route documented"@en ;
    skos:definition "A route of transmission to traditions already held is documented. Agreement with them counts as reception."@en .

pm:corpusNamed a pm:CoverageState ;
    rdfs:label "Korpus benannt"@de,
        "Corpus named"@en ;
    skos:definition "The transmission is registered and its principal corpus is named. No passage has been read, no edition has been settled, and no node stands under it. A tradition in this state witnesses nothing and may not be counted as a party to a convergence."@en .

pm:Tradition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Tradition"@de,
        "Tradition"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "A knowledge tradition understood as an ongoing act of transmission rather than a static corpus."@en .

pm:textualAttestation a pm:Attesting ;
    rdfs:label "Textzeugnis"@de,
        "Textual attestation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim stands in a received work, at a passage that can be cited and read independently of this graph."@en .

pm:Conceptualizing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Begriffsbildung"@de,
        "Conceptualizing"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pm:Process ;
    skos:definition "The formation of a concept as an act within awareness; instances of this class are concept nodes such as pmc:Pleroma or pmc:Hen."@en .

